      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>McDOUGALI 'S PATFNT SELF A LSING FLOU R. A ONOK TURD LWAYB USED THE IA A MIN ER, tRIDAY, JUNE 15 1900</p>
        <p>CHAPTE,I, I.</p>
        <p>(Copyright.) THE TEMPTATION OF DUNCAN RUTLAND.</p>
        <p>DY JOSEPH HOCKING,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Birthright," "Mistress, Nancy Molesworth," "All Mcp are Liars," "The Story of Andrew Fairfax," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Wily ALIZON NEVILLE WENT TO TUDOR CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The story of the Garden of Eden is not only said to explain one of the greatest mysteries of the world; it also reveals one of the most striking traits in human nature. No sooner did Eve know that 3ertain fruit was forbidden than she longed for it. All of us long for forbidden fruit, no matter what our nationality or education may be. "Stolen grapes are sweet," says the old adage, and most of the old adages are true. This may explain why Alizon Neville, of Neville Priory, desired to enter a Nonconformist Church. Had she been free to do as she liked about the matter, the possibilities are that she would not have had the faintest inclination in this direction; but being trained a Roman Catholic, as her forefathers had been for many generations, she had been led to regard worship within consecrated walls as a species of blasphemy. Hence the desire to take part in a Protestant service. Of course, this feeling only sprunc, from curiosity, for she was in no way dis- satisfied with her own Church; indeed, she regarded it as the only Church, and had a proper feeling of pity for those who did not belong to her com- munion. Nevertheless, the fact that she had been led to regard the worship of Protestants as utterly wrong, and, for a Catholic, sinful, led her to look with curious eyes towards Tudor Chapel, which was situated in the centre of Lynford, a large mann- acturing Lancashire town. In the carriage opposite her sat an elderly man, who, by his attire, was evidently a Roman Catholic priest. He was a mild, kind-looking man, one mho evidently took life easily, and troubled little about the problems of the wced.. He prided him- self upon being a good orthodox Catholic, who did 'the work of a parish priest faithfully, and accepted without question what the councils of the Church had decided upon. In this way he saved himself a great deal of trouble. "Mother is a long time, Father Sheen," said Alizon presently. "If I had known I should have gone with her." "Her ladyship is a keen lawyer herself," replied the priest, with a slight Irish accent, "thus, when she gets talking with a lawyer, the time will pass quickly with her." Alizon laughed gaily, She was a pleasant- looking girl, with frank, open countenance, and dark, mischievous-looking eyes. She was tall, and well-developed too, and the dress she wore set off her fine figure admirably. "These Protestants build costly chapels," she said, looking towards a huge building, over the doors of which, and carved deeply into the stones, were the words: "Tudor Chapel. Father Sheen nodded his head gravely. "Have you ever been inside one?" asked the girl. "Yes. Never to their services, of course." "What are they like ? " "Oh, very much like an ordinary hall." "But you know nothing about—about, what they do there ?" "No." "I should like to go," said the girl.</p>
        <p>"My child," said he old man, i:eprovingly. "Oh, only to see, of course. I have never been inside their: buildings. What sect does that Tudor Chapel belong to ? " "I don't know, "• replied Father Sheen. "They are much the same, all these Protestant sects. They multiply like insects. Someone quarrels with someone else about some point of doctrine of which they know nothing, a split follows, and then a new Bethel springs up. They are a mere rope of sand—these Protestants."</p>
        <p>"Do you know the minister ? " "No. I have heard that a new one is just coming. Look, there is a placard announcing something about it." The girl looked in the direction towards which the priest pointed, and read a bill, which announced that the Rev. Duncan Rutland, M. A., would commence his ministry at Tudor Chapel on the following Sunday, and that a meeting welcoming him as minister of the church would be held on Monday evening. When the girl had finished reading she turned to a lady who was just entering the carriage. "You have been a long time, mother," she said. "If you had stayed much longer, Father Sheen and I would have engaged in a theological argument." The old priest laughed good-humouredly. He was Alizon's confessor, and had known her all her • life. Moreover, he often acted as chaplain at the Priory, and had been a friend of the Neville family for many years. A few secoads later the carriage rolled through the smoky streets of Lynford. The operatives were just leaVing the cotton factories, for it was half-past five in the afternoon. The time, how- ever, was early September, and the day being fine, , the hood of the carriage was thrown back so that they might enjoy the little sunshine which filtered its way through the clouds of soot and smoke. I Neville Priory was a fine old pile standing some distance from the town of Lynford, and Alizon loved her home. Throughout her life she had been surrounded by pure influences, and except in matters of religion, she had been allowed to act and think pretty much as she pleased. She was trustful and loving, quick of temper, and somewhat wilful, but a true, wholesome girl, nevertheless. She had read a good deal on certain prescribed lines, was a fair musician, and spoke several modern languages fluently. "You will stay and dine with us, Father Sheen? " said Lady Neville, as the carriage drew up at the Priory door. The priest murmured his acceptance, and. entered the house with a satisfied air.</p>
        <p>"I wish it weren't Friday," thought Alizon ; "I do hate Fridays' dinners. What's the good of it, I wonder?' _ _ _</p>
        <p>Presently the dinner-bell sounded, and the three sat down to the dinner-table. "Father Sheen," asked Alizon. "why is it a sin to pray in a Protestant chapel ? " "Because by so doing you encourage heresy, my child; because, in a way, you deepen the wound 'made at the so-called Reformation, and you recog- nise in a fashion the position of a Protestant minister." The girl nodded her head and was silent for a few minutes, then she went on:</p>
        <p>"But it would not be a sin to go to one of their meetings if you did not pray, nor in any way join in what they do ? " "No, not sin, but I think it would be unwise." "Why, Father Sheen ? " "Because, unless you are careful, the seeds of doubt may he sown." "I see. But if one went loving the Church and determined to resist Protestant influences, it :would not be sin ? "</p>
        <p>"No, in that case it would not be sin," and the priest emphasised the word. Alizon asked no more questions, but sat quietly throughout the dinner. Presently,. when alone in her room, however, she thought over what the priest had said. "Yes," she said to herself, presently, "I'll go. No one need know anything about it. I'll wear a thick veil, and slip in at the back part of the chapel." She found it impossible to go on the Sunday, but on the Monday evening she left the Priory alone. It was half-past eight o'clock when she reached Tudor Chapel, and daylight being gone, she was able to get into the vestibule without attracting any attention. Here her heart almost failed her, for, strange as it may seem to Protestant ears, this young Catholic girl felt, in spite of the priest's words, that the step she was taking would oe counted to her as sinful. She was 'about to turn back when a gray-haired old weaver came towards her. Evidently he did not recognise her through her thick veil. "Want a seat, lass?" he asked, in a friendly way.</p>
        <p>"Yes," she answered. "Come along, then." "I shall have to come out soon. I want a seat as near the door as possible." "li," said the old man. "Coom in to Mester Ashton's seat, there's nobody there." He accordingly opened the door and chewed her into a richly furnished pew, at the extreme back of the building. "You'll be just in time to hear Mester Rutland," he whispered, conti&amp;ntially. "Th' owd Matthy Dray is :lust a en. After he's fipished</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>ASSOCI ATI DA FOOl BALL CLUB.</head>
        <p>ASSOCI ATI DA FOOl BALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Secretary's Report.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Werriegton Asancittien Football I Club (Mr. T. P. leraoshaw) has j let i.supe annue report from which it appears that during the season the club has played 27 matcbee.won 12,tost 11,and drawn 4, scoring 64 goals against 39. The first match of the season took place at Goths) ne when ihe team was de- feated by one goal to nil, but the form displayed that day justified the conarnitt, ein again selecting the same team with one exception to play the following Saturday against Ske'rnersdale. at Warrington, which match they won by one goal to ell. Ihe following Sat- urday they travelled to eliddlevrich to oppose that team in the preliminary round of the Engtisb Cup Competi- tion and though cne goal behind at f a!! time succeeded in winning by five goals to one,after an excellent display by the Warriegton team. Present, the favourites:for the Alliance Champienship,appeared the following Saturday and proved a big draw, and though again in a minority at half time Warrirgtcn eventually won by three goals to one after is goecl exhibition of foottall. The second round of the Eeglish Cup was played at Nantwich •% and after a hard game Warring- ton were defeated by two goals to one From that time. Octoter 14th to December 23rd, they did not lose a sinele match, but were defeated at Hrela mersdale on the 23,d by two goals to nil. The Wigan Cup Competition proved very interesting: Warrington played Golborne in the first round and a good struggle was anticipated seeing that Golberne had taken threa points out of them in the Alliance Competition,but War- rington defeated them rather easily by four goals to nir. This match was the last one that the Captain, Colour Sergeant (now Quartermaster Sergeant) Jones played with them he hevieg received orders to rejoin his regi- ment in Ireland hut bet ere his eh parture in recegnition of his valuable services to the club he was preseet•el by the members with a hands ime travelling bag,suitably engraved. The presentation was made on the ground immediately after the match by the Rev. H. G. Roberts. The 2nd round of the Wigan Cup Competition with Castle Hill reiulted in a drawn game of 1 goal each but in the replayed tie Warrington defeated them by 3 goals to 1 and met Wrean County in the 3rd round at Wizen, and at the call of time the score was 1 each. Extra time was ordered and Wigan County scored in the last minute ; winning by 2 grais to 1 alter one of the best fights seen on the Springfield enclosure. Wigan County eventually withdrew from the Com- petition, and Warrington were invi-ed to play St. Helens Recs. to decide who should enter the eetni-final tie, St. Helene winning by 2 goals to 1. In the first round of the Warrington Cup Competition they played and defeated Frodsham by 9 goals to 3, but in the second round of the same competition they we re not so succnsful, Newtorele-Willows defeating them by 1 goal to nil, much to the surprise of the epectatrrs. , This was the ft st match they had lost on the t Ground and from that time the club had a very up • i fight, as owing to the support received which was in a large degree accounted for t y the bad weather experienced during the greater part of the season, they had to curtail the expenditure by droppine the outside professionals their places being taken by amateurs. The local pro- fessionals from Uri period gave their services free, their action in so doing beine much appreciated by the Cern- mittee and members alike. With the team ft) weakened the club could not compete. with the teams in the Alliance and eefeat followed defeat with the reeult that they only obtaieed 4th piece in the Alliance table. The Committee of the Club are to he congratulated on the way in which they 1 ave faced a strong financial diffi- cult? as owing to their pert ooal tff ,rts and to the invaluable assistance rendered by the President, they finished the s,astia with a balance in hand, a reu t which will no doubt be very cratifying. Though not so tauccesAul as r xpeeted the committee are not disheartened, and hope next MOOD to be able to present a more favourable report. The ba'ance she( t i ae pended to the report and shows a balance in hand of 14a 6-ie. GOAL SCORERS. The liar of goal sowers is as follows :—R Pierpnint 16, L Riley 14, E A Connor 14. Sergeant Senders 4, Murray 4. C Taylor 3, Pains 3, Creour-Sereeant Jones 2, N Fielding 2, E Ball 1, Hackett 1, total 64.</p>
        <p>Primrose League Fete at Walton. In connection with the Daresbury flab tation of the Primrose League the annual te'e was held in the grounds of Walton Hall by rermission of Sir Gilbert Greenell Bart., on Saturday afternoon and evening. The visitors who numbered-three or four bundred,were allowed to make an inspection of the stables, shippons, and piggeries, and most availed themselves of the privilege. The Walton Bra's Band was in attendance and played selection of music frcm 3to 6.30. while from six to nine Mr. Percy Randall's suing band provided dance music. At four o'clock a short meeting was held on the lawn, the chair being taken by the Ruling Councillor, Mr R I Wynne Jones. and amongst those present we noticed Sir Gilbert Greenati, Mrs Cyril K. Greenall (dame president), Mr and Mrs Griffiths, Mr hobert Pierpoint. M.P., Mr and Mrs John White, Miss Greenall, Miss Whitley, the Rev b J T and Mrs Darwall, Mrs rigor. Mies Pigot, Mr and Miss Seeker, Mr Frank Tinsley, Mr T S Burgers, Mr C W Phillips (hon. secreta,y), Mr Joseph Tickle, and Mr Lockhart. l'ue Ruling Councillor moved " bat this meeting ot the Daresbur'y Habitation of the Primrose Leagu., wbilst expressing its unabated confidence in her Majesty's Ministers, congratulates them on the prc- gress of the firm policy nursued in south Africa and the success attending her Majesty's forces, the re- sult of which must be to the immediate welfare of South Africa. and also must greatly tend to the general stability of the whole of 1 er Majesty's vast Empire." He said at the pre,ent time they hen no politics, it was all Imperialism Pro Boers were very few, and the great beast of the nation was Imperialist. (Applause) They ought to be thank- ful that they bad a united nation behind them,that they bad a Gm ernment that not cnly knew and felt the pulse of the people, but was able to protect. and rule that pulse. Finally, they ought to be thankful that in Lord Salisbury they bad a man who was fearless and firm, and had csused this great nation to be respecter. in every civilized country in the world. (Applause.) Mr. W. Fowles seconded, and the motion was carried.—Tea was provided in a large tent and afterwards sports were held-</p>
