      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>aTT DEAR NET-LIE,</p>
        <p>A hat 1 saw the other day shows revival of the lace " fall," which was worn some thirty years ago, when soup-plate bonnets and round-brimmed hats were in vogue. The material is heliotrope straw, trimmed with glace ribbon in the same shade, and folds of cream-coloured lace, a graduated frill of the latter forming the "fall." Clusters of mimosa and shaded violets complete it. The tie shown with this hat is one of fine silk muslin, about three- yards long, which go round the neck three or four times. Another hat is in biscuit- coloured straw, turned up in the front with a wired green velvet bow, puffings of tulle or chiffon and shaded roses. Hat strings are seldoli seen, even in tulle or gauze. They seldom survive the first cold entry of Spring, but very narrow velvet ribbon is sometimes introduced as hatf-strings.</p>
        <p>A pretty way of making era a foulard gown is with a tucked skirt and a bodice with a square collar of ecru lace over white silk, under which is arranged a fringed crepe scarf, light blue or cherry tolour in tint, according to the ground of the foulard, this scarf being knotted loosely on the bust in front, and the ends allowed to fall thence to the waist. Another fashionable style for a foulard gown has the skirt trimmed with three frills headed with lace insertion run through with black velvet baby-ribbon, and the bodice made with a lace bolero also edged with runnings of the ribbon- velvet, the vest being of lace and velvet alterna- ting with bands of tucked chiffon. The fringed scarves of crepe, soft silk, or silk network, now so popular, are very useful for supplying a little brightness to an otherwise quiet gown, and, if the colour of the latter is quite neutral, the scarf may be changed according to taste.</p>
        <p>I have noticed that printed panne is very much used for the trimming of dark dresses, and also fer hoods of coats. The colours are so softly brilliant that too much of the panne would be a mistake. Foulard is also made in the very brightest hues, and some of the designs have no more coherence than the splash of colours on a painter's palette. Blue and green, amber and rose, are all dabbed together in a manner that, somehow, works out effectively enough. In fact, these indefinite designs are often more pleasing than those in which the various sections stand apart in a fashion that induces that extraordinary machine, the human mind, to begin counting them up and down, diagonally, and horizontally.</p>
        <p>So many ladies now go cycling tfAitt I think I may remark that cycling costumes display no strikingly new features this season. Tin cloths in khaki- colour with stitched bands of white cloth are popu- lar, and the Eton *coat is pre-eminent. Linen coats and skirts will also be fashionably worn by wheel-women, but .the wise cyclist will avoid both linen and cotton, and have her coat and skirt made of some woollen material, either the thin cloth mentioned above or one of the many pretty flannels now to be had. Serge wea.rs well, but has the dis- advantage of showing the dust badly, and the close-woven, slightly smooth tweeds are preferable in this respect. A neat gown seen the other day was of (lark plum-colour t‘s%:ted, nude with an extremely well-cut skirt with two box-pleats at the back and a belted bodice with slightly full fronts and a square sailor collar of biscuit cloth edged with rows of stitching. The belt. and cuffs matched the collar, and the very small vest was of biscuit silk tucked and stitched. The hat worn with this dress was a boat-shaped Panama, with band and bow of plum-colour glace silk.</p>
        <p>Your loving,</p>
        <p>JENNIE.</p>
        <p>RICE Soup. —Put 6 oz. of rice in a two-quart pot of water, boil for an hour, thicken with the yolks of two eggs beaten with some cream and 6 oz. of butter. Serve with grated Parmesan cheese. RicE MuneiNs.—To half a pint of boiled rice a4d, a tea-cupful of milk, three errgs well beaten, olio spoonful of butter, and emougli wheat-flour to make the batter as thick as pound-cake. Bake in a quick oven. The same recipe with less flour -Makes griddle cakes.</p>
