        <p>"WOMEN -S CHAT."</p>
        <p>Both the Queen and the Prince of Wales will entertain two impertant royal visitors tads summer, the Khedive of Egypt, and the Shell of Persia. The Khedive has not beet) in this coentry since he was quite a boy, and some political impertanca is atesched to his present visit, which is to e xtend for %beer, ten days, to the end of June. Tee- &amp;LA'S; not expected until the latter end of next month, and he will be received with the same ceremony as in the (145 c of the visit of the fate Shah. There will he several entertainments at Buckingham Palace and Windsor Clitte, and the Prince of Wales and Lord Sdiehnry are aisle to eiv e ba-quets in the gueets' honotar. 'the Shah, woo traces his descent from Japhet toe son of Nth. is descrioed as being a very simple man in his habits Nit only is he Shah of Persia but he is the King of Kinge, the Shadow of God, the Footpath of Heaven, and the Centre of the Universe. These are only a few of the titles, in which he rejoices, but it seems the Shah dots not really care for the pomp of his position. This menth the Queen completes her sixty-third year on the throne, which reminds us that history furnishes no parallel to the leegth ad greltne la of Hsr Majesty's tele In years she has heen exceeded by only one of Ver predecesaora on the throne, viz., George M., who. attained the age of eighttetwo.lwhile the Queen's re:gn is the longest but on- in Eoripeus history,ancl even that exct pion does tint offer th same comparison. The reign of Luis XIV extendet over seventy-two years,but to was only five year ree when he came to the throne of France, and was. until 1681, under the regency of his mother. He actuelly held the reins of government for only fifty-four years, se that the Queen's reign may be said to he actnally the longest on record. About Miss Kingsley, whose logs we are all deploring. there was not one trace of the "New Woman." A most gravieua hrSteFF, entertaining conversationalist, an alto- gether delightful c,mnanion, this intrepid exielorer will indeed be sadly inised by her exceptionally wide circ'e of friends, and her wider cirete still of readers, for who among Its has not perused,w;th the keenest delieht,Misa Ktngaley's "Travels in West Milos?' Had she been sparel to us, she would most probably have given to the world, some ;account of her impressions in South Africa, teat, Alas, the new volume, of West African stu lies, ahoet tte pnblisheO, ii the last we can receive from Miis Kins sles'e pen. " Sine, her return from Calif° nia, two months ago Miss Hamden has been mere or leis indisposed, and her work eetirely suep-nded. But her many friends will rejoice to learn tha'• ste i now mekine most favourable progress, and—a beeinnin 'IT with her new novel. From a very severe tireakdearn just at the csmmencement of her literary care r, the result of hard readine and Motions sttely,Mile Harraden has never fully recovered and it is in reat m aasere far lieelth's sake that so much of her time of late years, has been spent en a friend'e ranch in Californie,wli-re she lives tee usual unrestreined open air life of the settler. Mrs. S.,erl, whose new and ieteresting nose% " Volces in the Night,' has) been attracting considerable nein, has a Iltt in Lendon—hieh rap to•yerda the clouds, and as full of briele'ness sane t CO.OUr as her Eastern taste can make it—where she usually spends the vason, but most of her writing is done in her lovely Scotch home " Dunluease" near the town of Ttatriff ie Aberdeenshire. In 'he grounds she has hr " retreat" in the shape of ea real Norwegian house, orought over as it stands. .It centeics three rooms, one a kthro, wipe an inele-nook and is alt ieetther whet • the eifteu novelist calls" de- lieh:Julle ur civilised " Ther • ir nothing of the languid dreamer about Mrs SLIM: She ha quick, alert, capable, and managing, ere:Cher keen tirewa eyes seem to read far below the serrate.. There is quite a new .craze over the derarfed trees and !alerts from Jeri an. A co 1-action nt these euriositicS. holonejng teethe widow of the late Dr. Ernest Harr, was ssidin LO:iden'the reeler die. and .attractsd great atten- tion. Sknne of the in:niatere f irest. trees which fetched as mutth as £2O or £3O each were only 19 or 20 inches high. eed reeesentee all .the enpsaraeace of great age. severel which were perfect epenimens ef real giants' of the foiest, only diminutive in elm, were said to he 70 or ,80 years rid, and ta-o, which were heuglet by the Mar- quis of Camden, were et ited to be 180 and 120 years old ree'teetti vel , They are vety effective for tittle decoration, or for reorrs, and they live well in our climate. Whe`her these fi.sarf trees and plants can tee Successfully cultiva- ted e-t !his c-untry is tanorher matterl but if sn, it would seetn to open out a profitable prespect ter ahorieulturists. Tale ferret of dwarfing at practised in JSO3n, is said to ansist of .pinching hack the buds, and keening the plate's teea` ()ord. But ,tercese not only depends upon tee reieetity ewe quelite ee-the roil, but also upon the q ractiv end tempereture of the water supplied, and no doubt ripen oteer cl-teils belonging to this ancient art as 'pr.:a:elide in China anti lateen. •4'sierv, 'Short time livt,k the up-ro-date woman was glieve6ele;,CT etnebsciet the fact that hsr eainty summer .eowne St`...ill'hung in .eclusion and that the chance' rie?,.raesrl mall indeed of their emergine therefrom, ere the feshione had completely altered. Patience however has iatJenerth been tewarded,and lovely airy fairy frocks .are ca evidence on every side. Black and white al) tin&amp; teriote tretna neeessiteehet not infrqnrntly from chic', end 'unitarily few combinat;one are so becemine. Miles :of. mincer black velvet rOiben is teine lewd on thee while imaiin freetze, so narrow that there 44 scsreely tureen C) St in the .titcees that sew it en, and with such a- gowe, never recture hats are generally chneen. In the hoice of dress p eservere, we c,antne, he