      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>ESTABLISHED</head>
        <p>ESTABLISHED</p>
        <p>CRAFTY ANTS.</p>
        <p>Something new and interesring about ants was re- cently learned isy a fl3rist. For a week or so he had been bothered by ants that got into Loxes of seeds which rested on a sh,lf. To get rid of Vie ants he put into execution an old plan, which was tr., place a meaty bone close by, which the ants soon cevere , deserting the boxes of smds. At som as the bone became thickly in- habited by be Ii tie oref-pera the fllrist tossed i into a tub of Water. The ant; having been washed off, the hone was azain put in use of a trap. The florist betliongLt himself that he would save trouble by placing the b int' in Vie centre of a &amp;Met of fly paper, 13:Having that ants would get csurt on the sticky fly paper while trying Li reach the fold. But the florist wag surprised to find that the at.t , uhnu discovering the native of the paper trap, formen a working force and built a path on the paper clear tn the bane. The tna'erill of tile walk was sand, reamed from a little pile noir by. F.T haurs the ants wortsei, and when the path was completed they made their way over its dry surtaee lO ciup es, as in a inateh, to the bone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WITH THE BOERS IN ST. HELENA A Prisoner Interviewed.</head>
        <p>WITH THE BOERS IN ST. HELENA A Prisoner Interviewed.</p>
        <p>Among the Beer prisoners now safe in Se Helena is a man named Potgieter, formerly of the Reda ay Hotel. Potcnefstroom, and a sturdy ,act tv feltow he is,Pndowed with an intelegence end education far sutpes-ing the ordinary &amp;Wl—on-feral Boer. Some notes of a chat with Potgittez,taken two lears ago by a press correspondent, are no, reproduced, being important in revealing a glimpse of the taaidAiips and pain that the Bcer has to lace qually with aur own countrymen. I travelled a great deal in the Orange Free State three years ago," said Mr. Potgieter. "My business made it aecessery for me to j iurney in carts and on horseback. often for m tells at a time.and, as I was out in all weethers, I frequently got soaking wet throng)), and had to let my wet clothes dry on me. I caught celd _upon i cold, tolle wed at last by a severe attack of rheu- matism. My legs and back only were affected at first. but by degrees the pains fiLlw to the back of the neck and, in fact, all over my bcdy. Dee.tors semed powerless to cure mese I geve up my rtree State business and came to the , Transvaal, first to Klerksdorp and after- ' waras to Potchefstroom, for further advice. In each town I consulted doctors, but without succe's. It was here in Potehetwroom that the events occurred of which 1 ern go.eg to tell you. The rheumatism at last attacked nee so na.dly that I was laid up in bed fcr ihree men' hs, duriog which tune I eufferel agonies. I dare not In eve, the pains were so terrible. nrtunately I was in me fetnet's house, and although my people did all they c tilt) I could hot get relief. My appetite 6.18,3 left ire, and altogether I felt that life was uebeareble. Then a gooe Samaritan rccomenended me to try Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale peopte.l had heard of many ireeances of their curative powers in the two Republics, and treverst a box o'f the bills at a mere. Very soon I felt the pa ns deparcine. In fifteen days I was able to get up, and felt much better in general health. My apret te teturefd, and I could eoj ey my food. I ,n—nued Dr. Wi limns' pink pills or ameher three weeks, and by' teat time I felt perfectly well. factually started work agaiu, and since then I have never felt the slie.htest relapse or feared a return of that terrible rhea- ' matism. 10 t:o give a triend of mine a box of Dr. Williams' pink pilk for his wife, who hail been ill in led for over a innieli ; he wrote t me afterwaids to teank me, for she had neen petit ctiv cured, and was up again and quite we 1." . There are tutu instances 11 this country of similer cues of a h umatisin b,; Dr. Williams' pink pels,but the above facts of a typical case in Such Atrica are given as an it uirrwirtia of the car-reaching v fieenee of this world f invite; nvdiciue. Even in the mst remote C)llritr:o4 or the world Cores are bsing censtautly invescipt d, and these include cases of acre net. eeesumptien, fez -me, rheumatism and the otner disease&amp; which raise atom one form or another of imeoyeriched b oed! By reason of their etrengthening I fft,tet en the nerves,De Williams' pink pills are also no- das tonic, a: d have cured debility, St. Vitus' dance, Lonsumptien,, ard paralysis. They may be oh. tainet post free at two sh•liings and ninepenee a box, or thirteen ar d nine for sre b xes. from Dr. Withams' COMpirly, nolbore Viaduct, London, but are also seld by most chemist and retailers. Mind you ask for Dr. Williams', however, and dishonest traders offt•rin subsututo pills under mirleadin,g titli a should be a void( d . • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>ther'll be th' 'Alleluia chorus, and then mester Rutland." -• •</p>
        <p>Alizon found herself comfortably seated where he could see the entire building, and yet i3O .nnoticed herself. She SAW that the chapel was atndsomelv decorated, and that several hundred seople had gathered together. "I wonder why all those people stay outside the .;hurch ? " she asked herself. "From what one ,en see, the folk who come here are intelligent, hrifty, respectable people. Most of the Catholics yf the town are Irish, and they are poor and hriftless. " _ _ _</p>
        <p>She did not hear much of what the old man who tood on the rostrum was saying. She had a vague mpression that there was a certain free and easy .tmosphere in the meeting, and the fact that the people clapped their hands and laughed confirmed her in her belief that the people were not religious ss she understood the word.</p>
        <p>Presently the Hallelujah Chorus was sung. It 'vas heartily and correctly rendered, but the singing n her own church was far more to her taste. So far there was little to interest her. When the singing ceased, however, she saw that the congrega- tion settled itself to listen attentively, and that to viany the real interest of the meeting had corn- •ooced.</p>
        <p>The chairman, a strong, tall, typical Lancashire man, in a few words introduced the new minister. "We sampled him pretty well yesterday," he said, "and most on us thought that he preached two rare sermons. Some of the lads and lasses said: Ile vvur champion' ; well, to-night we are here to welcome him, and now I hope you'll give a hearty cheer for the Rev. Duncan Rutland."</p>
        <p>Alizon looked eagerly towards the rostrum, and saw a young man rise. He wore no clerical attire, and might as readily have been taken for a lawyer or a doctor as a minister. He appeared to be about twenty-six years of age, was rather pale, but otherwise gave evidence of being strong and vigorous. The moment he rose to his feet Alizon felt that he was no ordinary man; before he had been speaking three minutes he had riveted her atten- tion. Perhaps it was because the address was so entirely different from what she had been accustomed to hear. It was really an appeal to young men and maidens to be true to their great heritage. He came there, he said, as an ambassador of God, to preach a great Gospel. Our fathers had struggled to give us freedom, freedom of thought, freedom of conscience, and that now, in the closing years of the nineteenth century, there was a danger of our young men and women losing their hold upon those principles uhich had made the nation great and mighty. To Protestant ears this was, of course, common- lace; to Alizon it was a plausible kind of iafidelity. And yet she was impressed. The sur- roundings were strange, the atmosphere was new to her, while the man who spoke possessed a kind of magnetism. To her, moreover, he was a dangerous man, his evident sincerity and earnest- ness were dangerous, while the fact that in every sentence he revealed that he was a student and a scholar fascinated her. She had been brought up with the idea that all Nonconformist Protestants were ignorant, Bible-worshipping fanatics. Of course, this young minister was utterly..Nvrong,----and doubtless Father Sheen could prove how lamentably false and dangerous all these, to her, startling statements were; but at that moment she felt carried away by his fervour, and in spite of herself, he made her mental sky higher, and her horizon broader. _ _ _</p>
        <p>As soon as the young minister had concluded his address, she rushed out. "I'll never go into one of those places again," she said. "No wonder Father Sheen says that Protestant chapels are dangerous." When she neared her home she slackened her pace. "I'll have to tell him, I suppose," she said ; "perhaps he will preach a sermon to answer this dangerous address. I am afraid I've done very wrong." What I have written in this chapter may seem of but little importance; nevertheless, this incident commenced anew the history of two People, a history that became almost tragic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHAPTER 11.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER 11.</p>
        <p>HOW DUNCAN RUTLAND MET ALIZON NEVILLE.</p>
        <p>Duncan Rutland was sitting alow in his lodg- ings. He had just anished his brelkfast, when his landlady entered, bringing two newspapers. One was the Guardian, the other was the Lynford Observer.</p>
        <p>"Th' Observer's bin a-givin' it yo hot," remarked the woman. "Oh!" "Yi. Ther's a leadin' article abaat the speech yo made on Monday neet." "I thought that was done with on Wednesday. The editor had a paragraph about it then, besides, giving a full report." "Well, ther s t leadin' article, and a letter from some Catholic or other." _ _</p>
        <p>Duncan did not reply, but taking the paper from his landlady, began4to scan its pages. The leading article was supposed to be a judicial summing up of the statements made in his speech, and did ilot interest him much, but the letter which Mrs. Nutter had mentioned was a vigorous attack on the position he had assumed. Duncan paid but little heed to it, however, and quietly went on with 'his work.</p>
        <p>"Thou mus'n get cross with th' Catholics," remarked Mrs. Nutter, as she left the room, "thou'lt mak it warm for theesen if tha'dost." Duncan laughed. "I've never said a word about them as yet," he said.</p>
        <p>"But they tak it thet tha bast." replied Mrs. Nutter. "What they didn't like was abaat th' Reformation."</p>
        <p>"Were you at the meeting ? " "No, but I read the report in th' Observer. It wur fair and strong." Duncan Rutland was the Youngest minister who had ever taken charge of Tudor Chapel. From the Nonconformist standpoint, it was the principal church in the town, and the "leading men " of the plact3 had been very anxious to secure "the right man." For some years the "Cause " had been *somewhat languishing. The building was situated in the heart of the town, and as family after family removed further and further into the suburbs, the difficulty of keeping up the congregations became increasingly great. Several ministers had been "heard," but none seemed to suit, until one day a young- student from a neighbouring college was invited to preach. Ile- was not regarded as a candidate, for the simple reason that very little was known about him; besides, the thought of inviting a lad of twenty-six to take charge of "Tudor " was out Of the question. Accordingly, he came to Lynford as an ordinary supply, and a very small congregation gathered to hear him. When the day's service was over, however, a nnanimous desire was expressed to hear him again. A few weeks later he had accepted a call to the Ihurch, to commence his ministry when his college course had ended.</p>
        <p>He had taken his degree in Arts before leaving college, and had come to Lynford full of natural pride because of his success, and full of hope con- cerning his work at Tudor. his first Sunday's sermons and his address on the Monday night had cost him a • great deal of time and study, for he had determined to state in them the lines of his working and thinking during his ministry. I suppose all young ministers think they can mark out a course of action, and most of them have all sorts of hopes concerning the impres- sion they will make, never dreaming that events may in a few weeks entirely revolntionise their plans. Duncan Rutland's course of reading during the past few months had led hint to believe most strongly that priestcraft was 'an evil thing—that no man should in any way stand between a man's soul and his Maker, and that before anyone could realise the Christianity of Christ he must himself fight out his faith on the solitary battlefield of his own son]. This fact had led him to make the statements which had somewhat startled Alizon Neville, and which had also caused a devoted Catholic y) write to the Lye ford Ohserver. "It seems I am unintentionally stirring people up," thought the young man, after he had been working for half-an-hour. "That Catholic asks me to substantiate my statements which were reported in the paper. Well, I will." The nest day Tudor Chapel was more than ordinarily well filled, and Duncan, with the im- petuosity of a young man, proclaimed his message. ife did not notice any reporters present, and did not anticipate that any special notice would be taken of his words. "If thoult tak warnin' front an owd man," said an old weaver to him, at. the close of the day's services, "thoWlt have nowt to do wi' newspaper discussions."</p>
        <p>"I do not intend to," replied Duncan, "but what is your particular reason for saying this now ? "</p>
        <p>_ "For one reason thonit be makkin' thesen cheap," was Matthy Bray's, the weaver's, answer, "and for another, no good comes of it. Ya see</p>
        <p>ther's silos a lot o chaps as wants to see thet</p>
        <p>names i' print." "Why, do you think there will be a discussiod in the papers? " "I do n all." "Why? " "Well, for one thing, although no names 'arti been mentioned, you've 'it the Cathlicks 'ard nowadays they're longing to 'ave summat to say. They're ready for ony means to git noaticed." "Are they strong in the town? " "Nobbut middlin', and they're mainly Irish. A.ll the same, Lady Neville's a Cathlick. So'e her lass." • "Her lass ? " "I mean 'er daughter. They're big foaks here. Mind, I'm noan findin' fault: Thou'st praiched! rare sermons to-day. But ther'll be .a lot of talk, mind that. Thee keep from mentioning nallleat and keep from writin' to th' papers; that's rut advice.' The Wednesday's edition of the A-I/Ilford Observer contained several letters concerning Duncan's state- ments, for and against, and the young man, heie,g new to the life of the town, was much interesteo. For the next few Sundays he said nothing in bia sermons concerning the questions discussed iu the newspapers, nevertheless they were flooded wit" Correspondence. In a country town editors are' glad when some question of local interest arlses' and there are always a number of people who fancy they can throw light upon it. In a few weeks the young man was tired of it. There was very littl instruction in what was written, and there we much personal abuse. One day—it was early in November—he startel for a long walk across the moors. It so happelle that he was free from engagements for the &amp;Ill', and as he had spent much time in visiting 113'3" hard reading for a few days past, he determined to go further afield than had teen his wont. Th? morning opened beautifully. This was an unusual, occurrence at this time of the year, for, as a rule, between October and May there are but few fine days in that part of Lancashire which is devoted to manufacture. As a rule weavers hate bright, frosty days, the threads are constantly snappitE.' and as a consequence there is a great deal of e%tl. work, for less pay. Usually the less work a weavear has to do in minding looms the better is the stU'',. manufactured and the more money can he draw 5' the end of the week. Thus it is that he enjors most those days when the air is humid and when the sky is dark and dreary. On this morning, however, the sun she,rif brightly, the air was keen and frosty, and as trudged along a well-worn footpath towards t“' wide moors, Duncan felt his nerves tingle with ° delightful sense of enjoyment. He had been wait; ing perhaps half an hour when he came to one the lodge-gates of. Neville Priory, where he SO young girl on horseback. It was evident that those who lived at the lodge were neglecting theiS duty, for no one was there to open the gate, a% the girl wa3 on the point of dismounting as Dunca'' came up. "Excuse me," said the young man, "but will you allow me to assist you?" He opened the gate as he spoke, giving a side glance at the girl, wile sat on a restive young horse. She passed through, and then, with a heighteeed colour but with a bright smile, thanked him f° his courtesy. "What a bonnie girl," thought Duncan, as sile galloped away. He had hit upon the right adjec: tive. She did look bonnie. There, in the brig winter sunshine, with the great leafless trees stand' ing around, she looked a perfect picture of youth, and health, and beauty. He saw that her hair wafa dark brown, her eyes were of the same colourra while her clear dark skin, flushed with health all., exercise, were the accompaniments of a face of 11'7, beauty. Her movements on the horse were per,,-- fectly graceful, too, and the young man had doubt but that she was some one of distinction the neighbourhood. Up to the time of his corainit to Lynford, Duncan had had no love affairs. is true that while he was preparing for collegefl had fond fancies concerning a girl who lived in L" native village; but he had never spoken to her eon' cerning them, and on entering college at twenti years of age his mind had been too full- of liii studies for these fancies to ripen into love. null the six years of his college life he had worke hard. Indeed, when shortly after his arrival the! his Principal had told him that if he set his railit" upon it he could take his degree in Arts in additioq to passing his theological examinations, he resistel all temptations of social intercourse and direeteu the whole of his energies towards study. Thus, when he came to Lynford, he was hearts, whole. Not that, he was adverse to female society; No healthy-minded young man ever is, ana although he had seen no one in Tudor Church wltc! attracted him in the slightest degree, he hove that at sometime he should have a home of 14.61 own, and that some pure, good girl would hewn° his wife. The memory of this dark-eyed, brown-hair maid remained with him through the day, and du, memory was pleasant. And if the truth must be told, he thought more about her than of tb! sermon he had intended to prepare for the cornip•O Sunday. About one o'clock he had called at a farrnhoul where the farmer's wife had given him a cup (.4,/ tea, and had, moreover, insisted that he sh°u'y have some of the dinner she had prepared for heti own family. It was after three o'clock wh,:, Duncan left the hospitable farmer and his and by this time the sunshine had gone, and a he..alg mist had settled upon the moors. Still, the rw-ve man knew his way, and, in spite of the gathetiov. darkness, enjoyed his walk homewards. Edo Presently he came to a style, at which he stopy„-„, "I think I'll try the footpath," he said, -":le later than. I thought, and I shall cut off a nil pr two." Ire accordingly tramped across some fields iI he came to a farm-house. By this time dark, and the fact of the mist made the road' cult to find. Just as he came to the farm-g'te° PA. heard women's voices. The one rather harsh on strident, the other sweet and girlish. "I'm rare 'n sorry, Miss Alizon," he heard es/ say, "but th' trap's gone to Lynford and it'll ll° be back for a long time." "Oh, never mind, Mrs. Crossfield, I dareK4 shall be able to find my Ivey across the flelde,,,e'd," when I get into the main road I shall not no° I never thought this fog would come on, Of should never have ventured so far." "Good-night," said Duncan, as he came up. 411 "Good-neet," said the farmer's wife, an° "t 2 looked at him keenly. "It's rather dark," ventured the young 111°40; Ile had heard what had passed between and wondered how he could offer his serviettal Miss Alizon, whoever she might be, '14'19 intruding. ra •404 , "It's rare and dark," replied the elder we " 'Appen yo're goin' to Lynford ? " "Yes, I am," replied Duncan. A "'Appen yo' know the rooad weel ?" t' "Yes, I know it very well," and he passed °lie]," 0; He heard the two speaking in low tones toga :A and then, before he had proceeded far, the vre"-- called him back. "I say, felly! " "Yes ' " and he went towards them again., • cc 'Appen yo'll tell me wot they call yo' ? "Certainly. My name is Duncan Rutland. "Tim' minister at Tudor?" . 01° "Then 'appen yoll walk wi' this young word as far as Boggart's Clough. Oo's a bit afeea" th' dark?" hli "If the young lady will allow me Te- 3 Calh' .pleased to accompany her so far," replied Dull politely. "Thank you," said the girl, "I shall beZ,,eff., glad if you will. Good-night, Mrs. Crosslano "Good-neet, Miss Alizon." ( To be continued.)</p>
