      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>MYSTERIOUS DROWNING CASE Al WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MYSTERIOUS DROWNING CASE Al WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Cattle Dealer's Body Found in the I River. Inquest Adjourned. At 10 45 on Monday morning a man named thvard Gleave was in a boat on the Mersey at 'tank Quay, Warrington when he noticed the body It a man floating in the water. It was at once re- red and subsequently identified as that of Veheird Hughes, a cattle dealer, of Shopfawr, Llan- eaCtreth, Anglesey. ; Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, conducted the Ilicitlest at the P)lice Station on Tuesday evening. A John Thornas Powell, draper, Regent [louse. rbilwch,AngleEey said the deceased was his brother- -law. He kst saw him alive on Monday the 14th fit his own house and be was in his usual t‘ealth and spirits, lie departed trom Valley 2tation for Manchester at tour o'clock that alter- on business, It had been ascertained that he tlrrived in Manchester and transacted business,hav- -4.43 sold some sheep. It appears that, last Tuesday a deceased received from Parr's Bank six £lO '4°tes. one ..E5 note, £3 in gold and some odd silver, tld from Brown's Bank he received 1"1:1 5s in cash. fl:e had £lO when he left home and they knew that '42,a transactions would amount to over £lOO. It had 7:118 0 been ascertained that he was at the Victoria vc/tel, Deansgate at 11 p.m. last Tuesday. Some E:trks ago deceased wa.4 addicted to drink and used 'urow his money over the tables, but for the last waree or four years Le had been an abstainer. He ,rahn ever a racing man. by 1,9 Coroner : If yoa are doubtful as to hovi he came 'll death and suspect foul pia) I will order a post ortem Wit tteils replied that he did not suspect anything. 111;6:active Barlow stated that he found .-P.3 odd in xiet."'Y and a gold watch and cuain in deceased's posses- was also a leather case containing a teie- form and a clique book. The olice had no au.- Thelon of foul play. Witness had examined the body kid found a scalp wound on the back of the head pro- bably caused by a passing boat. In answer to a juror c.itness said there was no rail- Way ticket upon him neither was there a pocket hand- kerchief. The witness Powell said there should have been a re- turn half of a railway ticket. fie also stated that deceased kept his notes in a leather case. The Coroner said he did not think it would ba safe t.O close the inquiry. He thought it should be adjourned Pr a post-mortem Pxamitiation to be held. The brother- et-law might be quite sstißfied in his own mmd, but wronosing they returned a verdict of "Found drowned," ead in the course of a month or so some evidence cropped 111) to show that the man was in suspicious company ,the rice would probably connec: the man's deeta with the :1813icinue company. As evidence, a postmortem ex- Vlination would be required ,and if the man were buried at could not be done. The inquest • s ad' mimed until next Tuesday morn- ing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SHOCKING SUICIDE,</head>
        <p>SHOCKING SUICIDE,</p>
        <p>ik Tanner Cuts his Throat with a Penknife. Mr. p A Jones, deputy Coroner, conducted an in- quest at the Police Station on Friday afternoon telative to the death of Fed% ell Harrison Granger, tanner lodging in Ship-yard who committed sui- -sde by cutting his throat with a penknife on Wed- (leeday morning. RkThe first witness was Virilliem Rogerson, 2:and 4, "IVIP-sard,who stated that the deceased lodged with —213. He knew him by the. name of Fred Granger, 11 he was a tanner by trade. He had lodged with Zi!ta for about twelve months and was eccentric in Ida ways especially when he had been drinking. De- ".eased was a very heavy drink?r. He often used to 'ItY he wished he was dead when he was becoming 40ber. belhe Coroner : That's the time they generally do "Ith they were dead. Witness continuing said that previous to his death "esleceased had been drinking for eight or ten days. 4le saw him on Tuesday night when he went to bed 41tao5t drunk. The Coroner: Where did he get his drink from? Witness: I could not say. Continuing he said " saw deceased ou Wednesday morning at about twenty to seven when he was downstairs. Witness riked him how he felt and the deceased replied Iflt he felt very bad in his head and his stomach. e further said that if he did not get better he Itt°l-11d go into the Workhouse. He had been told t deceased had some tea at 5.30. Witness went sank Quay Station at seven o'clock and returned Ix? twenty minutes to eight. When he got home 141,8 Son came downstairs and told him that Fred d4B bleeding to death. He immediately ran to Ceased'e bedroom and saw him lying on the floor. ite Was bleeding profusely from the throat. He a:811 went for a policerean and afterwards for medi- Resistance. Witness had never seen tha pen- Itnife produced in the deceased's possession. Sankey stated that he was called to the use of the last witness at 7.55 on Wednesday morn- He at once went to the deceased's bedroom tru found deceased lying on the floor. The only ist°tlaing that he was wearing was his shirt and thc'ekings. He was bleeding from a cut in his ?oat and a penknife was in his right hand. Wit- biim applied a bandage to the wound to stop the eeding and afterwards sent for Dr. Smith who allfticl the case was hopeless and advised the man's 48, uval to the Infirueary. Witness conveyed him Rtiv:re ih a cab but he died last on entering the in- t, ution. He had seen deceased drunk several le..lhe jury reterned a verdict of" suicide whilst o' '4"4ound mind."</p>
      </div>
