      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>!WORKINGMEN'S HOUSES.</head>
        <p>!WORKINGMEN'S HOUSES.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Permanent Benefit Building Society. The thirtieth annual general meeting of the mem- bers of the Warrington Workingmen's Permanent Benefit Building Society was held in Golborril- street, Schoolroom on Monday evening. The chair was occupied by Mr. Robert Isherwood and the attendance included :—Messrs James Hardin !, Wallington, 3 Longland, Edward Fenton, J W Bouchier,J Baxter, T H Jowett, A Jolley, S Lawton, tLittlewood, W W Leigh, Robert Lee, George Armstrong and Peter Dalton (secretary). THE ANNUAL REPORT. The annual report was read by the Secre- tary and was as follows :—The managers have pleasure in laying the thirtieth annual statement of accounts before the Esher-holders, as she .va in the annexed statement. During the year, the sum 0fi£1,582 123 ld has been received as subscriptions- £2.o4s 63 91 tor repayments and interest; and £6 is 10d entrance fees, fines, St e, making the total rece aloe £3,634 03 81, the working expenses beieg £7l 0. 21. The withdrawals amount to £1,051 53 id. The canna ,f £2,125 has been advanced to mnnbers. There are 233 subscribers, holding 308,'t shares; and 48 borrowers.hel '- hag 188 shares; making 281. members. halite! 4961- shares. The bonuses this year ameunt to £371 17s 61 k the:amount lent,of which sum £6B is divided as prod ,and £303 17s 61 carried to the prospective interest accouat, Under the Building Societies' Acts, 1894, the accounts of all the Building Societies are required to be made up in each a form, and to caret lin such paeticulare as the Chief Registrar of Friendly Societies may, with the ap- proval of the Secretary of State, direct. The form of account which has been prescribed by the Chief Regis- trar requires that the balance of captal remaining due upon the moetgage securities should be state i separately from the prospective interest thereon, in other words the balance of the £B2 103 (the net amount advanced per £100) remaining unpaid is to be distinguished ho n the balance of the £l7 103 (the bonus charged) also remain- ing unpaid. The accounts of the Society for the ,as t year have been made up on this basis and work out as follows .—The total amount remaining owing upon mortgages £9,707 12s 7J, of which only £8,648 12s can be regarded as capital, the balance of £1,059 0, 73 re- presenting the proportionate amount of interest thereon, calculated for the number of years the several mortgages have still to run. The profits for the year, including £l6 64 10d from last account, after at...ducting worki expenses, amount to £346 12s Bd, out of which the managers recomm nd a dividend ot 4 per cent., which will take £342 23, leaving a balance of £4 103 8,1, which they direct to be carried torward to next year's account. The whole of the managers retire. Messrs. Harding,' Wallington. Fenton, Isherwood, Boteshier, and Baste, are eligible and offer themselves for re-election. Six additional managers will ne taken from the register in rotation.--JainEs HARDING, Chairman. The CHAIRMAN remarked th4t he. thought the report was very creditable and very encouraging. The Seciety was increasing in numbers, shares and receipts. Every item was being worked most economically. Mr. ENGLAND moved the adoption of the report. Mr. BOITCHIER, in seconding, said he might remind the members of what was really the most important item in the balance sheet. That was that instead of carrying £4 103 83 forward, they were carrying forward £716 18371, which was undivided interest that the Govern- ment did not allow them to use. lii years gone by they used to pay greater dividends, but he thought they did not make sufficient provision for the future. They were now in the happy position that whatever might hanpen to the Society, they would not only be able to pay 203 in the pound, but they could pay interest for the whole ot the time they were liquidating the Society. He thought they might congratulate thEmselves upon being in that position. There was another matter for co a- gr atulation and that was that the mortgages were n,t heavy ones. There were only four mortgages over £5OO and he could assure them that the managers were always exceedingly careful in taking any property. They con- sidered it thoroughly before advancing money and they could congratulate themselves upon the fact that there were only 48 mortgages, 44 of which were on property where the money due to the society was under £5OO. They had no property at all on their hands and that was a pleasing feature. The report was unanimously adopted. THE DIVIDEND.—A COMPLICATED BALANCE SHEET. Mr. GREALEY moved that the society declare a four per cent. dividend. He said that they were in a very prosperous condition. He thought, however, that the balance sheet might be made out in a simpler manner. Mr. E. WALLINGTON seconded and explainel to Mr. Greeley that the managers had no option in the pre- paration of the balance sheet. They had to make it out according to instructions. It certainly was a very com- plicated affair. (Hear, hear.) The proposition was unanimously carried. It was suggested that in addition to the tormal balance sheet the secretary should in future years prepare a simple statement of the accounts. A YEAR OF PROGRESS. The CatinmaN pointed out that their subscriptions last year amounted to £1,276 us 91 ; this year they amounted to £1,582. fneir repayments la3r year totalled £1.946 63 9..l;this year they amounted to £2,045 They had 30 more members reith 50 more shares and they handled something like £650 more than in 1899 which he thought was very satisfactory indeed. ELECTIONS. Messrs. Harding, NrYsllington, Fenton, Isherwood Bouchier and Baxter were re-elected managers ; Messrs. W Bodla, junior, and Robert Lea were re-appointA surveyors, and Mr. A. W Howard and Mr. Armstrong were re-elected auditors. AN ALLOWANCE FOR MANAGERS. It was unanimously decided that the managers be allowed the sum of is for each meeting they attended during the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>THANKS</head>
        <p>THANKS</p>
        <p>A hearty vote of thank—s—w-a—s— accorded to the trustees Messrs. John Hatton,Charies B Haines and Joseph Harr:- son for their services during past years. A vote of thanks Po the Chairman for presiding ter- minated the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
