        <p>£75 he ace ptel ; and that the Watch Committee bore.) nd improvements, it was impossible to cure the smoke and the stokers used the necestary care and qu,,ted allow the clerk of the works of the new, attention. That, in brief, was the Case with tee corn- pelice buildings to act as clerk of the works of the new mittee. There were firms in Warrington wh3 spent euspical to tilKiu-3treet.—The Enough Sarveyor in. hundreds of pounds in try log to cure the nuisance, and formed the committee that he had appointed Mr. Arthur still they were brought before the committee asoffenders. itton to act under the retolu ion of the 15 le November It was felt that it was hardly fair to take proceedings lest with respect to the preparatioe of plaits and speciti agaiost them, or fine them,when the matter was beyond cations for the improvement of nOlia'S dealt with under their own contr el. As a committee they had no means th. , Hoireirre of the W)rkiog Classea Act or the Public of getting at the man who was to burns; by fining the alth Aets.—lt was resolve] that Mr. O. H. flindle, employer they would no get at him. Mancheeter, be engagel to take out qaentities for tee „ Councillor BENNETT : How do they do in other towns? pr mos d small-pox hospital. Ceuncillor Memo replied that Warrington was just as Alder mae SUTTON', in reviving the aloption of the well ff if not better than other townnand it was unfair ml utes, said a saving would be made by the appoint- t 'make a comparison between Warringtoreand;Seeffield, icent of the cleik of the works of the new police build- as the Nature of me work was entirety cliff e'ent. For logs as clerk of toe works of the new hospital in Aikin- himself he did think not the town suff ire] very much. street. and the people looked healthy. They certainly bad a very The MAYOR seeended, satisfactory death rate. Alluding to Sankey Brook, Councillor Beteeerr.in alluding to the Medical Officer's Cmencillor Monks said that if it was a case of sewer gas report, drew attention to the special report of Mr. J. B. the smell would be confined teethe spot from which the Calo,nael. Be said it was very interesting, and well worth gas issued. It really came from toe sediment brought perusal. Ref.noing to the subsidiary reports on the down the brook from St. Helens lend which settled on scavenging cal cleansine department,he called attention tbe banks. They would find the same smell on the banks to the nuisance caused by the littering of the streets of the river at Bet* Quiy. with papers,and thou ;:let something ought to be done to Couecillor Jose EVANS seconded, and the motion was MiliM'Z3 the outs Ince, which was a growing one. agreed to. (flaar, hear) Another matter he desired to refer to was tee sm ake'neisance. Be hid several times referred te it. Triough he believed the committee to be one of the best of the wh ale Corporation, they were in this particular matter behind the times and did not do all teat might be done to obviate it. To extent of the nuisance was evreanely obvious. Though Warrington was progressive in many respects it was as far as this m dter was concerned one of the most retrogressive towns in England. The report said that the total nuns- her of ebservatione by the Inspector amounted only to 51. Hie own experienee had been that it woulti be passible to find 51. smoke nuisances in any one day. (Laughter.) Having regard to the fact that the town was under a continual pall of smoke the number of observations was inadrquete. A closer eye ought to be kept on the offeneers and more cases rPported. He did not intend any refientioe on the inspector; the fault was with the committee. The committee always seemed to make light of the nuisance, and instead of making an rffert to diminish it were disposed to throw cold water OR any suggestion to that effect. Although the nuisance was se obvious and intolerable every day the total penalties amounted to tee magnificent sum of 10i. (Leughter.) If that was not reaucine the functions o 2 the committee to a farce he did not krow whet would elo. It was simply nonsensical to think for a moment thaf, any wealthy firm would be deterred from creating a nuisArce by such a ludicrous fine as that. It was quite time that someone spoke out and said that the committse were not performing their duties. as was told that it was necessary to trade and that h. must not interfere with the trade of the town. He could only say that he was as much interested and as much anxious to see the trade of the town flourish as anyone, but other towns dealt with the smoke nuisancel in a more business like and progressive spirit, and fi ;wished without having this nuisance. Those who were the greatest sinners were not the sufferera. The mennfacterers lived out of the town and it was eaey for them to he apathetic. They required a stronger stimulus than a 103 flue if thee expected them to be very ere rgetic. they who lived in the town suffered very severely, and some steps should be taken to reluce the nuisance. He had been reading a beak by a Sheffield engineer, which Mr. Dees had kindly given him on practical a oke prevention. The writer showed that the appliances for smoke prevention were of little useiend pointed out that the smoke nuisance could he reduced by the exercise of a Itttle care on the part of those in charge of the boilers and furnaces. He passed through Sheffield the other day, ,lana although there was a large number of chimneys there was not as much smoke as he sometimes saw at Warrineton. Alderman BOLTON thought Counciller Bennett bad been out of order in bringing this matter forward. The MAY in said they were discussing the Medical Officer's rep set, and out of courtesy he had al'owed Councillor Bennett to disease that portion referring to the Sanitary Works Committe. Cooncillor SMETHURST complained of the sounding for a long time of a certain works' whistle twice in the early morning. Alderman BOLTON was pr needing to make some further objection, when Toe MAYOR said it was for him to decide whether a member was out of ordet. Alderman BOLTON: I am not going to argue with you. The MAYOR : I shall not allow you. Alderman BOLTON said that Ceuncillor Smethurst ought to have brought his cemplaint before the Com- mittee. With reseerd te the smoke nuisance the cern- inittee could not be blamed. As to what bad been said about Seeffield, it was one of the bleckest places he had ever been in. (Laughter.) Tee Warrington manufac- ture a did their best to mitigate the nuisance,and should be allowed some little latitude,</p>
        <p>HEALTH t TATISTICS</p>
        <p>Alderm‘n SUTTON s Lid tie had forgotten to read same &amp;Titres which had been given to him by the Medi ii Officer, and te would do so now with the permission of the Council, and it was granted. The Alderman tben vad as follows .—Quarterlv returns, fir4t quirfer : Population, 65.000; births, 636 ;.birth.ra,te,39 pr 1,000; (1E3464, 347 ; death-rate, 20 7 oet 1,000. England and Wales, same quarter, 23 G. Zyrnotic death-rate. 1 04. In the 33 large towns it WAS 2 02, and in the 67 other large towns, of which Warrington is one, ther average was 1 73 so that this quarter is satisfactory as tar as the death-rates are concerned.</p>
        <p>Councillor Tremon called attention to the present eonlition of Sankey Brook. He had had very many complaints recently. He had himself made an investiga- ' tion and he found be complaints hid not been exagger- ated. The condition of the irook veal such that it was a surpris) that people could live in the immediate neighbeurhood. If the committee could do et-mete:bine the inhtbitantm about there would bo very grateful. The brook ettended for a considerable distanc, outside the district of Sankey Bridges, and if the committee could only be induced to spend half an hour on the banks he Was Sure they would go away quite as surprised as he did. Toe DVIITY•MAYOR : It is no worse than the Ship Oital. C nencillor TINNION It is considerably worse. Con- tinuing he said he would like to know from the chairman of the commit'ee what steps were being taken to do sway with this intolerable nuisance. Councillor PLIMPTON supported the remarks of Coun- cillor Tinnton. It was not the first time he had referred to the matter but he could assure thin he had never received more complaints than recently. The people in Level -lane had to close their bedroom windows and keep closed the front doors of their houses. Be did not know whether the St. Helene p,ople had done what they promised te do,but it was very evident that the nuisance was caused by sewer gas. It was positively unbearable for anyone living in The neighbourhood, and somethine ought to be done. The Council ought not to rest until the nuisance had been rienoved. Councillor JAME 3 EVANS called the attention of the Council to a paragraph in the report of Inspeetor Nor- man, referring to seizures of meat, and said the tratlers were not fairly represented. In ROtTI9 cases the feutchers I had themselves reported the 'matter to the Inspector, but they were not given credit for it in the report. The butchers of the town were much aggrieved at the manner in which it was stated in the report. The MAYOR explained that the cases referred to in the report were not cases of meat ‘ebich had been surren- dered, but of actual seizures. The cases of surrender were not included in the schedule. I Councillor EVANS thought that should have been exnlained in the report. The MAYOR: A few words would have made it plain. Alderman SUTTON in replying, said the exp!anation the Mayor had givee in reference tp Councillor Evaes' eemeleint was eufficient. He did not see that, anyone had reason to feel aggrieved. The committee had nothing to do with this report, and they had no Power to add to or take away from it. With regard to Sankey Brook, the committee were fully aware that .there existed a nuisance there, and a very serioue nuisance, but the committee had done all they could to remedy it. They were indebted for the nuisance to St. Helens and other places outside their judo lictino, and therefore thee had difficulty in dealing with them. It was similar to the diffi-tulty in regard to th, river. They, were- indebted to Manchester and neighbouring authori- ties for the nuisance which exi-ted there. The Corpora tion had done all they could to comnA St. Helene te prevent the pollution of Sank y Brook. Councillbr Piumaton comoleined that the people had to °lore their windowS and doors. That was so in ether parts of the town. Only last night he (Alderman Sutton) was posi- tively sickened by a most abominable stench, that had evidently come from the river or some works In the , town which ought to be looked after. Everything that r could be done in regard to Sankey Brook woul i be done. With heard to the works' whistle he was surprised that Councillor Smethurst wiehed to sleep after half-past five. (Laughter). Be (Alderman Sutton) could not. With regard to Councillor Bennett's complaint about the littering of the streets with paper he thought the hill posting companies were the chief offenders. The workmen were not careful enough when taking old paper off in order to provide room for new bills. The old paper was alloeied to blow &amp;beat the street, and became a great nuisance. He hoped steps would be taken in the proper quarters to prevent it. The minutes were then approved. SANITARY WORKS COMMITTEE —REPLY TO COUNCILLOR BENNETT. Mr. Dees having submitted a report on the condition of the public and private sanitary conveniences, it was resolved, at a meeting of the COT mittee, that his recommendation for their improvement be carried out Councillor MONKS, in proposing the confirmation of the minutes of the above committee replied to the observations which had been main by Councillor Bennett as to the smoke nuisance. Be said he should not have thought so much of the critinisin be had made if he had suggested a remedy, but it Wa,S rath.r the contrary. He had noticed before that if they gave Councillor Bennett rope enough the result was that he hung himself. (Laughter.) Cetincillar Bennett had referred to an expert whose Look he had been reading 1 and he wound up with a quota Con to the effect that, notwithstanding all the attention engineers had given to the subject, arid the introduction of the latest appliances</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL STRONG C-o-Vl-FLAINTS OF NUISANCES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL STRONG C-o-Vl-FLAINTS OF NUISANCES.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Town Council met on TueFd ty even- ing, the Mayor (Alderman 11,berts) preoling. Tnere were also present ; Alderman Fairclough (deputy Mayor), Aldermen Sutton, Beaton, Frinco,nb, Balton. Councillors Ellison, Duncan, P_unaoton, John Evans, A J Jolley, Smethurst, Bennett, Lee, A Jolley, finnion, Greening,Forshaw, Wilkinsn, Monks,F 11 Muerte, with Me-srs. J Lyon Whittle (town clerk), S Wilsan (deputy town clerk), J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), J Deal (sanitary eujneer), T L-ingdin (borough surveyor), J Sutcliffvt (assistant surveyor). THE LATE ALDERMAN WRIGHT. The MAYOR read a letter ne had received from Mr Wright expressing the thanks of the family for the ki,d resolution of sympathy passed by the Council on tin death of Alderman Wright, the deputy-Mayor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>BY-ELECTION</head>
        <p>THE BY-ELECTION</p>
        <p>_ The MAYOR formally ann4anc,-(1 the election of Mr W J. ForshAw or the Town Hill Ward in the recent by- eluCtion.</p>
        <p>THE CONFERENCE OF THE ASSOCIATION OF MUNICIPAL CORPORATIONS REFERENCE BY THE, MAYOR. The MAYOR said that, before proceeding with the minuns of the Watch Committee he wished to state that it would be withiti the recollectioo of all of them that the D puty-Mayor and himself had during the past week attended a very important gathering of toe repre- sentatives of the corporations of the United Kingdom, held in Dublin, at the invitation of the Lord Mayor. It was his duty as well as his pleasure to make a brief report on the proceedings. The report would not he subject to what be felt sure would be their kindly because it was " not on the minutes." (Laughter.) In order that there might be no delay it would be better to present the report at once. The gathering was pr bably the mest important which had ever been held in connection with the Association of Municipal Corperatione, in the first place because it had been the most largely attended, and in the second place because it was held in Ireland fur the first tone in the hisiory of the association. It was also important for another reason, namely, the nature of toe subj icts suhmitted for coLeideration. Thr business includen dis- eu4si ins and resolutions upon I subjects which he woeld bri fi eiiumerate. The first nisoluti ,n which was adopted wa in favour of Private Bill legislation being referred to j mint c immittees of L )rds and Commons ia order t save munnipalities a laree portion of; the enormous c.,st which was entailed lay the double exeenditure conse- quent upon a fieht before the s .parate Committees of the two Houses of Parliament. They had had consider able experience of that unnecessary expense, and tee IConference was unanimous that it wag not right that having obtained the consent to a Bill by a Committe-, of the House of Commons that they should have to go befoie the Lords, and probably have the labours of the Com- mittee of the House of Commons upset. Then there was a resolution in faveur of power being granted to munici• palities o extend their service of tramways outside the limits of their borough. In towns like their own, where the suburbs were rapidly growing, and the ovulation incresaing, it was extremely desirable that such power should be granted, especially as the people who lived in the suburbs worked in the town. Then there was a resolution to the effect that the Board of Trade be urged to promote legislation at the earliest possible moment to carry rut the recommendations in the report of the Water Gas Committee of 1899. Ho must confess to a profound ignorance of wan report. (Laugh- ter.) Then there was a resolution calline atten- tion to the delay and cost involved in the acquirement of land by municipalities for public purposes, and requesting arntliorative legislation thereon at an early date. He had it in his mind to illustrate the necessity for tee alteration in regard to their own hospi- tal extension, aa they had stopped for a consid9rabie period owing to the dill:11,11V of acquiring land while with regard to the Water Works extenslon,the Chairman would hear him out that the same condition of things olontieel. The final resolution depiecated any interfer- ence by Parliament with the powers possessed by muni- cipalities in regard to gas, water. tramways, electric liehting and power, markets, docks, piers, and similar .tredertakings, affirming the right of municipal authsri- ties to be entrusted with all undertakings, in the nature of local mcniop-ilien or quasi monop.oliets, and protesting that as town Councils were representative bodies and that manufacturers and traders were members of such Couecils, or were fully represented thoreonn here was no fear that any undue interference with the trading firer- este of any town would be proposed or carried out. The resolution was passed. But the utility of the conference was by no means confined to the discussions in public. The meting to- gether ineividually of representatives of different authorities, and their private intercourse and exchange of opinion during the time they were together, was or immense value, and one Came away with a c nisiderable amount of feeling stimulated in the direction of those ideas of municipal progress they all more or less believed in. It was an extreme pleasure on behalf of time Deputy. Mayor and himself to say with what gratitude and sur- prise they witnessed the profuse and generous kiwi- tality of the Irish people from the Lord Mayor down- wards. It would be impossible for any body of men to have been received wito greater courtesy and kindness ' than they received. He had a strong opinion, which was generally shared by all who attended the Confer- ence that the results would be very lasting and satig- fl ening in helping to do sorkiething to bring about a much teetter Feeling between the two parts of the United m Kingdo than had hitherto obtained. It would, he hoped, remove many of the prejudices which had too long existed between them, and do very much to make their relations happier than they had been. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>LIGHTS ON VB7 IC LES</p>
        <p>At a meeting asi:Ce-i;Vatch Committee it was resolved that Councillor John Roberts be applieted a member ; that the proposed new bylaws with retard to lizhts on vehicles recommended by the Home Secretify be sub- mitted to the General Purposes Committee ; toat at the request of the Health Committee, Mr. Henry Hall.rd be allowed to set as clerk of the works of the new hospital; and that the tender of Messrs. E F. Blakeley ai,d Co. ,for tbe ironwork required in connection with the new p )lice premises, at £192 55., be accepted. On the motion of the MAYOR, seconded by Alderman HEATON, the MirlUtES were circled.</p>
        <p>- COUNCILLOR SAIETHURST AND THE PURCHASE OF PROPERTY.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement ano Lighting Committee re commended that the sum of £B5O he paid to the trustee of the estate of Mr. Alfred Woods for the assignm-nt of the lease of the premises recently occupi-d by ele. Woods, and which will expire on the Ist July ,1918 ; rhat the plan showing the lines of the proposed new bride() at Bridge-foot be approved ; and that Mr. Arthur Flatton, one of the assistants in the Borough Surveyor's D partrnent, he appointed Building Inspector. Alderman SUTTON, in proposing the adoption of the minutes, referred to a resolution. "Th ,t the tender of Mr. J S Garland for the supply of unclimbehle rails at 7s. per yard he accepted." and said they were for the widening of Knutsford-road. Referring to the prop)sed purchase of the lease of the shop recently occupiel by Me Woo Is, he said he had no doubt the object of the purehaste would be obvious. Councillor PLUMPTON seconded. Cnencillor SMETUURST desired an exelanation of the £B5O to be ptid far the less 3of Mr. Woods' shop. It was, he said, now something like 20 sears since they started the Bridge-street improvement, and they had not hlt finished it. Now, they proposed to spend £B5O for a prospective improvement. If they had eigh years' interest on that sum it would increase to over £l,OOO, and to spend that sum on what, in his opinion, would never be done either in his lifetime or that of a greed many members of the C mncil would be very inadvis- able indeed. How were they going to receup teemeelres ' for this expenditure? That was a questien he should like to ask. If they bought this shop they e mild have to buy other property. He thought it would not be necessary to purchase on teat side at all. Why should they tamper with the Prope:t v at all ? Th-ey were constantly buying property. Tlvre was hardly a meeting of the Council without c. Inehtioo of some pur- chase. He considered this particular expenditure would be a waste of money. Alderman FRANCOMB asked if they were buying the lease with the intention of tre log to purchase the pro- perty at the end of the time? Would it not be possible now to make a bargain for the purchase of the p opertt ? If they were cuing to boy simply for the sake of the !ease they were better without it. Alderman SUTTON said that if the property was re- quired for improvements they would be able to obtain compulsory powers for the purchase before the lease expired, and without having to pay compensation to the Itenant who might then be in possession. They would. therefore, be gainers financially. The property was let at what was considered a low rental—£llo per annum —and they could let it at a higher figure. Probably they could get from £l5O to £2OO. The MAYOR said they would have the lease They could fix the rent, and if they did not require the prce perty for improvecnents they would make a profit on the arraegement. The minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>THE SMOKE NUISANCE. . SMELLS FROM SANKEY BROOK. The Town Clerk at a meeting of the' 9alth Commit- tee, reported that the Local Government Board had sanctioned the appointment of Dr. Goraall as medical officer of health.—Dr. Gornall's annual report for the year 1899 was read, approved, and ordered to he printed and submitted to the Council, and it was resolved that a building sub-committee of the hospitals he appointed, to consist of the Chairman. Deputy-Chairman and Coun- cillor Smetburst•—in application from the Whitecross Company, Limited, to use a steam whistle for denoting meal hours was granted.—The committee also recom- mended that the proposed small-pox hosibital to be erected at Great Sankey be constructed to h)ld 24 beds ; (bat the tender of Mr. William Fletcher for painting, at</p>
      </div>
