      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>BITER GAR.</head>
        <p>BITER GAR.</p>
        <p>It was resolved at a ineatintz the Gas Committee that plant for the enriching of the gas- at an estimated cost of £3OO be purchased and erected.. The members, at the conclusion of the meeting, invited the Chairman to formally open the new offices at Longfrrd. The cere- mony was subseqwritly pertorm,d by Alderman Fur- dough, and he w.A presented with a silver key by thc architect and builder in csmmemoratioa of the event. The members of the committle with the bead officials of the department and the Mayor and Town Clerk after- wards took lunch with the Chairman, at his invita- tion.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY-MAYOR, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, obse,rved that it was intended to give the pub- lic better gas. (Hear, hear, and laugh'er.) COURCiIIOr TINSION seconded, and the NlAvon added that it was als ) his pleasure to partake of the hospitality of the Gas Committee.</p>
        <p>The minutes were agreed to. "WASTE NOT WANT NOT*"</p>
        <p>It was resolved at a meting ot the Water Committee that the Chairman and Eagineer be appointed to attend the C 'ogress of Water Engineers to be held ia Cardiff. Alderman Fnaricomn,in submitting thin minutes of the above committee, said there was a great stress upon them at the present time with regard to the supply of water, and the members would l4e pleased if the users would be as careful as p)ssible. They did not want to alarm anyone. They had plenty of water, but they did not want there ta be any waste. Councillor LEE sEconded, and the motion was carried,</p>
        <p>rhe minutes of a number of other committee 4 which we published in our last issue, were passed without comment, and the C3uncil rose.</p>
      </div>
