        <p>CRICKET</p>
        <p>Warrington were unfortunate in being defeated by Liverpool nn Saturday. Batting &amp;gr. the visitors ran up a total of 79, an 1 Warrngton on g dal; ti th 9 motto:, res ponded with 78, Liverpool thug gaining a vic':.ory by a sins,de run. Warrin4ton placed a vritk tatn ivi the fic:d lacking the services of J W Bibby and E A Connor.</p>
        <p>The heavy'rain of Friday night and Saturday morning, f b 7 a dryiiig wins, tis I re lerid the wielc.q diffinilt, and the b., wines always held the upper hand. Liverpool won the top, and opine I their innings with El G Girnett anti W rinr,thi bowling bsing snared by Thewlis and E Ginson. Wir,h only six runs on a fine each by Elampsou sent back Enwrinz. whilst lour runs later Garnet; was bowled by t grand b3ll from Gibson:</p>
        <p>The Rev, H. E. Stu"lev foreezi the game, and got Tbnwli3 twice to toe boundary in succnssive °vow, but at 24 the Liverpool captain, H. Leach, was taken at point. H. C. Harrison came next:, and alter experiencing a `• life," assisted • Stanley to talvm the total to 35, %hen that batsman was bowled by Gibson. llarrksu cantinutql ta play steady cricket, and Dr. Smicla relieved Ttiewlis at 45. The new bowler speedily nv.t with succesi, for in his second over, Cole got his leg, in froat of a stra.iillat oce and Tn.-impson was taken ht 62 by Rawson standing back. Alter Ginson had bowl, d Harrison at 66 hr an invaluable 16 Dr. Smith finished off the innings in a summary manner, the last vrimc,ts behg taken in six balls, the aggrega.c.e reaching 79.</p>
        <p>Warrington started their inuings with the usual pair, Thewlis and B. &amp;cock, the bowling being shared by the two professionals, Rod! and Oktk,iow. With only two on the boar.l The gills was out to a brilliant one- handed catch at mid-on, the ball beiog tlkea very high up. J. Callon came next!, and alt.:lr adding four and assisting to take the t0r.,1 to 12 he tell to CV.;,,311 at third man. With D:. Smith as Silc-)ck's al,.sociate the pro+. pec'.B of the local side ihapro7,d, the tas.; t:.maer foming the game in sp'endid style.</p>
        <p>Twice he got Rue awa'e for four in one over, both splendid drives, wtailit t minute liter he placed him cc the bowling green for Tai, however, was hi i last contribution, Cole tailing him a cover pit for a fault- less 22. Affairs cll,l nor, go so well Warriti.4ton, now, for at 42 13. Silva, W. 11. Podrrore,and J. Briggs were all clisrni;svc.l, the two latter without Fe:ning. Tnompion forcil toe game splerrlidiy, soon lost tho company of Clark', wn3 was typee:l at 53.</p>
        <p>Three botindary strok&gt;s bvTi ipirt were lowlty ap- plaut!e 1, but after he hal made 18 no playei Acro6s :1'; a good ball tro'n It Jed. T U Gori ana G liArap.on took the score to within o to of Lite Livapoll aggregate, when tho last nainei ran l hitniel ou. ant (jib-ion taken at the wicket off tie first bA!I he reneived, the ,game ended in a win Livarp )ol by a single run.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2nd engaged Liverpool 2nd at Aighurth on Saturday. The home ream on the tess and knocked up a total of 201 runs when the innings was declarel, With ordy an hour and a half to play,a win for Warring- ton was out of the qai sMon, and the play becama very slow. Admsin au:l flawtiu faced the bowliag of Davidson and Smith. Both batsmen were content to play on the defensive, and Roper took the bad from Smith. The new bowler tried to tempt the batsmen with his lobs, and pliy had been in pr,'.‘gress for half-an-hour before Adamson was s`.umpA. Wb.n time was called Warrington had lo3t s'x wickets for 70 runs. Praise is due to Elawtin, who.played a most patient innings.</p>
        <p>Way rington Grammar Scho.)l met ArplPy on Saturday. The game resulted in a ti:?, each team scoring 39. fled- keth contributed 13 hr the Grammar S3hool.</p>
        <p>W4rrington Catholic.; had I.:WA diffi !tatty in dispiqing of Minre. Thew knockl up 73, tr. which Moire re- vended with 35. E Rit;b7 sc.tared 23 11: the</p>
        <p>Eirlestawn P-S.A. succurnbcA to S. Barhabas. Tho latter team ran up a total of 42 to their opponents 13. The only plap,r who reach-d d ,üb'e figures was G Rutter whose innings realiz A 16 for the winners.</p>
        <p>Lltchford Bliotist der,,Ated Lowton Pirish Church 13; a maj .rit of 22 runs, till scoresbing 44 and 22 reso,c- tively. W Flallrn wai iustratwatAi in scoring 15 for the winners.</p>
