      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ST. HELENS DOUBLE DROWNING CASE. Another Remand</head>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS DOUBLE DROWNING CASE. Another Remand</p>
        <p>At qt. Helens Police Co.W. onS,icurriay, Mrs. Sarah Walton, aged forty-six, of 15, Sun•moss, was 7gn before the court on the cnarge of wilfully murdering her two children.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. W. Bailey, Town Clerk,said that a very serious &amp;large had been I.referred against prisoner, and on Friday the c-troner's it,goest to,h ple.ce, and an open verdict was returrva, the jury" Expressing the opinion that the circum4anzes were somewhat sus, picious, especially in the face of the woman'a sta,e- ments, but that they did not fec'l there was sufficiem ev'd,ece on which to send pr.soner fcr trial. The connyr after this expretsed bl 4 agreement with the verdict., Rai said he was strongly of opinion that there was no stic.a evidence as would jusAfy a jury in c,nyictin.z. Under the circumstances, he (Mr. Bailey) thocglit it was lin- • portant that a copv of the denesitions made bef•-,rs the coroner should be obtaineJ and sent to the Home 0 li^e for submission to the solicitor t) the Treasury, wh, directs public prosecutiols, en that he mieht advise the chief officials of the notice. tie therefore applieu for . a remand until next Friday •</p>
        <p>..... _ _ Mr. U. L Riley, to:- the defence, said h had exp.,eted that the chief of poic, having then the retponsioili y, no doubt in a despPrtte hurry to arrest: the womari, ' would, advised by the Town Clerk, have been able to determine what conrse he would ad et that hirTnieg. The Town Clerk only appetred officiddly on the scene on Friday, and be felt now that the prostteution was in his hands reasonable time f•lriuld be givo-n !iim. He did not thiLlt people should be hurriedly arrested on charges lice that, and their frisnes put to anxiety and expsese under circumstances whicti might have been avo,thd ; but he would *gran to submit to the adjournment. The case was accordingly adjourned.and tie unfortun- i ate Odre. Walton again sent to Walton Gaol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. DENIS DAVIES.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. DENIS DAVIES.</p>
        <p>The people of Wairiugton and district will hear with deep regret of the death of Mr. Denis Davies ' eldest son of the late Mr. J. E. Davies. of the Old Warps, Warrington. He was seized with an attack of typhoid fever at Southport where he passed away at 1 30 on Friday afternoon By his death the life of a most promising actor has been cat short at an early age. Ile was only 21 years old but still he had already established a repu- tation on the stage. At vie time he was seized with illness he was travellin2 with Miss Kate Rorke's company in Pinero's play " The Squire." and a dramatic paper speaking of his performance at the Opera House. southport, remarked that "Mr Denis Davies desert es praise for his portrayal of 'Fell'." Many Warrington people will probably remember his last appearance at the Royal Court Theatre in Rylands-street, when he app.,ared in Mr. Cuthbert's Company in tl.e "Black Bishop." He drew large audiences, and his career was all along watched with interest by many Warrington friends. His dramatic career commenced about six years ago ' when he joined :tint. Louis Calvert's Company in " Proof" in which piece he also appeared in War- rington. Prior to his last engagement with Miss Kate Rorke's company he spent about six months in Paris,tudying. fie took a pride in his profession and displayed histrionic abilities of a high order. Recently he had written several plays, and was contemplating their appearance on the stage. The news of his death was a painful surprise to hit, many frien is in the town. The. Funeral.</p>
        <p>The grave closed over the remains of the late Mr. Denis Davies, whose d-atla was reported in our last issue, at the Warrington Cemetery on Monday afternoon. The funeral was a very quiet one. The body was brou2,ht from Southport shortly after noon, and the interment took place at a quart.= r past two. The funeral service was conducted by the Rev. A. S. Cameron. The coffin bore the follnwiog irocrlyton :—"Dtnis Davies, died June 8 h, 1900, aged 21 ye ,re." Among those present were 51,s Davies (olorlvr). the Misses Davies, Mr 11 Davies (uncle), Mr W Ftirhurst, Miss Fairhurst, and Mr F W Gilbert ; Me,srs A Browne, S Wallhead and T J Hulse (representin? :Ai,. Warrington Dramatic Society), Mr Joseph Potter(Royal Court Theatre),and Mr Tunstall. Wreaths were sent by "flue mother," Mrs 8 Lees (Birk- dale), Mr and Mr Turner (Buxton), "From 80,Windsor- road, with loving sympathy," "Uncle Harry," " His godmother," "Auntie Clara." Mrs Craw,' and !Miss Smith, "Harry and Bes,i.s," Mr and Mrs William Pierpoint and f,mily, Mr and Mrs R T Fairclough and family, Mr and Mrs W Fairhurst, Mrs Warburton (Dallam), "Friends at Brookside House. Penketh," Mr and Mrs Milling and family, Mr an.' Mr, Arthur Bennett, "His old comrades of the Warrington Dramatic Society," "The 'Squire' Company," the Misses May and Maggie Pierpoint, Mrs N B Percival, Mrs Pickmere' Miss Fairhurst (Whitley), Mrs B Pierpoint and family. Mr and Mrs Hugh Ferguson and family, " the members of the Warrington Society," "Friddle and L Ile and Mr and Mrs Etworthy," and Mr E I ward Kirkcorinpl. Toe funeral arrangements were carried out by Messrs. Boothroyd, Sons-and Ric-rimer, of Southpert.</p>
        <p>TO THE QUEEN. _- BY W. H. LEATHWOOD ;Author of Reflections upon toe Aesthetic Principle, &amp;c.) As rules the Tropic o'er thr Trale Dominion'O ot the eternal sun— As. by the moon's sweet influe%ce sway'd, The ilimitable wate, s run— So. set as some magnetic star. Victoria, in the height alone; Thou giv'st the wide world peace er war. And callest nations from afar Subservient to thy throne ! And jealous Time for thee lays down E'en for awhile, Ilia sceptre and his crown!</p>
        <p>Old Time—the monarch Time— For whose imp,rial state iC ngdoms and dyn 'sties sublime Are rendered desolate ! His hand !al th crusb'd th mightiest thing, And only Iv. is :Y,rat ! But red of God—bv Fate's decree He wells thine empire with the waves, And makes his majesty to be Chiefcst of thy devoted slaves ! He takes thy life—the groat—the good, Thy nisi:len—and thy motherhood— Devoted and divine— And, of thy viruss, mlkes a wreath About his brows 'o shine ! And thou halt wither fr ,m his front, and fade from out heart, Only when Tims hath lost his crown, and all his powers depart ! Written M iv 24. h, 1900</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Open Meeting- of —Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>Sunday's meeting being open to both Sexes there was a larger attendianco than at the weekly P.S.A. meetings, and still more would probably have been present, bad not the weather been so fine as to make irrioar meetings unponula.r. Mr. G. Thompson, of Great Sankey, pre- sided, and exoussod his sympathy with the movement. He honed that as they were now beginning a new cen- tury a greater eff art would be made to increase both the membership and the usefulneits of the society.—Dr. Waite, of Liverpool, delivered the addrtst. and made some instruc'ive observations on the unconscious life of the body. He &amp;scribed it as a great manufacturing plant which existid in every living body and which was ai:ir work from the mom-mt of birch to the moment of death, and was engaged in a most wonderf al process of converting food into the blood, the frame, the bone and the tissuo of the body. Ho proceeded to show the difference between the con- scious and the tricolacious life of the body and enter-d into a physiologicai ex tmination of each. He stated that the; were the result of pure wisdom, intelligence and (goodness, and what were these but the attributes of God ? These bodies of ours whether we realised it or not, were sil ,nt wirriPsAi sto Divine wisdom. If w,. appr:,ciated more their wonderful organisation we should not be inclined to abuse them by excess or over-indul- gence, but on toe other hand we should desire to culti- vate gratitude, obedience and love to Him, who had created us.—Dr. Waite's son—Mr. W H. Waite—recited a pathetic story of a School Board Officr-r relating to slum life in London.—Mr. R. H. Pearson gave a brilliant interpretation of Handel's Sonata in A on the violin.—Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus, Bac., presided at the orgau.—A collection wag taken on behalf of the P.S.A. funds, and the Rev. J. Y-,nge in appealing for a generous collection, pointed out that there was a deficit of about £l7.</p>
        <p>Christian Education. The Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth, vicar of S. Barnabas', preached a sermu on Sunday evening on " Christian Education," taki Ja for his text the words " Tr ain up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not depart from it." He said that the Church. of Eagland acted wisely in te4ching religion in the day schools. They wanted their children to know what their religion was and meant. Now, in all fair- ness, was there anything wrong in the Church desiring her children to klow her faith ? Charges wPre brought against the Church in this respect. Some people talked very loudly about pritv,tcraft, sacerdotalisrn, undue influence, bigotry, popery and th 3 like. He did not suppose for a moment that .hey knew the meaning of those words. Roman Catholics and Dissenters taught their children their religion in their day schools and no charge was bvnt.lit againat them. Why should those charges, then, be preferred against the clergy of the Church of England? Some peep!. said it was very hard on a Ussenter if lie had to send his child to a Church of England school because there was no other in the neigh. bourhood. and the child was taught what the parent did not believe. That was a spacious argument if it were true, but it was not. In the Education Act there was a conscience clause which prpyioed that if the father did riot want his children to be taught what the Church of England held to be true, he had only to give notice to the headmaster, a.ed the child would be withdrawn from religious instruction. Referring to the teaching carriad on in the scho•als, the rev. gentleman said there was nothing in the Churoh Catechism that anyone who pro- fessed to believe in the able should object to. Every atom of the Catechism could be proved by the Bible. Church of the Sacred Heart. The acnnal sliaoll germ i-s were preached in the Church eaf the Saerad Heart, Bank Quay on Sunday by the Rev. Father Yate., B.J. of Liverpool. In the morn- ing he spoke on " Christian E lueatinn," and the subject of th evening's sermon was the " B:essed Trinity." fite F L'her Ityao was the celebrant of the mass. The for the mass was Father Turret's " MASS of th- G xl S r .pherd." this b iing the third time that the no io,c been ;io aril in Warrington. The music is by no menaßy.hutbchoie rendered it most efficiently A, ,tae offenory Neakhinnfa " Ave Maria " (witti flute °Agate) was rencl,ral in an admirable manner by Miss A Fairclonth (snprario). a Agnes Richardson (alto). Mr John Gib t ins, (*vas) and Mr. Alban Boyle, flautist. The choir was under the cmaductorship of Mr. John Gibhine and Miss M BAlian presided at the ergan. Tee choirmaster and organist are deserving of praiee ler the manner in which thy have trained the choir.</p>
