        <p>SPRING CLEANING COMPE CITION.</p>
        <p>We have received such a number of really good parodies on Kipling's celebrated verses that we should like very much to give the priz: to several dozens or them. As that, how- ever, is impossible we h tve hid to make a choice and have decided upon which we think is really the most appropriate. We publish this one and also a sele,!tion of the others, not adjudging them the best of those which have been sent in but as a representative selection. We desire to thank all those who have taken the trouble to send us contributions towards this competition. L3was AWARDED to EDWARD H. DAVIS, Harbor:le, for the following verses :—BOOTs</p>
        <p>AIWIOI3 to HOUall</p>
        <p>When you're started with Spring Cleaning—ad you want</p>
        <p>to do it clean _ (And you know you cP.niot do it with your mouth)— Will you kindly buy a battle of our ' rurnitewrer Cream. Which is used in East and West and North and South. It's a powerful kind of compound, and it's very good to mix, And you and Jane can tske it as you find it. When it's used in ac.ive service, cleaning stains from off the " sticks," You will find it wont leave many marks behind it. Boots shop, Boots shop, shop of a hundred things, An en, erprising Company, its Kit the rage to-day, Everything sold for the lowest cash (as you can all see for yourselves). Then step in ladies, take your choice, and pay I pay t pay!</p>
        <p>There wre wives who do it secret—asking no permission to— For their husbands wouldn't give it if they did ; But suppose you use oar polish"—it will make things look</p>
        <p>- like new— - And you need not stop the work "cog" there's a kid. You could do your cleaning casual—without worry—when</p>
        <p>he's gone, For if the beggar's absent ne7er mind him. You might use our " Ammonia," which would take</p>
        <p>away the mell Of tobaceo--which he always leaves behind him. Boots shop, Boots shop, ere.</p>
        <p>You ckn manage so as later, when you lock him in the face. You can tell him what he'll think is oaly fair, How that while he earned the wages yolk had tidied up the</p>
        <p>place And had polished up his special old arm chair. He's an absent-minded beggir, and he may forget it all. But you do not want his Kiddies to remind him That you locked them in the coal house while you gleaned his house and hall, Awl were brightining up the home he'd left behind him. Boots shop, Boots shop, etc.</p>
        <p>When you've started on your cleaning, when you're scour— hag all your rooms, Use " Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing," made by Wills, You can buy it for a shilling. and you 11 find As pleasant fumes All the dir, nd noxious germs quite quickly kills, Boots' an advertising beggar, but his stores are very great. And 1 and you must take him as we find him, He'd sooro-r have your cash than put it en a slate. And he's leavi-,g all the other shops behind him. Cooks Boots, dukes Boots, Boots of h hundred kings, Boots of a market public iu,he'll fit them all.we say; With Varnish, or t.;r -am. and Paint, and thousands Of other things, Ask for them all for your cleaning's sake and pay, pay. pay. There are girls who make no secret,'cos they'velno occasion to., Boots Cream to polish furniture is good, That Gold Paint as made oy Morgan is the finest thing that you Can gild upon old ormolu or wood. There are girls you talk to casual, will assure you that they love To polish up their brasses, and their tins, With Paste tha's sold at Boots, so much better, and above All others, covering multitudes of sins.</p>
        <p>Cooks' sins, dukes' sins, sins of a belted 'sari, Fifty thousand soldiers' sins, gone from them astray, Buttoas and buckles and gun, will shine our khaki girl, Lay it quick for your polishing's sake, to-day. day, day. There are families by thousands using Boatses Oaken Stairs To furbish up bare spots 3round their fl iors, It saves them yards ot carpet, for it will wash again When varislied well to close the open pores. You can polish up the chairs with the Cream they sell Boots— You need not go to Africa to find him. He has so many branches, from scores of good old roots. He is mostly At the fruit, nor looks behind him Dukes' Cream, cooks' Stain, Polish for metals, Gold Paint, These are the things you will ask for going to Boots to-Jay, With Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing, to save you from a faint, lake yoar cash in your pocket and pay. pay,pay. Nom de plume JAJOB WILSON. Nottingham.</p>
        <p>If your slavey goes and leaves you when spring cleaning's coming on, And you cannot get another maid that suits. I will tell ion mw to manage with little extra toil, Just walk off to toe nearest shop of BOOTS. They are eivii and obliging, von will very quickly find, Their goods are cheap and-tresh and up to date. And you'll buy for say one shilling what most firms would sell for three. As they trade for cash and do not keep a slate. Pure Drugs, Gold Paint. Furniture Cream and Pills. Stuff tor saving elbow grease. powders and various roots, Stains and Polish of every kind, things that will cure all ills, it3o cease to fret, but put nn your shoes and hist try BOOI'S. If you think your chairs sod tables get a little worse tor wear, . And the sideboard is not what it used to seam, Don't bre sk your cups and saucers aad say you wish you're dead. For 4d. only need be spent on Cream. With a rag apply it gently. any child can put it on, as easy if you want to stain the floor. Spend another 44d. on a pot or Varnish Stain, ' Then your home will look just as it did of yore. Soft Soap, Brunswick Black, Paste for a hundred tins. Oils and Paints of every kind, Cloudy Ammonia. too, Bach of them certain todo its work, just as you're told it will, So keep home clean for your credit's sake and you'll not rue. When you've finished in the bedroom, and the parlour and the Althonah yotere well repaid do ft think you're done, There's the p .ts and pails and kettles want a little clean- ing out, So again to 1300 i'S you'll have to run; Get a tin of lets' Polish, jii3e one peony does the trick, No stains or sera coos you wl.l ever trace, Then you'll sing about your housework and be glad you are siiv.., And in everything you'll see a smiling face, Cash Chemists, Good Chemists, things at the lowest rate, Everything superior for a price that always suits. 50,000 hou,.ewives sing praise of one accord, And never deal ,with another firm but Boo Ts. Boors, BOOTS. F. JOIINSOS, 4, Victoria-rd., Penketh, near Warrington.</p>
        <p>When the c.lprlngtime surreptitiously steals on us unawares With a sudden light ff pale and sickly sun, Then the prudent British matron takes to polishing the stairs, And there's lota of other house-work to be done. So in order that the mansian may be thoroughly made bright. BOOTS VARNISH STarss and other things are bought. And the hubby's consolation is in thoughts of speedy flight. For he dorm'', understand things as he ought. Rub, rto, scrub, scrub, ain't it awful work. So the hubby thinketh, though he really doesn't. know One little bit about it when he fancies terrors lurk. And the hest thing he can do is to go, go, go. Oh. the saving in the coal bide with thecoming of the Spring. When the black bacteria wobble in their flight, And the income tax c llectors and the birds begin to sing, And the dirty smoky c-thugs turn to white. If you view it as you ought to it's a most congenial time, With UNRIVALLED METAL I)4r..tsa to the fore. In burnithing doo: handles and removing all the grime from the metals while the women scrub the floor. Rub, rub, scrub. e rub, making thirigs look like new. PUI-lIV TURF: OREsM and BRUNSWICK BLACK are ;be article that pay, Buy all you want at Boots, you'll keep that well in view? And do your work when hubby's out of the way. way, way. When the wcrk is nearly over there's a transformation scene, And you don't regret you've t ulkted it in time, As you realize the n.ct that now your howe is nice and clean. „., To have kit it dirty would nave been a crime. Oh I the glory f h carpets which now look as geld as new, Oh I the briglv n-es or the fenders and the chairs, surprisin to the nubby as he takes a startled view. And the wife enjays hie wolder as he stares. What, what I what, what I done the whole lot in a day? (Heaven seems to open to the guilty man who scouts) Wonderful 1 how did you do it? Tell me your secret pray, " I did tall with things I bought from Boots. Boots, Boots."</p>
