        <p>THE Temperance question is Problems of not so simple as it looks to Temperance. be. It is a compound Of several problems, and no single solution can be found for all of them. As a rule, the logical tasu distrusts the single remedy for any evil, and he i 5 well within his rights in holding the same attitude towards temperance reform. The legislative sphere of action is considerable. If we were to accept some enthusiasts as competent guides, we should bend our energies towards a single point prohibition. If you forbid the sale of intoxicants' it is said, you nrist reduce all the evils connected with the use and abuse of alcoholic beverages' The facts are not so strong as the theory. Drunken" ness does not absolutely cease, as by magic,where prohibition is the rule. There are always ways of getting liquor, even in States where prohibition i 5 the law of the land, and there is always a con" siderable proportion of intemperance. To (rive the community a better control over the sale of liqu°f is not the same as prohibiting its sale. The liquor itself can be sold in a purer state—for there are alcohols and alcohols—the hours can be regulated' the customers that shall be served can be defined, and sales to children forhidden,and the punishment for offences can be made more severe. To naurli" cipalise the public-house rather than to forbid its existence is a remedy that finds increasing favour' The abuses of the tied house system can only be met by legislation. The difficulties in the way of legislation are due to the vested interests, and to the votes they can command in general elections. Just now, in this country, the votes of the trade naturally go for that side which is tho more likelY to resist the demands of the temperance reformer. That :side happens to be Unionist and Lord SALISBURY and several members of the present Government are hostile to any important legisla" tion. In another general election, after resisting legislation, either on the basis of the Licensing Commission or on any other, the party in power iS almost sure of retaining the votes and the col&amp; deuce of everybody in the trade of making and of selling alcoholic beverages. What hope is there ef real licensing reform under such conditions ? We lear very little. The best hope of reform is ill mutual declarations that licensing reform is not A party or political question. It can only be achieved by unity and agreement, and the best use of temperance congresseS and temperance agitation is that they tend to promote this agreement, under the conscientious fooling that a great evil exists and, must be resolutely grappled with. The whole 01 the Christian Churches should unite to lift the temperance problem out of the low plane of Part/ strife. Until this can be done we have very little hope of that power of local experiment whleb would lead to an adjustment of systems to the needs and wishes of different localities. It vifie proposed at the Congress to take Church action in another sense; to exclude all drink-sellers fr°nl Communion and to make abstinence a conditiell of Church membership. This would be a verl extreme step indeed, and it would ill accord witil British sentiment and ideas of justice. It would still be incomplete, unless all public positiorl! were denied to persons in the wholesale or rettli We trade, and they were virtually disfranchised. 0 shall not be likely to see a serious movement e 0„, these lines in England. Our action will be ro. stricted to legislative work and to moral suasie,o In short, half the problem to be faced consisti /A a better understandinc, of what can be done what cannot be done. It is clear that we esunot-04 banish alcohol from a free community. We re,er make up our minds to that, or we shall us'' achieve anything real at all.</p>
        <p>EVENTS are produell To Accept a more unity of feelioo Annexation. about South Africa, ail the position of the Liberal party upon annecati: has been made clearer by the speeches of Sir CAMPBELL-BANNEIDIAN and Mr. JoHN 31°Itifeli Good Liberals everywhere will accordinglY of to accept annexation as the inevitable resut;;o war,and as in accord with national sentiment. 1- 5 situation is one in which the Boers themOlveof havo rendered assistance by, it may be, a feelillleo hopelessness, a:conviction of the ultimate jns;v of British rule, or a want of political solgac7i; Even in the Orange River Colony there has Dely no attempt whatever to establish a new GnviAc-to ment when the Steyn Administration fly—a Government which might have tre'pg for peace upon independent lines. Perl/380 the materials were wanting, the elperial:oo was regarded as useless, and the deterrainatio to keep the war going manifest awaY frri° Bloemfontein mwle it impossible to form eve-to, Committee. When Lord ROBERTS entered Bile fontein he took over all authority, and ho c't co, authority has appeared to dispute his dorniean,ja Even in the Transvaal the occupation of Fret°l has practically destroyed the South Africa' 11:0; public and its Executive. Mr. KRUGER asSlit it that where he is there the Government is. 331.104 has no authority in the Western Transvaal, the general submission of the towns to authority is an indication that wherever civil:if:: 1' 4. jog can find expression it regards the indeperitA of the country as gone. There are stillicseu'rpeirty commandos in the field,but they seem and a common purpose. They isiareiB:eatbo parts of what was once a coe ... acting under definite leadership, and :land ported by a central authority cainun:bnaoloo4. of all the resources of the State. dist. ac'f.r now before Lord BMWS proeee</p>
        <p>I.-</p>
        <p>l'rp.ansvaal what he has already done for the Orange "er Colony. He will annex it, he will change its 141.atee, and he will hand over the command of Pre- , "eria, if he has not already done it, to a military NicY• As we conquer, we must assume responsi- , bilitY for rule. No organisation exists with which We can make terms, and by the tune Mr. KRUGER akea up his mind to surrender or ask for peace, vr be ceases to be a free agent, nearly the whole thecountry will have passed out of his control. the had remained at Pretoria, he might have made belles as representing de facto Government. He oas Preferred to leave the centre of his former )a.evernment, and to attempt the reorganisation of 1,8 beaten forces, who will be beaten again Wueuever they elect to run the risk of tlO let. It is thus quite obvious that annexa- ei.o4—ugly as the word may appear to some politi- oans----is the only practical policy for the moment. °licluest had made it so, and conquest was the Way in which to proceed. The two Republics thave become British, and that is what they risked Chance of becoming when they began the war. m ere i 3 no necessity to advance beyond this point Present. Conquest is incomplete, annexation is ent Wholly decided, but the two are certain and al. National sentiment is thus in agreement 411 the facts of the case, and it would be inex- ni/e4ieut, to put it mildly, for the Liberals to range 7,erhselve3 against facts and against the national This is not fatalism, however, but common kilsa, and it puts an end to a good deal of con_ eversy and division. Accepting annexation, we 74 free to deal with a new situation, and in no Pirit ot haste and impatience.</p>
        <p>thThe census in America has, as usual, revealed existence of a considerable number of incorri- t4 cranks among the populations of the big wee• It is difficult to understand what satisfac- ,(34 any citizen can derive from covering his census Per with stupid remarks, yet we know that °laxly ten years ago, on the last numbering of our people, there were many cases in which the Zaehinery of the police-courts had to be put in I.2tien, and small fines inflicted upon otherwise ;73Pectable householders for having filled up laaesbt spaces on the forms with foolish words; nor tht'ere any reason to doubt that when next year teeieeurnerators make their call upon the people a gen number of papers will again be returned ofribbled over with comments about the impudence 111 the questioner. In America, it seems, the 4,;441 ber of useless forms was so large that unless thi°,llB steps are taken to enforce compliance with th,e 'my' the results of the whole census will be ap- r,eelably vitiated. So widespread, indeed, is the -4Lesal to answer the questions of a paternal I,,h(P?errmient that some people have been asking to ether the flippancy displayed does not cover oftreh strong religious objection to the enumeration Sr -se People, basedi on a passage in the Old Testa- r it) such as n the past produced almost a state ev • in this country. At all events, the foqierican papers cannot be accused of complicity, tore jest before the date appointed their columns pry_nled with appeals for fair treatment of the re- Citizens chosen as enumerators, some of kr!ch unconsciously suggested the pathetic in- over the head of the pianist in the wild est) " Don't shoot; he is doing his best."</p>
        <p>Ev -- th erYone who has handled a pair of reins knows ota,.; a horse's action is determined by the nerves 'ne driver. No physician can judge the qualities 11,,4 Patient so quickly as the quadruped reckons ilti'athe determination of the man or woman who , lcuortakes its control. But it is not generally sarnwn how in warfare the horse is guided in the Se;° way by the conduct of a company of soldiers. Afrei ral stories, however, have citne from South targa to illustrate the fact. It is related that a wherer„sergeant at Ladysmith was shoeing a hone sjhehl came screaming through the air. The his °r°ke before the sergeant could make up ncl to move ; the animal,relying on the men's bco.7"ll°rlt, kept its foot in the man's apron, and ull escaped unhurt. Mr. Noble, of the 7th ~n,tliskillings, tells that in charges properly sus- horses keep their places in harmody when ‘4eir own riders have been shot down, which is !,arrying the Principle a little further; they are ciiii.inated by the courage of the mass. When, on thte contrary, there is a retreat, the horses of the liaet are the first to dash into camp. In this they quick to recognise the desire for escape. The of this intelligence makes the thought of the ;sufferings of those wounded and left on the field "I"nore terrible to contemplate. But most fre- Zleutly the cavalry soldier has a spare bullet to :c1 their miseries when he sees an animal in pain. 11c1 if his horse is cut down under him he will run '"roe risk to relieve it in this way.</p>
        <p>--- 4p.T_o-niorrew our local Volunteers return home It'e,r a fortnight's hard training on Conway Morfa. th nas been a real period of instructive work and baere is every reason to believe that the men who thv° had the benefit of it are far better soldiers an. they were at any previous time. The war in 4^3_o4th Africa has taught us the value of Volunteers t'Ecl what is perhaps more important still,the value eE4cient Yolunteersa is no reflection on anyone ei 4,37 that the training which was usually asso- gt4."-Led with the annual encampment of a few days to"Ie seaside, did not give Volunteers that experi- iloet3 and knowledge of military work which are required of the Volunteer if he is to take the Le" hgainst the enemy. For many years we have ttell under the impression that our volun- -I:tts were for purely defensive purposes Af after recent experiences i a South ;ea we can no longer fondle that tEv."3lon. The Volunteer is new a soldier in 110,:rve and such proceedings as those which have let' Witnessed at Conway during the past fort- tipt are certain to heighten the efficiency of the thez'3ll soldier. Our own men have acquitted Illselves admirably and won warm praise for the 4,411ner in which they have performed their new `"-t niore exacting duties.</p>
        <p>Conway canvas picnic has undergone a change this year, as most of the Volunteers 4Lit) hY now, have found out. In addition to a fort- training being insisted upon, much of the arhelliekY element has been eliminated and the h-"ateur "Tom tnies have to do a good deal of ard th and useful work while they are encamped on tunous Mon a. There is somewhat less time 4veted to battalion drill than formerly, a certain htlitl,lllint of monotony being thus dispensed with, 14" the men are put through a course of training th_teilitary works which the present war has shown " value of. Entrenching is one of the innovations, 11(1 t °lir volunteers have to exercise their muscles nriling over the earth and other more or less 17 things to form shelter trenches against 41s aginary foe. Scouting and range-firing are th° included, and by the end of the fourteen days eivlri9 Will be many a thousand vigorous young -lans who know much more about soldiering ita4llthey did a fortnight ago. There is one other bl2"3rtant item in the day's routine, and that is the seilatt,hing parade" by which a whole battalion are of thinte the cool sea at once. This should be one d° duties most enjoyed, for what healthy youth not love a plunge in the sparkling briny? tenl any of those at the Morfa have been accus- Nhtli_to surreptitious dips in canals and ponds to CO Yof the sea, and the new " parade, is fast tavinTeg a popular one. If it teaches the men to well, its value will be immeasurably 'eased.</p>
        <p>there will be considerable satisfaction felt by th4tP.Ttale of this part of Lancashire at the news Promoters of the electric express mono- to 0 3l between Liverpool and Manchester intend tio„ling a new Bill probably before the next ses- thr- °I Parliament: Though the preamble. was the 'wt! out by Sir John Kennaway's committee, I:Ystoe,Pl4,i°o was expressed that "the mono-rail Itto„-.7 is likely when fully matured to make an tail-wr‘atit contribution to the development of liistliaYf traffic at hioth speed." Such a pronounce- -80 authoritative a quarter ought to be</p>
        <p>more than sufficient to convince anybocly that Mr Behr's system, notwithstanding presen:,: r_:buffs,has. a (great future before it, And with a capital of £2,000,000 behind it, in the hands of shrewd practical Lancashire men, who shall deny it success ? The opposition is not of a very alarming nature, and judicious negotia- tion ought to make the way clear for Par- liawent to pass the Bill when next it comes before hem. It must not be forgotten that introducing a Bill of this kind into Parliament is an expensive matter,and the promoters of the mono-railway pro- bably spent £50,000 in their ineffectual endeavour to secure the sanction of the House of Commons Committee a few weeks ago. We hope that their second attempt may meet with the success it de-</p>
