        <head>servss.</head>
        <p>servss.</p>
        <p>The Daresbary Sehol Board have just extricated themselves from a difficulty which raised a very fine point as to the extent to which free education can be demanded. Mr. Weir, one of the members of the Board, has two children afflicted with deaf- ness, and at the present moment they are inrn4tes of the Royal Schools for the Deaf and Dumb at Old Trafford. As a ratepayer, Mr. Weir is called upon to pay the School Board rate which provides free education for the children in the township,and the question arose whether Mr Weir was not in a position to demand that the ratepayers should like- wise pay for the education of his children. A difference of opinion existed on the School Board and the Board of Education was appealed to. The reply which was read at the meeting on Thursday last week is very explicit. The Board of Education state that _ the Elementary Education (Blind and Deaf Children) Act of 1893 expressly recognises the right to free education. In possession of such a document as this the School Board could not do other than come to terms with Mr. Weir, and in future h 3 is to receive a grant of 16 a year to- wards the education of his two deaf children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Though journalists appear to have made up their minds that the situation in China is fraught with greater cause for uneasiness than that in South Africa, it is curious to observe with what apparent ineiffereace the man in the street regards John Chinaman and his mysterious" Boxers," while still , hanging about newspaper shops and office,s,eager to acquaint himself wite the la! est news as to the movements of Lord Roberts or General Buller. Any- how, this is a noteworthy circumstaace in the pro• vinces. The split among the Afrikanders has been of more importance in his sight than the march upon Pekin and the news of more murdered mis- sionaries. And so it promises to be to the end of the chapter, or the campaign. Then, perhaps, the "foreign devils in China, and even the famine- stricken natives of our Indian Empire may be ex- pected to receive a larger share of that attention which, in the latter case, has, to our lasting dis- credit,been so scantily bestowed upen the sufferers. True,the gansion House Fund constitutes a praise- worthy contribation, and many parts of the pro- vinces have subscribed liberally, but the sufferers now number nearly six million persons receiving starvation allowance,and millions more who punish themselves to keep their kindred alive, while com- mittees are being formed in Boston and New York, and a further appeal is made for help. Yet the war appears to be nearly all the average Briton can think of; and for many reasons others will rejoice exceedingly when peace ha e been proclaimed, not merely for the great blessing of peace itself, but so many other things which it involves that are at present so lamentably neglected. There is little doubt that under the altered con- ditions which will shortly prevail in South Africa, English emigrants will be attracted to the Orange River Colony and the Transvaal, while many of the reservists and Yeomanry now there will settle in the country, if they receive the least encourage- ment from the Government. In the near future, a South African Customs Union will no doubt be formed, and facilities will be afforded for the de- velopment of trade. Already German and Austrian manufacturers are making arrangements in view of new openings in South Africa, and Englishmen are not likely to miss the opportunities created under the new regime. But here comes a strong note of warning. Sir Alfred Milner has more than once begged of the authorities at home to caution the ordinary class of emigrant against going out there to swell the number of the unemployed, undeethe existing state of things. The labour market is greatly overstocked, the Colonies in a very unset- tled condition awl likely to be so for some time to come, and the country not at all suitable for those who have no capital and imagine they have nothing to do but get there and enjoy the products of the soil with scarcely any trouble. The Emigrants' In- formation Office has now emphasised the warnings of the High Commissioner, and strongly enforces the inadvisableness of men , without any capital going out to the two new colonies in the present depressed condition of the labour market. The Government may be relied : upon to say when there is a reasonable prospect for emigration, but their first duty will naturally be to provide, if possible, for those Volunteers and time- expired men of the reserves whoweut out as much with the intention of settling there, as of fighting the battles they have fought. Under the most favourable conditions, it will be some time before the mines can be placed in working order, and numbers of those now rushing to the Rand, as they imagine, in defiance of repeated warnings, will be detained at ports of call and made to suffer for their folly. By-and-bye it will be different,and the prospects become good for those who exercise proper judgment and do not make the mistake so many are now making. Dissolution rumoers have not been so prevalent or the forecasts so warmly discussed, within the pest week as they were at the beginning of the month. It has begun to dawn upon the minds of enthusiasts that the war may not so readily or conveniently adapt itself to the exigencies of Parliamentary elec- tioneering as had about the end of May been gener- ally anticipated. So that at present few of the wire- pullers appear any longer disposed to favour a July election. October is the month favoured by the Prophets, and by many members of Parliameet, for the reason that it is considered a good time for the agriculturists. and is expected that some fifty thousand or more yeomen and reservists will then have returned from South Africa, not merely to swell the ranks of voters, but to " officiate " as Khaki electioneering agents. But "there's many a slip "in electioneering, as in other matters, and October will not prove an ideal month bra General Election this year. having regard to other impor- tant elections that will hepoen about the same time. "As 'Bobs' decides" is becoming a favourite phrase, but even that is not, everything, and April of next year is not at all out of the running. Rifle Clubs are springing into existence in every part of the provinces, and much dissatisfaction is caused in the club circles at what is regarded as the inadequate assistance proposed to be rendered to the movement by the War Office. But much may be expected from a proposal on the part of the Birmingham Rifle Club, to first of all hold a general conference of all the clubs that may send represents- : tivee, and then and there decide upon some plan of combined action to make their influence felt in the proper quarters. It has, indeed, been determined to hold the conference at Birmingham. as being the town most centrally situated and suitable for such meeting, and there is no doubt that independently of the question of State assistance which can be raised later on, the conference if established as a periodical gathering, would, by its labours, he able materially to assist the formation of other clubs,and to formulate a practical organisation eventually governing the whole movement for the common advantage of all the towns concerned. - It is net generally recognised that, as an eminent legal authority has just reminded us, children of Board Schools are by enactment prohibited from being regularly employed out of school hours. It is very doubtful whether the fact is generally known, and, anyhow, it will not be disputed that a change of occupation during the day is in many cases desirable, even with adults; no is it easy to find any reasonable objection to children being occasionally employed, as they often are, by trades- men and housekeepers, to go errands after their day's duties are over at school. A change of light employment, within reasonable limits as to hours and the nature of the task, can scarcely be other than conducive to health and strength, after con- finement for come hours in a schoolroom. But the reminder, as such, appears to be worthy of note. What other municipal authorities might think of It, we are not likely to know until they have been asked, as was the Chief Constable ef Glamorgan, when Major Lucas, officer of recruiting in that dis- trict,requested that the services of the police officers L might be utilised for that particular kind of mid-</p>
        <p>tary duty. Having been authorized by the Chair- man of the Police Committee, the Head Constable " conformed" to the request, at a time or pressure, of course, on account, of the war, and with eminently satisfactory results, from the " recruit- ing sergeant's" pDint of view. And, what is more noteworthy still, perhaps, the Chief Constable has just reported the matter to the Standing Joint Committee of ,:,he county, whose Chairman was commended for his conduct in the matter,and there the matter ends ; proving that the Standing Joint Committee, its Chairman, and the Head Constable of Glamorgan are a very accommodating people, with liAle of that fear of ratepayers and other unsympathising outsiders before their eyes, that sometimes disturbs the serenity of other similar bodies elsewhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TTTIE EX MINER. FRI OA..Y, JUNE 15, 1900.</head>
        <p>TTTIE EX MINER. FRI OA..Y, JUNE 15, 1900.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A BAD CIIA.RACTEIL_</p>
        <p>Ellen Murphy, alias "Kernel," of Widnes, was on Wednesday charged before Messrs, W. Timmins and W. Handley with aiding and abetting in the keep- ing of a house of ill repute at Runcorn, and sent- enced to one month with hard labour. THE TEIUNDERSTORIf AND ITS gFFECTS.</p>
        <p>During icinday and Tuesday the district was visited by a very svere thunderstorm, and the extra oidinary phenomenon of sunshine and heavy rain was noticeable in Runcorn, whilst almost through the whole of the Frodsham diwrict the country was rendered white by an extraordinary fall of hail. The vast district almost escaped in- jury but a house situate at Clifton was struck by lightning and suffered terribly. The tenant,an aged woman, was in bed at the time and though the bed was severely damaged by the lightning she mar- vellously escaped injury. THE GIRL, THE BOY, AND THE TEAPOT. A singular accident occurred at Higher Runcorn, on Wednesday evening; June I3th. A boy named Minshall aged about seven years, whilst running up an entry between Lif.,/htburn-streeu and Curzon st., callidcd with a girl who was passing from one house to another, carrying a pot of hot tea. The paar little fellow ran head first into the pat of bailing tea, sustaining severe cuts on the face and being bad!y scalded on the breast. Dr. Ormsby WAS soon in attendance and problounced the boy to be' in a serious condition. RUNCORN POLICE COURT. _ .</p>
        <p>On Saturday, he-fore Nle,ssrs G. L. Davies and W. Handley, Alice Johnson, a well-known character, and George Tomlinson, of Bate's Bridge, were charged with disorderly behaviour the previous evening. Ernest Pailips, employed on a steam tile, was charged with being drunk on the canal bank at the same time and place. —P.C.'s Gilpin and Gregory proved the charges.—Johnson. against whom the police had a lengthy record, was com- mitted to gaol for one month's hard labour. Toin- linson was fined lOs and 5s costs, or 14 days' hard labourand Philips was fined 5s or seven days' hard</p>
        <p>labour. DROWNING FATALITY AT WESTON POINT. Donald Peter Burrows Bain, the six year old son of Mr. Daniel Bain, joiner, of Weston Point, went to school on Wednesday morning last as usual. About 11 o'clock he came back and asked his mother for something to eat which was given to him, ani he went out and was net seen ag,rain by his mother, The boy went back to school which place he left after school hours in company with another boy named Henry Hughes (aged 4). Both boys went into Post Office-lane, and the by Bain climb° I over the fence on to the Canal Bridge. The little boy Hughes was then seen and called home by his mother, and nothing more was seen of Bain. About one o'clock George Bain, uncle of the bay went to the canal and found the boy's cap floating on the water. The conclusion was then arrived at that the little one must have fallen in and on the canal being subsequently dragged by William Makin the boy's body was recovered The matter has been re- ported to the coroner. HOLY TRINITY CHURCH, RUNCORN. The anniversary of the Sabbath schools connected with Holy Trinity Church was ohserved on Sunday: In the morning the service was conducted by the Vicar (Rev C R Killick, M A.), and the pulpit was occupied by the Rev Wright Williams, vicar of Fainworth, who based a most appropriate discourse on Dent. vi. 7, "And thou shalt teach them dili- gently unto thy children." A children's service was held in the afternoon at which the Vicar addressed the scholars from the text: "I am the Light of the world." The evening preacher was the Reg G B Thurston, rector of Lymm. The sermon was founded on the text, from the 14th Psalm That our sons may grow up as the young plants, and that our daughters may be as the polished corners of the temple." The scholars had benefited from a careful and painstaking training at the hands of Mr. EL Turner, and their rendering of a number of specially selected hymns did both them and their conductor the greatest possible credit. Two of the most striking pieces were "You are a soldier of Christ the Lord" and " I wandered forth at eventide." They were assisted by the choir in most cases, and the general opinion was that the musical portion of the services was well done. Mr. Grica was at the organ. The offertories were in advance of those of last year.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. [BY A CORRESPONDENT]. There appears to be a slight disagreement be- tween the boat-leaders and the Nlanchester Ship Canal Company. Since Monday last the men have declined to work. The question at issue is the vexed one of "more pay" but as things appear at. present the qtrestion is likely to be settled amicably The men have made their demand and the ship Canal Company have shown a disposition to meet them, but in the meantime the attitude of the "loaders" is imperilling the employment of a great number of the boat-people. The Anderton Company and others will be likely to suffer heavily unless the matter is quickly settled.</p>
        <p>As promised there appears this week in the columns of this edition a short history of the rise of "Old Brunswick." It is reserved for me however, to make mention of the fact, that Joha Wesley him- self missioned Frodsham and Preston Brook, which was at that time known as Preston-o'th-Hill. Here a Sunday school was founded, and it might be of in- terest to Preston Brook inhabitants to know that their school possesses a greater antiquity that that of Runcorn, and that too it probably owed its in- ception to the great Wesley himself. Far many years &amp;tr. Simpson (Runcorn) possessed a square of glass taken from the Preston Brook Chapel win- dow which had upon it the date in which John Wesley visited the chapel. It would be interesting to trace this and get it put back in its original place.—The little act of vandalism which robbed Preston Brook of its treasure is attributed to a Runcorn minister ; how interesting, therefore, would it not be to see a _Runcorn minister the restorer of the relic?</p>
        <p>Fishing is the prevailing "fever " in Runcorn. Everyone possesses a fi 43ing rod and tackle, fish basket and bait tin, but the sight of a gool catch is always conspicuous by its absence. Now. I don't care very much about fish, and don't want any of my readers to deluge my domicile with the product of their lines and rolls, but I would rosily like to see some of the specimens I hear so much talked about. Each time a certain" fisher " goes out he catches a,bigger one—and as he has been out often of late—l should like to see the SiZ3 of the one next .due.</p>
        <p>The opinion is going round in Runcorn as well as elsewhere that if there is to be a general elec- tion this year it will be very late in the autumn. Of course, to the Liberals of the Runcorn portion of the North wich Division it matter little whether the election comes soon or late. Now, as at all times,they are prepared. They may not be exactly "spoiling for a fight and there may be certain things in the present aspect of political affairs that cause divergence of opinion, but running through all criticism there is a spirit of gratitude to Sir John Brunner for good already done, and a spirit of loyalty to party and to Sir John himself that. would need much more than a slight divergence of opinion to cause an estrangement. What the mem- bers of the Liberal Party are more concerned about is—are they going to have the chance of testing their strength against their opponents?</p>
        <p>Are they going to have the chance of unsheath- ing their swords and keeping their armour bright? Twelve months ago there was a great parade made of a forthcoming Conservative Candidate,then there was quite a jubilant song of a most palatial club in a conspicuous position in the town. Not for four months has a whisper been heard of either. Are the Tories of the division really dead? Or do they only sleen ? It would appear on the face of things that Sir John's groat majority of 1895 has given them a pause that will last until the next general election is over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>BRUNSWiCK SUNDAY</head>
        <p>BRUNSWiCK SUNDAY</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>IN RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>NONCONFORMITY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HISTORY OF</head>
        <p>HISTORY OF</p>
        <p>Brunswick and St Paul's.</p>
        <p>This (Friday) evening Runcorn Wesleyan Metho- diets commence, with due pomp and solemnity, the celebration of the Centenary of their Sunday School work in the Town. And as no fitter occa- sion for the review of the history of Nonconformity could present itself, it is with feelings of diffidence, at the many shortcomings that an article of this nature must necessarily possess, that I undertake such a task. There are some evidences to show that the ham- let of Runcorn was first visited by Lvan zelical tioinistera as early as 1780, just 120 years ago, but it is obscure as to what denomination they repre- sented. As is well-known,that year was the one in which Robert Raikes commenced his first Sunday School Movement, by the teaching ot the unkempt urchins in his rude workshop,and to trace Evangel- ism in Runcorn, in however remote and raeagre a form it might be found, to that historic year would be interesting. Evidence, we have, though, in the statement of M.r. W Myles, a minister connected with the Liverpool circuit, of the first attempt at planting the seeds of Nonconformity in Runcorn, for in a memoir he has handed do ma we find the statement That when he came to, Runcorn in 1787, for the purpose of preaching. . . . he was forced out of the village.' Rude and vigorous was the treatment meted out to these pioneers of Wesleyanism, yet still were their efforts renewed ; and, at length, in 1789, the works of those holy men bore fruit; and in that year it was no uncammon sight to see Sun- day after Sunday, al frzsco services where, truly. religion was free, and hence more comforting and enjoyable. Time strengthened the movement, and suosequently a house was kindly placed at the die posal of the Pilgrim Ministers, and utilised as a preaching house" when the weather was too un- propitious to continue services in the open air Later a smell building was fitted up, which was in reality the "first chapel." Its site is doubtful, but evidence seems to point to it being in the vicinity of the Locks of Brindley's great .creation—the Bridgewater Canal. A site, upon that where now stands the Goods ,Station of the London and North-Western Railway Company, was the next selection, and here a cot- tage was secured, oeo.upiecl as a chapel, and -in 180) a Sunday School was commenced, but by whom it is difficult to trace. Eighteen hundred appears an authentic date, however, from the additional fact that in 1837, the Brunswick school acquired a new silk banner, and had painted upon it .t he data 1800 "as well as a figure and the name of the school. Indeed the banner is still in existenee,and has been seen on many occesions heading the Whit- suntide processions of the Old Brunswick School. The memoirs of the late Rev. William Jones, a Wesleyan minister residing in Runcorn in the yeir 1801—(retired)---acquaint us with the fact that that year he came to Runcorn,end found about 16 members in the Society." Mr. Jones and the several members of his family were, however, vary indus- trious and zealous, and in conjunction with other members of the Wesleyan cause—notably the Hezlehurst's and the Johnson's—combined and in 1807 commenced to build a "good chapel" destined to supersede the " inconvenient preaching room." which had hitherto given the cause a home. This building, which stood in Well-lane, Rancorn, was completed the following year (1808), but has since been incorporated into the works of the Runcorn Soap and Alkali Company Limited. Mr John Datton, Devonshireequare, Rdncorn, has iu his possession, a well preserved " hymn paper" and a "plan of the chapel • giving the pew holders and amount of rental, each of which it is pretty evident were drawn up by the Rev W Jones. Added in- terest i.e also derived from the fact that the Rev W Myles (the very pioneer who was so rudely handled upon his first visit to Runcorn in 1787), and the Rev—Ward, another Liverpool minister, were the very gentlemen who officiated at the opening ser- vice. The building was use.% ten to 1827 ; the year in which Brunswick Chapel was completed and opened. Between the years 1808 and 1827, but possibly in the year 1821, the Sunday school, which was at that time being conducted in "Crowther'a Cottages," in 1 the Old Holloway, found a home in Martin's Geir- dens (Nelson-street), where a Warrington pin factor put an upper room at their disposal: and from whence by means of a light footbridge the scholars could attend morning service at the Well- lane Chapel. In 18I:e ituncorn was attached to the Warrington Circuit and for many years the locals and other minieters used to walk from that place to Runcorn and Preston Brook to perform their holy functions —actuated by a spirit that should emulate the "local" of the present day to put up with his in- conveniences ungrudgingly.</p>
        <p>As the trade and importance of Runcorn increased, the chapel at Well-lane was found inadequate, and efforts were made which resulted in the erection Brunswick Chapel. This was accomplished in 1827, taough one authority puts it a year later. The ; building is described as a brick oae, 54 feat by 48 feet, having a stone pertico in front. It was suit- ably furnished and contained three galleries. Be- neath the chapel was a large scho3lroom,and adjoin- ing the building and facing Brunswick-street was an excellent minister's residence. In the same year 1827, Dr. Beecham was appointed Runcorn's first resident minister, and took his abode in the new residence,since which time Runcorn his never been without a minister or mini,ters of the Wesleyan persuasion. An interesting conversation with qr. John Bir- kett, Greenway-road, and Mrs. Elizabeth Johnsoa, Cooper-street--(two of our oldest inhabitants)— gleaned that the laying of the foundation stone at Brunswick was a most impodng affiir. It was not, however, attended by the usual tea-party of the present day ; but was loike I upon solely as a pious dedication of a house to Gid. The minister, whose name they do not recollect, is described as a " little stiff man," and when the ceremony was concluded he raised up his hands and led off a brave hurrah ! It is feasible to assume that the Rev. Dr. Beecham took a part in the opening service, but it is certain that the Rev. W. Dawson (better knewn as "Billy Dawson.the Yorkshire locallpreacherr), preached in the afternoon. His text was taken from the story of the Prodigal son. The musical portion of the Iservice at Brunswick was for a long time lei by qr. Robert Birkett, who played the double-bass fiddle; other members of his orchestra were qr William Hizleburst, and Nix. Charles liazlehurst flutes; and Mr. John Hazlehurst, I-1 Idle (violin) ; NI, James Bibby,and Mr Win. Holt (other instruments). There were also several others but their identity has become obscured. When Mr. Birkett gave up the double-bass, Mr. Thomas Ilazlehurst took it up</p>
        <p>and subsequently it was ably Manipulated by Mr. John Btrkett. In the year 1839-40 the Brunswick 1 Chapel was extended,and about 14 feet added to one side of it. The celebration of the enlargement and alteration brought down the Rev. D. Beaun3ont, of Liverpool, who conducted a memorial service on January 17th, 1841. About that time too Mr John Birkett had severed his connection with the chapel and acted as a singer and leader in the Trinity Church Choir, at which place there was an organ. He was, however, consulted by Mr. Thomas !Lee- hurst about an organ, and a little later,one was pro- vided for Brunswick by Mr. Thomas Hazlehurst. The instrument for its day was considered a very fine one. _ _</p>
        <p>In the year 1851. the church gnderwent one of its most trying .3rdeals, which was brong,ht about by what is now described as the "Reform Movement" ,Vhat was then called the New Connextion wag formed, and about 150 scholars and teschers left old Brunswick and founded Ellesmere-street Chapel.</p>
        <p>This blow was a most serious one, but from that the cause has now fully recovered. Services were held in Brunswick Chapel until 1866, when through the munificence of the late sir. Thomas Hazlehurst, S. Paul's Chapel, High-st., was built at a total cost of £B,OOO The pews were at this time removed from the body of the chapel in Brunswick-street, and the room was converted into a commodious day school for boys. 'rhe room was also used as a Sunday school in place of the lower buildings, which were in turn handed over for the purposes of recreation and amusement. The farewell sermon, when the move waa made to St. Paul's, and when the Brunswick Chapel became in reality" Brunswick Sunday School," was preached by the late Rev. Edwara Knibbq, of Prorlsham, who founded a stirring discourse, still rPmembered by many devout Wesleyans upon the words: "Arise let us go hence." To de=cribe St. Paul's Church to Runcornians would be a waste of time, suffice to say it is the most notable and commanding builiing in the towß, and well adapted to the divine purpoo.e it fulfils. It was opened in 1866, and the first services</p>
        <p>were conducted by the Rev.D.llannah and the Rev. W. Arthur. Then followed a three months' course of sermons and mission work, and amongst the preachers who took part we can trace the Rev Richard Roberts, ex-president of conference; and the Rev. lohn Ratten bury, both of London. Fs`ro the date of its opening to the present time St. Paul's Church has progressed and grown in strength and utility; little or no alteration hats taken place in the stradture, and indeed there is very little that could be done to improve things. There are now three resident ministers in Runcorn Circuit :—The Revs C W i Christein (superintendent), J A B Mal- vern, and Humphrey Morrison; as well as many local preachers; and in the near future, it is quite probable there will be need for a fourth resident minister.</p>
        <p>The Superintendents of the "Brunswick Sunday School "were:—Messrs. Burgess, Jonathan Potter, and William liolt (whilst in the Holloway); Mr. William Dutton 1829 to 1853; then came Mr, John Simpson the moit zealous of all superintendents, and whose name is even now a "household word." Ha became superintendent in 1848, and left the district in 1897. The late Mr. Charles H Kent and Mr. Charles Soalby were in turn superintendents, and the present holders of the offices are Messrs. Timothy Simister, and Edward Taylor. The following, now in the Wesleyan Ministry were formerly scholars or teachers in the school : Rev Henry Lewis, Davonport; Rev J Harrop Taylor, Market Rasen; Rev S Francis Colber, Manchester Mission; Rev S Hough Ravenscroft, South Africa; Rev J Walther Simister, Bromley, Kent; Rev C Ernest Norman, Oldham ; J D Brynford Jones, Porklington; also the Rev E Hope of the Methodist new connexion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Offshoots from Burnswick.</head>
        <p>Offshoots from Burnswick.</p>
        <p>It requires no imagination to realise that the Methodists of 1827 believed that Brunswick Chapel and school appeared a dignified, spacious, nay, even a magnificent building, sufficient for the needs of the congregation and scholars for many years to come. But the day of small things had passed. Those who accepted the faith and principles of John Wesley, were earnest, zealous, untiring, ever desirous that others should share the truth with them. They were missionaries in many instances probably unknown to themselves and the principles of Wesley found a ready acceptance. Brunswick that had appeared so palatial in comparison with the preceding preaching places becamt, too small within the short space of twelve years and was enlarged. Growth continued and expansion became a neceisit). In 1842 earnest workers commenced a Sunday school on Mill Brow, a neighbour;lood peopled by rough men and women. The school was field in a building on Windmill Hill and soon was the centre of a vivifying religious life, which re- ceived a further impetus in 1848 when Zion Chapel was erected in the same district. This sufficed for the needs of the place for 23 years. In this period vacant land had been covered with dwellings and the population had greatly increased, and Mr. Thomas Hazlehurst realising the need of further accommodation met the want with a generosity that in him seemed to have no bounde. At a cost. of £B,OOO he erected Halton Road Chapel and hauled it over to the control of his co-religionists. This building is almost an exaci; replica of St. Paul's Chapel. Thence, until now, Zion Chapel has been utilized as a Sunday school. Another off-shoot from Brunswick was a Sunday school established in 1858 in Gilbert-str set, Top Locks, and this likewise flourished. Four years later, in 1862, the school was removed to a new home provided by the munificent Mr. Thomas Hszlehurst. This was the Catuden school in Lowlands-road, which has proved one of the most vigorous and successful of the children of Old Brunswick. In 1870 the school was enlarged to meat the requirementq of the children in attendance who now out number those of the parent school. Camden in 1880 gave birth to a school in Nelson-st.. where numerous children are being benefited. But the Brunewickers were not content to confine their attention to the religious instruction of the young one day in seven. There was a determination that the secular training of the children should have the attention it merited, and in 1848, Granville. street Day Schools were built. Here united classes were taught by an efficient staff until 1866, when the boys were removed to Brunswick, which was established as a boys' school. In these two schools valuable work was accomplished until about five years ago when it was deemed wise to trend the scholars over to the School Board. How Wesleyanism permeates the children of the present day in Runcoon may be gleaned from the following figures ;—Bruns wick has 63 teachers, 492 scholars; Mill-Brow, 17 teachers, 217 scholars; Cam- den 35 teachers, 504 scholars; Nelson-street, 10 teachers, 100 scholars. Total 125 teachero,and 1,308 scholars. _ _</p>
        <p>The lamp of Nonconformi y therefore appears des- tined to shiae brightly ia Runcorn for many a generation to come.</p>
        <p>"HISTORIAN."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>TRINITY SLINDA.Y. SCHIOL ANNIPERSARY.—On Sunday the 107th anniwrstry WAS Ivid in Trinity Chapel, when ,r• General Campbell (late of her Majesty's forces in India) preachel in the morning and evening. In the afternoon an address was given to the children by the Aajor-General.- Special hymns were sung by the choir, teachers, and sch..lars. The soloist was Mrs J Edge. Ur. T. Sant conducted, and Mo. G. S. Frith acco:n- panied on the organ in the morning and evening, and Mr. S Sharp in toe afternoon. The collecticns reached the sum of £24 Ili. 61. Prior to the commencemeat of r.he evening service the whole congregation (which was a very full one) an the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A Lurrr..E GIRL. —Joseph Chritnes,lab mrer, of Frodsham Lordship was on Thursday mroiog ,u.tht up before 1,1”. J Ozkle4on, J.P., charged with Vnaultinz alen Wnitley. aged eight years and eight Months, and remanded to this morning (Friday). TEcE flit,Ls.—Tnis year the number of visitors to the hills is quite in keeping with the figures of previous ve Ira, an shows that their popularity has not in the least abated. Indeed. Frodsham presents, especially since the street improvements carried out by the County C nncil, a tempting invitation t 3 the holiday seeker. An improvement of an acoeptaPle nature would los the tlicing of seats under the shade of the Jubilee trees with which Main-street is lined, and which are now glorious in their foliage.</p>
        <p>_ SP:MOUS TRAP ACCIDENIT AT Acros BRIDGE.—Mr. Francis Frod-,harn, of Newton-by-Froisham, whilst out driving with his wife and child, on Frilay night last, met cvith a serious acdent. It appea:s that the float in wrlich the Frolsham's were driving met a lorry b.- longing to Messrs Fair.;lough's, near the steep drcnt by the Weaver (anal. The lorry horse took fright, and before Mr. Ft-Asher:a could get oit of the way the lorry was upon t.h.m. The shaft of the lorry stripped the harness off Mr Froisham's pony and entered the frost of the float the other shaft b,coming entangled in the wheel. Mre Frothharn and the child were thrown out about a distanco of 15 Feet, ani the pony freed of its harneas holed off. Subsequently it was stopped and broug,ht back The marvellous part of the accident is that the occupants of the fl lab eslaned almost uninjured.</p>
        <p>Touching the Spot at Bloemfontein; Dankershoof, Nt. Bloemfontein. 26th April, 1900.</p>
        <p>GENTLEMEN, I think it is only right that credit should be given where it is due, and I think Homocea deserves all credit. Before we left Aldersho: we were served out with a tin of Homocea each, and I don't think a man in the re,/iment hAs no'; f mad the heaefil of it. Vhra we came here first the heat affecd our lips ; they c,acked and were writ sore, we could not enjoy a smoke or our fond, but rub Homocea in over night, and you wouid be all right in the m)rning ; and the relief that it has given to men with Rh iumatism, brought on by s'eeping in the veldt, would surprise people. In fact our regiment swear by it. and use it for every ache and pain : ask any of our fellows whit they think of it,and they will tell you it is "Champion." Lots of my chums say you ought to be to'd about ails, so I have taken on myself to let you kno w. Faithfully yours, 2761 PTE. W. H. ASTIBEE, 14th King's Hussars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>NEWTON WOUNDING CASE.</head>
        <p>THE NEWTON WOUNDING CASE.</p>
        <p>Another Remand.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court. on Tuesday, before Mr. J. Tingley, Michael Mannion was charged on remand with wounding Patrick Delaney, at Newton, on tiae 4th inst.</p>
        <p>It was s•.ated that the man Delaney was not yet in a fit state to appear before the Court, and the doctor said he would net be able to attend for about a fortnight.</p>
        <p>Prisoner WAR remanded for ten dars.</p>
        <p>It appears that Mannion is a Haydock collier and was at Earlestown on Whit-Monday in the company of DrAlaney.ani others. The latter lives at Lime-street, Haydock, and in the course of a discussion high words arose, which cnlminated in blows being struck, until at length Mannion stabbed hie friend with a knife in the lowa part of the stomach inflicting serious injuries.</p>
        <p>TO THH DHAP.—A gentleman wbo cured himself of Deafness and Noises in the Head after fourteen sears suffering will gladly send full particulars of the remedy, on reoeiot of stamped addressed envelope.—lo. OLIFToN, Amberle House, Strand, London,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DIS- TRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening, the ordinary monthly meeting of the Urban Diestrict Council of Newton-in-Makerfield was held. Mr J W Collingwood, J.P., presided, and there were also present —Messrs CB F Borron' C.O , J W Emmett, J,P., N Siddeley, W Gilmour,F J Wood, J Simpson, W Thomp- son, E L Bent, J Fitzgerald, E I Evans,[' Ormand, with W W Shirley (clerk), A Bowes (surveyor), and D H Lloyd (gas engineer). TECHNICAL_ INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A letter dated 10:h May, was read from the Lanca- shire County Council with reference to the proposed dis- tribution of a special grant 'among the urban district councils in the county, who, during the last three years, have raisel for Technical Instruction Purposes an average rate of it least one-halfpenny in the £, and also desiring the committee to consider a scheme for the application of the amount (if entitled thereto), where- upon it was resolved that the letter be forwarded to the Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Committee, and ask if they will be good enough to report to tbis com- mittee upon the matter. The recommendation was agreed to on the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Gilmour. THE GS wORKS EXTENSIONS. SIOKERS AND THEIR HOURS OF WORK.</p>
        <p>At the Gas Committee meeting held on Monday, May 21at, the report of the Gasworks Extensions Committee, of the 30th April, was read. It contained the. f Alowing recommendations .—That the roof of the new purifying house be repaired forthwith ; that two extra carts be obtained from Mr. J. Sankey, Newton-le-Willows, at the prices of his previous tender, f ail.ne, which, other tenders be invited ; that it be a rebommendation to the Gas Committee to supply and fix, free of charge, one boiling burner with each automatic gas meter; that the Gas Committee be re- commended to allow the gasworks foreman the expense incurred by him in not being able to take immediate 005srtni012 of his house at the gasworks owing to Mr. Swann net being in a position to vacate the same imme- diately on the expiration of Ws term of office. A letter dated 3rd April, was read from the stokers at the gas- works, requesting that the three-shift system per day be inaugurated in lieu of the two-shift system at present in ororation. After consideration of the same it was re- solved that the stokers be informed that as an advance of wages had already been made, with which they ex- pressed themselves as being satisfied, the committee could not see their way tore-open the dupstion at the present time.—The committee adapted the, report.—Let- ters from Mr. .1. Liptrott, of Newton-le-Willows ' (addressed to the engineer), with reference to an alleged overcharge in his gas rent account, were submit- ted. The engineer reported that he had had the meter examined and tested, and that he found that the quarter ended 31st March, the meter had apparently registered wr cent. too fast, whereupon it was resolved that Mr Liptrott he charged according to the reading of the meter, subject to a deduction of 3i per cent.—The ten- der of Mr. J. Sankey. of Newton-le-Willows, for two carts for the sum of £25 each was accepted.—lt was also resolved that the tender of Messrs R Evans and Co., Ltd., of Hay- dock Collieries for the supply of an estimated qaantity of 4.000 tons of coal, as may be required at the gas• works during the year commencing Ist June.and ending 31st May. 1901, at the following rates, viz. :—Wigan 4-ft, or Rushy Park gas coal, 144 61 per ton; Arley coal, 161 per ton; slack (for boilers), 124 per ton, delivered in 1 their wagons at their Crow-lane sidings, be accepted ; that the tender of the Earl of Bradford, Bradford Evate Offiees. Bolton for the supply of an estimated quantity of 4,000 tons of cannel, as may be required at the gas works e the year commencing June Ist, 1900, and 1901, at the rate of 184 3d per ton nett rails, at Etrlestown Station, be edtidi ending gr de3dy i free Fee? oa ny ac- cepted; that the tender of the Uni'ed Alkali Co., Ltd., for the supply of brown vitro! r'r 140 (legs), filled in the Council's carboys, at the Company's Works, St. Helens, at 274 per ton nett, in such quantities as may be required by the Co incil during the year ending 31st May, 1901, be accepted; that the tender of Mr. J. Howarth. of St. Helens, for the supply of best Buxton Lime at the rate of 151 6d rer ton nett, free on rails,at Earlestowu Station, in such quantities as may be re- quired luring the year ending 31st May, 1901, be accepted. The minutes were approved on the motion of Mr. Thompson seconded by Mr. Simpson. WATER COMMITTEE. This committee resolved that the tender of Messrs W. Neill and Sons for steam pipes for the waterworks</p>
        <p>(including labour and fixint.;)' as per the "en-gine-e-rS specification, for the sum of £125, be accepted ; that the tender of the Glenfield Company, Limited. Bilmaroock, for three 9in., three Bin., two 6in., and one 41n.. double flanged sluice valves, for the total sum of £39 17s 63 be accepted. Mr. Berra, moved and Mr. Skldeley seconded the adoplon of VIE 3 minutes which were aoprovrd. HIGHWAYS AND SkNITARY COMMITTEE, MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>A', the meeting of the Highways and Sanitary Com- mitteo held en May 21st, the Medical Officer of Health attended and read his quarterly report. There were regieterel during the first quarter of the year 145 births an,l 65 deaths. The birth-rate wes 375 and the deaths rat- 16.8 of the estimated population. The infantile death-rate was 145 per cent. Twenty cases of infectious sickners were notified, end occurred in the following wards :—Town Hall, 1; Viaduct, 7: Crow-lane, 4 ; Wargrave. 4 ; Newton, 4. Ten cases were removed to the infectious diseases hospital. The Surveyor' monthly report was read, from which it appeared that, as instructed at the last meeting, he had visited the Golberne and Ashton-in-Makerfield sewage works, and found that they were doing all in their power to prevent eny pollution of Millineford Hen*, and consequently Newton Lake. A letter dated 17th May, from the Water Engineer, was read, stating that as the Water Committee of the Council were about to lay a 6til sewer in Southvrorth Road from the Water- works to the existing main, and suggested that if the Highways Committee thought that a 12in sewer would be of advantage, that such a cower could be laid at an additional cost of £l3s,if the Highways Cemmittee were prepared to incur the expenditure ; wherenpon it was resolved that the Water Committee be asked if they would be good enough te carry out the work of laying a 12in. pipe at their own cost. In conn,ctien with the offer of the Rev. J. Carson to give a piece of land to the Council for the widening of the footpath immediately adjoining the Rectory. Wargrave-rrAi, subject to the Council erecting a suitable fence, the Surveyor produced a plan showing the proposed widening to an average width of seven feet. and stated that the fence would cost about £25 or £3O ; whereupon it was renlved that the offer of the Rev. J. Carson be aceepted subject to the cost of the fence not exceeding the amount estimated by the Surveyor, and also subject to a proper deed of conveyance being executed. A lettel.. dated 18th May, was read from twelve rate-</p>
        <p>payers of the Town Hall Ward, complaining of a nuis- ance caused by the emoting of the pails on to Rome land between Upper Market-street and Newton Com•</p>
        <p>mon. It was resolved that the matter bs referred t, the Inspector of Naisances, for him to make a special report for the nest meeting of this Committeq. The minutes were annroved on the m)tion of the Chairman seconded by Mr. Thnmosln. GENERAL PURPOSES CONIMITTEE.</p>
        <p>TO CELEBRATE THE PROCLAMATION OF PEACE.</p>
        <p>A letter dated 10th_May, was read from thn National</p>
        <p>Telephone Company, in reply to the Clerk's letter asking if the subscribers to the telephone in this district could he allowed.to speak through to Warrington, Widnes, St. Helens, and Leigh districts free of charge, statine that the area had been definitely settled by the Postmaster Gsneral, and that the Company were unab'e in any way to alter the decision come to, and the Postrusster General was the proper authority to whom any application tr., enlarge the exiting area should be made.lt was resolved that the clerk forwsrd a cony of the National Telephone Comp tny's letter to Her Majesty's Postmaster General. At a meeting of the committee hem on Monday the following resolution was passed :—" That all the chair- men of committees he a.pnninted a special committee to carry out a suegestion of celetiratinz the proclamation of peace on the termination of the present war, if a day is set anart for that purpose by the Government." The Chairman moved and Mr. Simpson s,conded the adoption of the minutes which were ratified. This was all the business.</p>
        <p>Praise for the Guardians.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Board of Guardians are evidentw men of business who understand that if you want a good thing you must pay fer it. Recognising that fact, they have made excellent provision for their nurses. The statement quoted in the report of the London Workhouse Infirmary Nursing Association that" . . . Nurses have been expected to work under c:inditions and supervision that were con- sidered good enough for the old pauper helps," cer- tainly does not, apply at Warrington. The Superin- tendent's private apartments are excellent, but what pleased us still more the other day was to see that. the nurses' sitting-room is fitted—as all nurses sitting-rooms ought to be—with plenty of arm chairs. Nursing is hard work,and how hard is well known by Guardians who are members of the Medi- cal profession. The bright and artistic character of the furniture provided at Warrington is a whole- some change from the dingy fittings too often found. In making such excellent provision the Guardians will be found to have studied the finan- cial interests of the ratepayers. With good provision for the nurses both as regards aceommo- , dation and dietary, there will usually be found no difficulty in obtaining a supply of nurses.—" Poor Law Officers' Journal."</p>
      </div>
