      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>OUTLINE OF THE STORY.</head>
        <p>OUTLINE OF THE STORY.</p>
        <p>Ma JoSEPII HOCKING has chosen a great theme for his latest novel, and the widespread interest arousal by the dissensions in the Church of England makes It certain that his tale will be eagerly read and much discussed. Duncan Rutl4nd is the youthful minister of a flourishing Nonconformist Chapel in a Lancashire town. In the neighbourhood there is but one aristocratic family, known far and wide for is great tra ,itions, and its fealty to the Roman Catholie faith. Duncan imperceptloly falls in love .ith the daughter of the house. She is repelled by his Pro- testantism and My the feat that he is winning numerous converts to the Protestant faith. At first she seeks to discredit him in the town by inducing Father Sheen, bar confessor, to meet Duncan in public controversy, but Father Sheen is badly worsted and a dastardly attack upon Duncan by a gang of roughs whom she had unwittingly incited is foiled by the heroine herself.</p>
        <p>Father Sheen, demoralised by defeat, calls in the aid of the celebrated Jesuit Father Ritzoona. It is then and there determined to undermine Dow faith by means of his love. Every force that wellth and culture and noble traditions can command is brought to bear upon Duncan, who is enticed to Rome with his sweetheart, where iiiB final tempt-- tion takes place. This terrible inward struggle. gr3wing in intensity as time advances and as the tascinations of spiritual Rome unfold themselves, is admirably portrayed.and the breathlessly intec.isting climax will enchain the attention of every reader.</p>
        <p>JOSEPH HOCK ING'S</p>
        <p>l‘c E: IV STORY</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S "EXAMINER."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SLANDER AND FAKE IMPRISONMENT AT WARRINGTON' A Strange Story.</head>
        <p>SLANDER AND FAKE IMPRISONMENT AT WARRINGTON' A Strange Story.</p>
        <p>At the Lancashire Sheriffs Court s;tting at Preston, on Wednesday, the Acting Under-sheriff, Mr. E. T. Wilson, and a jury were asked to assess dimages in an action for slander and fake imprisonment in which the plaintiff was Michael O'Brien, nsiding at Dsllain-lane, an 4 de- fenchnt, Alfred Berry, moulder,43, Forshaw-steeet, War. ringt)n.</p>
        <p>Defendant did not appear and in explaining the caseto the jury, Mr. A. Browne said that Berry was just treat- ing the court as he had treated plaintiff throughout the whole action, viz., with centempt. The parties resided at Warrington, and it would be for them (the jury) to say what damages had been sustained by plaintiff by reason of def2ndaut's slanderous statement and his eon- s=quent detention at the Warrington Police Station. For the last 22 years plaintiff had been in the employ of only two firms, and although of an eccentric nature he bore the very highest credentials from everybody who knew him. Late on Saturday night, April 14, he was passing through Warrington Market when be was suddenly caught hold of by defend.. ant, and accused of having picked Mrs. Berry's pocket. The charge was denied, but defeadant repeated the accusation, and plaintiff was marched through the crowded streets of Warrington to the police station. There again the defendant told the Inspector in charge that O'Brien had picked his wife's pocket, but knawing plaintiff so well the police declined to book the charge and he was liberated, defendant being informed that if he desired to proceed he could do so by summons. This, however' had not been done, and plaintiff's friends had since taken the matter up, determined to have his character cleared of the stain which had been cast upon it. Defendant bad been given the opportunity of apologizing and acknowledging his error by handing over a small sum to a charitable object, but he had treated the proposal with =tempt. Extravagant damages were not invited, the one aim of the proceedings being, to vindicate O'Brien's character.</p>
        <p>Michael O'Brien, 99, Dillam-lane, who said he had been in the employ of Messrs. Peter Walker and Sans, brewers, for fifteen years bore out Counsel's statement. Whilst standing in the Market, Berry touched him on the shoulder and said" You have put your hand in my wife's pocket." Plaintiff retorted "If lam a pick- pocket you must call an officer of the law." He was then given 'into custody and detained at the Police Station a short time, the Inspector on duty refusing to book the charge.—Yfr Browne: Your employers got to know about this P—Yes.—And they insisted upon you taking these proceedings P—Yes. _</p>
        <p>The trodei Sheriff As I understand it you did not know the defendant Berry even by sight ?--No.—And when he accused you of picking his wife's pocket you simply said" If I am a pickpocket hand rue over to an officer of the law?—Yes.--Would it not have beea better to:have denied it. You rather invited him to send for a pol.cernan ?—Well the police knew me.—The Under Sheriff: I don't think it was a very wise remark ta make under the circumstances.</p>
        <p>--- P.C. G. Sankey said O'Brien was a well-known man but rather eccentric in his waya. Noticing a crowd in the Market Place on tho night of April 14 he inquired the reason and Berry said " I give this man (meaning O'Brien) into custody for attempting to pick my wife's pocket." He then askad Mrs Bary if this was the case and she said "I out my hand in my dr V Docket and I found O'Brien's hand in it. I took hold IV his hand." Asked if he would prefer a charge agaiust him Barry replied in the affirmative and O'Brien, followed by a large crowd, was tiken to the Police Station.</p>
        <p>Chief Inspector George Pniltips said there was nothing whatever known against O'Brien's character. Re was on duty at the Station when O'Brien WtiS brought in. Berry stated that O'Brien was found by his wife witifi his hand in her pocket but aft,.r eitisstioning him he elicited from defendant that O'Brien's hand was not ia the woman's pocket but at the side. now O'Brien so well he refused to take the charge and told defendant that if be desired to prosecute he must do so by sum- mpms. It was 9.20 when O'Brien was brought in and 9.40 when he left.</p>
        <p>The Under-Sheriff: Do you know defendant and his wife ?—Yes, they are respectable working class people— A Jorer : Were the parties known to one anoteer ? I think not.—A Juror: Then there can be no malice: —Mr. Browne : No we do not suggest rnalioe.—lnspec- tor Phillips : Jost at that time we 4 had numerous com- plaints of pocket-picking.-111r. Browne: And they have since caught the thiet ?—lnspntor Phillips : Yes— The Under-Sheriff: S.) it is pato possible someone's hand might have been in the pocket 2—lngpecter : Phillips : Oh yes, and it is also possible for anyone's hand to have been near the side of the vast. Addressino, the Jury the Unisr Sheriff said the only question tot them to decide was one of amount. It was a rather curious story and it seemed to him that if O'Brien instead of making what was rather an injudic- ious remark, practically inviting defendant to call a. constable, had denied the accusation this might not have happened. If defendant had a vasonable suspicion, , that O'Brien had been guilty of the alleged offence it was a matter which the jury might take into considera- i lion in mitigation of damages. The jury after a brief retirement assessed the damages at 40s.</p>
