      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRATHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRATHS.</p>
        <p>"ALWAYS speak well of your neighbour." " always do, although I can assure you in confidence she is the meanest woman in creation." MIKE (beating the carpet) : What's that spot there that's so worn ?—Mary : Oh, that must have been just in front of the rassis's mirror. SHE: But isn't it true that two people can live as cheaply as one? He: They can if they are married, not if they are engaged. GEoRG/E: Papa; may I ask a question ?—Papa: Certainly, my boy.—Georgie : Well ! Where does a snake's body end and his tail begin? WALKER : "Did you say your wile's a member of a secret society ?"—Talker : "it was isecret before she joined." IT has been proposed to inaugurate the gilt wedding, to be celebrated by people who have been married what seems like 50 years. Mu. PEPPER : "I don't believe there was a dry</p>
        <p>eye in the house when the curtain went down on the third act." Mrs. Pepper: "No; but there ba.t. Llidd to be the usual number of dry throats." PA? me the six-and-eightpence you owe me," said an Irish attorney to one of his clients. "For what?" "For the opinion you had of me." "F.iti, I never had any opinion of you in all my</p>
        <p>life." MR. WALLACE (impressively)—" Ah ! Here is another story of a man who killed himself because his home was unhappy." Mrs. Wallace (sweetly) —" And did that make his home happy, or does the paper say ?" A—" My wife says she saw the lights all burn- ing in your house as she came home from the ball at three o'clock in the morning. She thought it was a little strange."—B—" A little strange? It</p>
        <p>was a little stranger." ADVERTISER—" I wish this advertisement placed in some part of the paper where the people will be sure to see it." Editor—" Yes, sir—yes, sir, I can put it right alongside of an editorial, if you wish." Advertiser—" H'm ! Please put it along-</p>
        <p>side of the football news." FOR THE PUBLIC.—" Where did you get your information ?" asked the editor of the yellow journal. "From the butler," answered the reporter. "It would never do to quote the butler, would it?" mused the editor. "You'd better merely say that we have it on the highest</p>
        <p>authority." AN ALTERATIVE THEORY.—Minnie: "I don't think the count cares for money." Helen: "Don't you Do you think he is marrying for the benefit of his creditors?" OF one thing we never feel any doubt, and that is that whatever is the md.tter with us, our case is exceptional. NOT Necessary.—" I have just had a note from Willie's teacher," said Mrs. Parvenu, "and she says that he is very deficient in spelling." "What is he studying spelling for ?" demanded Mr. Par- venu, angrily. "Does his teacher mean to in- sinuate that he won't be rich enough to hire a type- writer ?"</p>
        <p>How IT HAPPENED. —Mrs. Potter: "What were poor Mr. Dunaway's last words?" Dr. Potter: "He didn't have any. His wife was with him." JOHNNY: "What is a socialist, pa?" Pater- familias: "A Socialist my son, is a man who, being poor, wishes to make everybody else as poor as he is himself.' FIRST Passenger—" Would you—ah--lend me your spectacles a moment, please 2" Second Pas- senger—" Certainly, sir." First Prssenger—" Ah, thank you. Now, as you cannot see to read your paper, would you mind letting me have it, to, please ?" "FAREWELL," he sobbed. The beautiful Mir- anda, the light of his life, was alas! another's. However, the same thing was true of the umbrella he managed to grab in the hall as he left her for- ever. STATISTICALLY MILD. —Ethel : Oh, dear me! I think football is a dreadfully rough game. It's a wonder you are not all killed." Captain : "It looks rough, madam, but there very little danger of casualties. Why, statistics prove that there are actually more deaths in the United States every year by cyclones than there are by football." "MY heart is in my mouth. I am afraid to near your answer." "You may well be, Mr. Dollboy," retorted Ethel. "I could never marry a man whose heart was not in the right place." A NEW KIND OF SPORT.—(Bquire, meeting neighbouring farmer in hunting field)—Squire: "How is it that you never hunt nowadays, Wuzzle ?" Farmer: "Well, we par farmers have started a new sport these 'ard times ! 'Stead o' hun tin' the fox, we try keepin' the wolf from the door !" MRS. GRADY : "It's an aisy Joffe yes are livin' now, Mrs. O'Style, in yer foine new house on de avenoo. 'Twas a lucky thing fer yes thot yer Mike wint to Klondike." Mrs. O'Style (haughtily) : " Yis, Mrs. Grady, it's a foine loife, but so tryiu' on me narves. Me Mary Ann is alwuz a-playin' on de payaner, an' me Katie is !Amin' to sing, an' me ole mon is for ever 5 ellin' for annuder bottle, an' Bridget makes sich a noise in de kitchen wid de silver, an'—oh, dear, Mrs. Grady, thank Hiven that yes are not wan of de aristocracy !" AN EVEN EXCHANGE. —Author Husband (trying to write) : "My dear, why don't you take up the study of German?" Wife : "Why, John, what do you mean?" Husband: "Well, my dear, I mean that you might then give the English lan- guage a much-needed rest." " BRIDGET, how did it happen that when we came in last night after the theatre there was a policeman in the kitchen?" "Sure, mum, Oi don't know; but Oi think the theatre didn't last as long as usual." Two men, a German and h Frenchman, had a heated argument over the question whether the wife of a State Governor had any official title or not. One contended that she should be addressed as "Mrs. Governor So-and-So." The other stoutly insisted that she was simply "Mrs. Blank, wife of Governor Blank." They finally agreed to leave the matter to the first man they met. He proved to be an Irishman. They stated the ease to him, ard asked for his opinion. " Nayther of yes is right," he said, after a moment of severe cogita- tion. "The wife av a governor is a governess." RBI: RAPTURE. —As she bent over, gazing upon it ardently, her golden hair tumbling about her fair face, and her great, childish blue eyes made a picture worthy of an artist's brush. With tender hands she laid it amongst its folds of snowy linen and lace, while she sang a happy song of love. Joy filled her soul as her eyes took in every curve and beauty of her precious treasure. Then, with a sigh, she covered it carefully as it lay in its soft bed. It uttered no sound as she placed it softly down, but her happy smile told the truth—it was her new hat. His Surssaiouisrs-.—" My father's a policeman," boasted a huh) boy. " Pooh ! that's nothing," replied another little fellow. "My father's a newspaper reporter." "Well, my aunt is going to be married se".[. week," said the first boy, return- ing to te attack. "One of my sisters was married the week before last, and my oldest sister has twins," was the triumphant reply. His victory was short, however. The other boy meditated for half a minute, and then said, conclusively "My uncle takes up the fare in church on Sundays." A ons:musaaN had left his corner seat in an already crowded railway car to go in search of something to eat, leaving a rug to reserve his seat. On returning he found that, in spite of the rug and the protests of his fellow passengers, the seat had been usurped by one in lady's garments. To his protestations her lofty reply was, "Do you know, sir, that I am one of the directors' wives ?" "Madam," he replied, "were you the director's only wife, I should still protest." STAINED DECANTERS.—To clean decanters and carafes that are stained inside, vinegar and rock salt will be found very useful. Mix a handful of salt with a gill of vinegar, put both in the decanter, and shake well until the stains have disappeared. Rinse well. RET-,ILED WHOLESALE. "Now remember," said a lady to her new man-servant,. a country yokel, who had but faint ideas of his duties— "remember, in announcing meals you are to say, 'Breakfast is ready," Luncheon is ready,' 'Dinner is served.'" "All right,- mum," replied the proud youth. Not long after this lady ven- tured to experiment on a dinner to a few intimate friends. Fancy the expression on her countenance when, on appearing at the drawing-room door to announce dinner, her new man exclaimed in loud tones, "Breakfast is ready, luncheon is ready, dinner is served ri</p>
        <p>LETTERPRESS PRINTING,</p>
        <p>PAPER RULING,</p>
        <p>AND BOOKBINDING,</p>
        <p>IN _aLL TfIEIR BRANCEIES.</p>
        <p>"EXAM INER " OFF ICO,</p>
