      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>RECEIVING MILITARY PROPERTY. At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday before Messrs. T. Birchall (in the chair), and R. T. Fairclough, James Callaghan was charged with receiving from a soldier at Orford Barracks certain military clothing and accontremeas, to wit a shirt, pair of socks, raz)r and housewife.—P.C. Cooper stated that at 11.20 the previous night he was on duty in Hill-street, and saw the prisoner in company with a young man, walking at a very feat pace. He heard prisoner say "I wiih I could get shut of this shirt as quick as I have 'got shut of them others." Noticing something bulky under his coat he stopped the prisoner acd found that he hada military shirt in his possession. Be asked him where he had got it from and he said that a man named 'Rome Johnson, of B Company, Liverpool Militia gave it to him to sell. Be brought him to the Police Station and found the other articles mentioned in his possession.—&amp; sergeant from the Barracks said that he had made inquiries and it appeared that the prisoner tried to enlist in the 3rd r.outh Lancashire Regiment on the 7ch inst but was re- jected • He had therefore no right in the Barracks.— Prisoner adhered to his statement that the man Johnson gave him all the things to sell.—The sergeant said that Johnson disappeared over the Barracks wall at 4 o'clock that morning with another man's kit. The articles in question had different regimental numbers upon them so that they could not all b long to Johnson. He had probably stolen them and given them to the prisoner to dispose of.—A fine of 403 with the alternative of a month's imprisonment was imposed. LN ARGUMENT AWAIT THE WAR.—ALLEGED BRUTAL</p>
        <p>ASSAULT. At the Borough Police Court on Monday, before Mr. Birchall and other magistrates,John Carter and Timothy Kivlahin, lodging at the "Model," Friars'-green, were charged with assaulting Arthur Cavenny at the Friars'. green dging-house on Sunday night.—The Chiet Con- stable stated there was a difference of opinion between prisoners and the prosecutor about the war, and it re- sulted in a struggle, in the course of which the prosecu- tor wag seriously iejured. It was alleged that Kivlahin knocked him down and knelt uponbimland Carter kicked him in the eye. He was taken to the Infirmary, where his injuries were attended to, and that moreing he (the Chief Constable) had received a note from Dr. Batile,the senior house surgeon, to the effect that the prosecutor was detained as an in-patient. Be would, therefore,ask for a remand, in order to see how the case developed.— Mrs. Davies, the keeper of the lodging-house said the prosecutor was her son. She corroborated the Chief Constable's statement.—Prisoners were remanded until Thursday. A MIDNIGIIT SCENE IN ST. JAMES-STREET— "YOUNG FOOTPADS." At the Warrington Police Court on Monday before Mr. Birchall and other magistrates, William Fearnley, 16, New-road, Alfred Wilcocks, 33, New-road, and Thos. Ashton, 18, New-road, youths, were summoned for committing a breach ef the peace by acting in a dis orderly manner in St. James' street on the sth inst.— They pleaded not guilty.—Douglas Hill, a stock taker residing in Failt9T-3treet stated that at midnight on toe 4th June he was proceeding home along Knutsford Road. He saw the detendants who said "God-night" to him. One of them then tried to trip him. He went on about 30 yards further when the three defendants sprang upon him from behind. He was badly knocked about and injered about the riles. In consequence of the injuries he had been unable to attend his work. When defendants left him he saw them thrash another person. He went for assistance and found P.C, Lewis. The officer told him to go and see if defendants were there still. He did so, and when he got back to St. James-street he found them" holding up" a cyclist in the middle of the road. P.C. Lewis then came on the scene, and defendants were molesting another man.— The Chief Constable • Feotpads.—Witness, continuing, said He t when the constable approached the defend- ants ran away.—George Donoghue, a cab driver, residing at 247, Knutsford-road said that at 12.30 he was proceeding home and when he got near Sparling-street he ea as stopped by the defendants. They commenced pushing him from one side of the footpath to the other. They continued this conduct until they got te York-st. but did not aesault him.—P.C. Lewis stated that he waa on duty in Bridge-street when the first witness came and complained of being assaulted in Ss. James-street. Shortly afterwards acs clist rode up and said that three young men had tried to knock him off his mactiine. Witness proceeded to York-street and saw the defendants molesting the witness Doncghue. They threatened him and when witness came up they ran away. Be followed Fearnley to his Ileum and he said" It was not me. I am not to blame at all." He said Ashton caused the "bother." The following evening he saw the other defendants and they said they had bad some drink and did not re- member much about it.—The Chief Constable said it was a very bad case indeed. If the streets were not safe at midnight on account of young ruffians like these what bad people to do P—The Chairman : They ought te have been in bed hours ago.—The Chief Constable ; I ask you to mark your sense of/their conduct by inflict- ing a substantial penalty.—The Chairman (after consult- ing with his colleagues) said it was one of the most out- rageous cases he bad beard at the Court for some time ; for mere lads like the defendants to act as highwaymen to peaceable citizens who were going home. He could I not find words to express the " abominableness "of it. Defendants would be bound over to keep the peace in two sureties of £lO each and would have to pay the costs (15s) or in default they would have to go to gaol for a month. BRUTAL ASSAULT ON A WOMAN.—SUOCKING CASE. At the Warrington Police Cuurt on Tuesday before Mr Birchall (presiding) and Alderman Faircluugh, Michael Fogherty was charged with committing an aggravated assault on Ellen Towey, a young woman residing in Erwood-street at 5.30 the previous evening.—The Chief Constable sad the case was a very serious one. It ap- peared that the prosecutor was the sister of the priqocer's wife and she was at the prisoner's house for the purpose of attending her sister who was ill. On Monday evenine they appeared to have bad a quantity of beer brought in and consumed on the premises and thia made the pri- soner quarrelsome and somewhat dangeroua. Afterreferr- ing to some old Cause of quarrel that existed between his sister-in-law and himself. he suddenly, without any warning picked up a jug and threw it straight in her f.ce. The jug brat- and split her nose in two. She Io4t a great quantity of blood. She put in r hands to her face and prisoner picked up a cup and threw it at her left ear, smashing the cup and splitting her ear. She was taken te the Infirmary and her injuries were attended to. The man was subsequently arrested by P.C. Lewis. He had had some drink but knew per- fec,ly well what he was doing. He threatened in the Presence of the unstable what he would do for the wrman if he was not arrested and also threatened his wife. Prisoner had been before the Court several times. He was liable/ to six months' imprisonment. Probably : owing to the hot weather assaults were becoming very common indeed.—Prisoner was committed to gaol for I three months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>To NIOTHEIIB.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing byrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their 2hildren while teething, with perfect success. It will relieve the poorsufferer immediately It Is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural (Inlet weep, ny relieving the child from pain sn -1 the itttt, Lherub awakes " as bright as a button."Ot all Chemists Is lid per bottle.—Advt. A CHANGE FOR TUE BETTER IS the change you want when the blootk gets out of condition, and Dr..J.scon OWNSE,DII SARSAPARILLA performs the needfulorith splendidly pleasing efecaey sad comfort. Tax IT. Vine e could's% say isers.—Advt.</p>
