      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRI DAV, JUNE 15, 1900</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRI DAV, JUNE 15, 1900</p>
        <p>THEFT OF SCRAP Lao.—BOYS RECEIVE THE MRCS. On Tuesday at the Warriugton Police Court, four small boy; named Thomas kloyles, James Green, Thomas Kirkham and John Birchall were charged with stealing from the yard of M,ssrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Dallam-lane between the Bth and 9th of June a qaantity of scrap iron valued at 2s 61.— They pleaded not guilty.—The Chief Constable said he did not think the hors isnew what they were talking about as they had already admitted the offene.. It appeared that Messrs. Walker and Sons had suffered considerably from pilfering in time past and complaints had b3eo made to the poli3e. Last Saturday P.C.'s Rylatt and Stewart were on duty in plain clothes when their attention was called to a bag of scrap iron in the yard of St. Ann's Church which was separated from Dallam Brewery by a wall. One of the constab'es went into the yard and the other watched the bag. Shortly afterwards the four defendants appeared on the wall and seeing the constables made off. [lot les and Green were caught and they said they had assisted the other boys to steal the iron. They were allowed ta go on giving their narnea and addresses and those of the other defendants. On Monday night thy were,biou.iht to th?. Police Station and each separately admitted toe offence. Eloy lea volunteered the foliowinz sta'emeat"l will tell the truth. We went on Friday all four of us and threw the iron over the wall of the brewery. At 4 on Saturdayove went again to take it away. Kirkham and I filled the bag. and ;the others kept" nix." It was too heavy to carry away. We remained about the wall and the police came." He also said that they sold some iron at a rag shop for 41 and each boy got a penny.—Evidence having hem given, defendants were ordered to receive six strolaes of the birch rod.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORI OF TriE HEXLTH OF WARRINGTON. Weel4 Endine: Satarll9, June 2 Id 1.9)D. During the week the following births and deaths were registered :</p>
        <p>Births. Daaths. Warrington Registration District 60 2) The excess of births over deaths 974 i 40. The Birth Rate was equal to 480 per 1,000. The death-rate from all causes was equ to 16 0 per 1,000, against 20.4 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 17.8 the corresponding period of last year. The death- rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 2.4 per 1,000, against 1.6 the average rate of the previous four weeks, and against 3.2 the corresponding period o" 1899. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 3 2 3 2 8 2</p>
        <p>Total. 20</p>
        <p>_ The deaths included-4 ft-nil membraneous croup, 2 from typhoid fever, 2 from phthisis. 5 from lung dis- ease, 3 from heart disease, 1 from injuries, 6 from all other causes. The folowing cases of Infe•Ai-m4 Disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever 3 cases, enteric fever 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital week ending June 2nd, 8. MSTEOROLOGY—The mean temperature was 53•7 Fah. and therefore 1.4 degrees higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 70F., was recorded on June knd, and the minimum temperature 42 F., on May 31st. The Rainfall measured *O4 inch. The general direc• tion was N.W.</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
