      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court. FRlDAY.—Befere Alderman Sutton in the chair) and Councillor Smethurst. WIFE ASSAULT.—Peter Cross, 45, Priestley-greet, was summoned for assaulting his wife Teresa Cross on the 4th inst —Complainant stated that she came home at six o'clock on the date in question and found that her husband was drinking in a public house. She brought him home and told him to lie down but he refused to de so and thrashed her. He made use of filthy language and continued his disgraceful conduct until 7.33 —Defendant who alleged that his wife hit him on the head with a poker was fined ós and costs. ARSAULT.—Mary liemblett, 04. John-street, was fined 5s and coats for assaulting Ellen Hankinson. 15, Winwick-street on the 4th inst. MONDlY.—Before Mr. Birchen (in the chair), Councillor Smethurst and Messrs. S Wright and J. W. Hayward LICENSING.—The following licenses were transferred Off license. 43, Folly-lane, from Martha Keenan (deceased) to George Percy Bradburn ; off-license, 14 and 16, Powys- street. from George Vernon to Wiliam Henry Mayers. A. VIOLENT WOMAN.—Elizabeth Tulles. 7, Williamson's Square was summoned for being drunk and disorderly in Williamson's Square on the 25th of May.—P.C. Clough stated that he saw the d fendant acting in a most disorderly, manner. She was bleeding profusely from a wound on the hand caused by smashing twelve panes of glass in the kitshen window. She was taken to the Infirmary and the injury was attended to.—Fined 5s and coats. UNPROVOKED Assaura.—William Lomax, 24. Silver-square was summoned for committing a bresch of the peace by assaulting a man unknown on the 25th May.—P.C. Clough stated that he was on duty near the " Lord Rodney " In plaits clothes and saw the defendant, without any provocation, strike a man in the face several times and knock him down.—De end:lnt was bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs. FIGHTING IN SILvER.+QUARE.—James Donlan, 3, Silver- square, James Leonardy, 4, Silver- quare and Thomas Farrington, 2, Bucaley-street, were summoned for commit- ting a breach of the peace by fighting in Silver-square on the 3rd inst.--Leonardy did not appear. -P.C. Lewis stat,cl that he saw the defendants in the centre of a large crowd of people fightine.--A warrant w 5 issued for the apprehension of Leanardy, and the other defendants were bound over to keep the peace. WOMEN FIGHTING IN DIAL STREET.—Bridget 0' drien. 3 Vernons-yard was summoned for fighting ia Dial-street on the 4th inst.—She pleaded '1 guilty in self defence."— Evidence was given to the effect that defendant was fighting with a Mrs. Barter who was brought before the magistrates the following morning and imprisoned for a month. There was a large crowd in the street and the scene was described by the Chief Constable sos being most disgraceful.—The Chairman Worthy of Dial-street.—The Chief Constable ; It was a running fight all day.—Defendant who had been before the Court 10 times and was said to be one of the worst women in Dial-street was bound over to keep the peace and o deresl to pay the costs. TUESDAY--Before Hr. Birchen (in the chair), and Alderman . .</p>
        <p>Fairclough. A DESERTER.—Patrick Leonardy was charged with being a deserter flan] the let Battalion Rest Lancashire Regiment, stationed at Fulwood Barracks, Preston, since Llama 21st.— P.C. Wright stated that at 11.33 the previous morning he re- I ceived the prisoner into custody Lona Sergeant Butler, at Orford Barracks. He admitted having deserted from the above regiment.—Prisoner was remanded to await an escort. FiGHTDra His WlFE.—William Quinn was charged with being drunk and diso,derly in Dallaua-lane at one o'clock that morning —P.C. Gibbs stated that prisoner who was arunk was figuting his wife.—Fined 5s and costs. BREACH OF THE PEACE.—James Leonardy, 4, Silver square, was charged with committing a breach of the peace by fighting in Silver-square on June 3,d.—He was bound over to keep the peace for six months and ordered to pay the costs. MAINTFNINCE.—Joseph Lowe, Scotland-road, was locked up on a warrant for arrears of maintenance amounting to £3 12s due to his wife.—He admitted the off once and said he had not been workh.g.—He was ordered to pay the money or go to gaol for a month. WENNI, SHAY. —BAore Mr. Birchall (in the chair), Alderman</p>
        <p>Sutton, Councillor Smethurst and Mr. Geddes. Nvemecr or FAMILY.—John Conway was charged with having on the 9th of June neglected to maintain his family whereby they became chargeable to the common fund of the Warrington Union —Mr. B Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the Guardiant,and said that prisoner's wife and two children were now in the Workhouse. There was a boy 14 years of age and a girl 5 weeks old. Prisoner had been working tor Mr. Mountield aud could earn from 30s to 35s per week.—The Chief Constable said the man had a terrible re3ord, having been before the Court 30 times He assaulted his wife, and on one occasion a separation order was granted,—Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour.—Timothy Callery, was summoned for neglecting to maintain his family, whereby himself and five children became chargeable to the funds of the Union.—Mr. Shearer said the man was admitted to the Workhouse suffering from delirium tremens. lie had now recovered and had an opportunity of leaving the house and taking his children out, but he refused to leave. They had ro option but to bring the case before the Court.— Prisoner was committed to gaol for a month with hard labour. THEFT BY A LODGER.—Jam's Stubley, a clerk, was charged with stealing from the dwelling house of James Tnomas, 48, Winwiek-road, on Tuesday a pair of men's boots value 3s the property of James Donoghue. —The Chief 4.,on- stable stated that the prisoner and Donoghue lodged at the house of Thomas.At noon on Tuesday Donoghue left a pair of boots under the sofa at his lodgings. Prisoner came in shortly afterwards slightly under the influence of drink wad lay down on the sofa. The landlady told him that as he had been discharged from his employment she could not ear to keep him and he must go. Prisoner did not, go but went to sleep. At 2 °clock she awakened him and insisted on his going away. Prisoner seemed to acquiesce in this seat before I going was seen handling the prosecutor's boots and changing the laces from his own boots to those of the prosecutor Mrs Thomas did not pay any attention to this and the man left unobserved. When Donoghue came In he missed the boots and went in search of prisoner. He found him drinking in the " Three Pigeons" public-house and saw that he was wearing his (prosecutor's) boots. He said to him ** Those are in, boots. You must come back and take them off." Prisoner said "Yes, hut don't be in a hurry ; wait till I have drunk my beer." Donoghue went out and stood at one entrance to the publichouse. When he went in again the prisoner had bolted throwgh a side entrance into Dal am-lane. He gave information to Superintendent Monaghan and he, in comppny with Detective Meacock apprehended the man in a publichouse. He said be had taken the boots in mistake.— Prisoner was committed to gaol for 14 days. THURSDAY —Before Mr. John Faireough (in the chair), and Alderman Fairclough. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>TREFT FROM A TILL.—A boy named John Edward Crosby was charged with stealia4 from a till at the Lion Hotel Vaults at 715 that meriting, 4s 7,1 the money of Peter Walker end Sons.—He pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable said that James Catterall, a bar- man, at the Lion Hotel Vaults, was attending to his duties when he noticed the prisoner, who had come for some beer to take to a lodging house. lying on hie stomach across the counter with his hand in the till. The barman got him by the neck and pnilett him over the counter. The boy said he had not taken anything. Caterall looked at the till and found it covered with black finger marks. He gave the boy Into custody and on being searched 4s 3il was found in his pocket which he admitted having stolen from the till. Prisoner was the soa of people who tramped about the country and his waif ire had been neglected. He was sentenced to 1% strokes from the birch. Runcorn Petty Sessions 3</p>
        <p>MoriDAY.—Before Messrs. W. Timmins presiding, and W</p>
        <p>Handley,</p>
        <p>THE DANGERS OF WHITE LEAD PACKING —The Matthews Lancashire Cheshire and North Wales White Lead Company Limited were summoned for a breach of the Factories and Workshops Act 1878-1895. by not complying with the special rules for White Lead Factories on the 29th of March 1903, and also for failing to give notice that Arthur Haddock an employee of theh's was suffering from lead poisoning on the 30th of December 1899.—Mr. R B Graves, Inspector of Fac- tories. Liverooo', prosecuted. and Mr. 3 Burton. solicitor, Runcorn, defended.—At the outset. Mr. Burton said he was instructed to plead a technical offence.—Mr. Graves stated the works had been in operation for about 2i yews. The work was conducted to some extent similar to the old Dutch process, but was none the less a White Lead Works. In consequence of the great number of cases of lead poisoning which arose trout the packing of white lead special rules were drawn up requiring the picking to be done by mechanical Mane, and this was more or less done in all su7ll works in the country. Due notice had been given to the defendants and the requirements of the Fac- tories Act bad been fully explained to the m inager of the works, but on the 29th of March, Mr. Brant, a colleague of his then in cnarge of the district, found that the white lead packing was still being carried on by the old process, and tnat there was no mechanical contrivance for taking the dust away. The Inspector had discovered numerous eases of lead poisoning at the works, hut only one case had b.en brought before the court. This could not be called a technical offence when it related to a sped-1 set of rules dealing with a very dangerous trade. A man might be employed in the works for several years, and apparently never suffer, others might be there a few hours, end get lead into their system and die. One of the worst symptoms was paralysis. leaving a man a wrcck for life, and it was imperative that the rules requiring the reporting of eases of lead poisoning should be strictly enforced.—Mr. Brant gave evidence of visiting the works on several occasions. itlid said there was a machine there which might h ve cost £l2O, but it was net applicable to the purpose of packing, and he bad pointed out to the manager where It was positively dangerous.—Mr. Burton contended that the defendants were desirous of doing everything possible to meet the requirements of the law, and had endeavoured to do so.—The Bench said they were of opinion that sufficient notice of the rules had been given the defendants, and they had not carried them out. But as it</p>
      </div>
