      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Presentation to a Post-Office Telegraphist. An interesting function took place at the Douglas Post Office on Friday evening, the occasion being the presentation of a morocco leather writing-case and autograph album to eliss Gertrude Canning, on the eve of her departure for Warrington Post Office. —Mr.Corkill,43issist ant superintendent, made the presentation on behalf of the ladies and gentle- men of the Doeglas staff, and said that during the three years Miss Canning had been in Douglas Post Office she had always cheerfully and willingly per- formed her duties, and in a very capable and satis- factory manner. Considering she had entered as a learner only three years ago, it would be difficult to find her i-qual as a telegraphist for the same period. The entire staff were sorry to lose her able assist- ance and cheerful presence, and wished her every success, and congratulated her on her transfer and appointment to tbe Warrington Post' Office.—Miss Canning expressed her thanks for the useful and valuable presents.—ell present then wrote their names and wishes in the autograph album. An Ironmonger's Shocking Suicide. About 8 o'clock on Saturday morning Edward Jones, an ironmonger, residing at 40, Cairo-street, committed suicide by cutting his throat with a table knife. It appears that deceased had been em- ployed at the shop of Messrs. Dutton, ironmonger,, Market-street, for the last twenty years, and left about three months ago. Since then be had been out of employment, and very much depressed in his mind. Us had however, never threatened to take his life. At about six o'clock on Saturday morning he got up to light the lire. At 730 he called his wife to get up. She did so shortly afterwards and when she went downstairs she heard a gurgling noise in the yard. She went into the closet, and found the deceased on the seat with his threat cut and with a table knife in hi* hand. Dr. Smith was sent for but deceastA died shortly after his arrival. At the inquest on Tuesday a verdict of " Suicide whilst of unsound mind r was returned. ASK. or CUNNINGHAM HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS—Advt. BEST BOOTS FO ALL, UNDER THE CLOCK.— . vt NEW RAPID CYCLES with Sin cranks and free wheels, J. S. GARLAND, Cycle Factors, Bridge-st., Warrington, LATEST DESIGNS and colourings in Prints. Mnslins anti Zephers, for summer, also a choice assortment of Sunihades just received.—W. T. WILLIAMS, 74, Sankey- street and 1, Legh-street, Warrington. EXCELSIOR CYCLES at £lO 19s. Sole Agent, S. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington, LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER THE •.3LOCK, BRIDGE-STREBT.—Advt. PER NiflElt CYCLES price £9 17s 63.—J. S. GARLAND, Cycle Factor, Bridge-street, Warrington. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt</p>
