      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>The Duke of Norfolk.</p>
        <p>A War Office tnetgage this evening says the'Duke of Norfolk is progressing favourably.</p>
        <p>_ Laing's Nek Tunnel.</p>
        <p>Buller reports about 150 wards of each end of tunnel has been :blown in. Line otherwise unin- jure.d. BOTHA EVACUATES HIS</p>
        <p>POSITION.</p>
        <p>Wakkerstroom SLI rre n ders.</p>
        <p>Roberts and Buller Co-operating. Lord Roberts telegraphs to the War Office yester- day from headquarters,Pretocia, June 13, 1 p.m. : June 13, 11 a.no. Enemy evacuated their strong positions during</p>
        <p>the night, and have retired eastwards. Butler's force and mine afforded each other mutual assistance. Our occupation of Pretoria caused numbers of the floe' rito Withdraw from Laing's Nek, and Bailer's advance to Volksrust made them feel that their rear would shortly be threatened.. Sir Redvers Buller telegraphs : Headquarters, Nek, June 14, 7.50 a.m. =General Lyttleton received the formal submission of the town and district of Wakkerstroom, which the enemy are believed to have completely evacu- ated. Boer Position of Prodigious Strength. Almond's Nek a Serious Disaster for the Boers.</p>
        <p>The "Central News war correspondent, tele- graphing from Laing's Nek yesterday, said The preparations that the Boers had made to re- ceive our expected advance via Laing's Nek and Majuba were most et.borate. A through system of entrenchments had been made from Majuba across the Nek and on to Por,..,wane Hill, and the position was one of prodigious strength. Baer prisoners regard Almond's Nek as one of the worst disasters to their arms that has yet occurred. Buller Congratulated. Colonel Himc, the Premier of Natal, has sent the congratulations of the colony to General Sir Redvers Buller upon his splendid achievement of having driven the Boers beyond the borders of Natal. The message concludes with a tribute to the unrivalled heroism of the troops under General Bailer's com- mand.</p>
        <p>Mr. Schreiner Resigns. (Central News Telegram.) CAPETOWN, WEDNESDAY 2.20 P.M. Mr. Schreiner, the Premier, and all his colleagues in the Ministry have tendered their resignation. Sir Alfred Milner has accepted their resignationss. , A Renter's telegram says that Sir A. Milner is urging Mr. Schreiner to remain in office until Parlie-C ment meets.</p>
        <p>Warrington Volunteers at Durban. 1111 The transport Assays which contains a dtaft of 20 Warrington Volunteers arrived at Durban from Cape Town on Thursdsy.</p>
        <p>Death of Another Warrington Man</p>
        <p>in South Africa.</p>
        <p>News has been received in Warrington from the War Office that Private (155) John Gilligan, 4th Battalion Cheshire Reglinsnt,*died of enteric fever, oa June 2nd, at Springfontein. Deceased, who was a brother of Mr T. Gilligan, fish dealer, of Buttormarket-street, resided at Mill-yard, off Fenne?-strees, was married, and leaves a widow and one child, li2sides three brw,hers and four sisters to mourn his loss. The sth Irish Company at - Warrington. • On Monday evening till second Special Service Com- pany of the 5b (Irish) Volunteer Battalion the King's (Liverpool Re gi ment) _left Liverpool fotThe district mili• tary headquarters at Wa.rrington, preparatory to sailing for the Care. The Special Service Compxrivis officered by Captain Flett, of the Ist Cheshire VB., and Lieu- tenants Lindsay and Wright. There are 4 with the officErs and rank and file, 116 in the corepany:.,The non-corn missioned officers are Quartermaster-sergeant Donnelly and Sergeants Burke,Elealsy and Stanlitreet. Lieutenari Colonel Carruthers accopapihil gection to War_ ington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MILITARY NOTES.</head>
        <p>MILITARY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Second Lieutenant Buckley Morg,an,a Welsh Volunteer officer has been given a commission in the South Linea. shire Regiment and will join detaila of the Ist battalion now at Bradford. Mr. Buckley Morgan IS transf,rni to the South Lancashire Regiment from the 3rd Volunteer Battalion Welsh Regiment with headquarters at Cardiff nd he haspeld a commission in that coma about. three a years becomiev second lieutenant in May 1897. . Lieutenant Jeaves, ,2nd Volunteer Battalion South Lancashire Regiment with headquarters at St: Reims; has left the service. He has-heen about. four yeara in officer of the corps being appointed seco-id lieutenant In May. 1896, and was promoted lieutenant in Neverober 1897. IlMaiar-General Geoffrey Barton. C.B who was lately chief staff officer in ,thia diStrict, and ig now in c,m. rnand of the 6th brigaie, Io,h infantry division in South Africa. has been appointed to the Co;oneter ef the Rol Fusiliers. He succeeds the late Sit; R Wilbral-ram was also honorary Caon'el of the 3'.,h Volunteer Battalion Cheshire Regiment. Major Lhavd late of the Royal Artil'err, and who was at can time staff caw.ain for FLA.,- in this district, hag joined the Volunteers and hat; been appointed Lieutenant Colonel commandant of the Ist Cheshire Volunteer Artillery whieh has just been iroreased from nine to 12 Companies. The Command of the 2nd Lancashire Enilineei Volun- teer Corps at St. Helens has become vacant owing to the retirement of Colonel Pendlebury, who habeen fivet-I years at the head of the regiment; Colonal Fendlebury became Liautenent ie4he St. t.,!ejen., (lams in Janne re 1880; was promoted Captain in 1888';. M.jor..th i 894. and Lieutenant Colonel-in Jun- 1895'when be au&amp;7eAcied Colonel Bothwell in command nf the coml. MjorJ D Murray will now i 1.5 e"Pet.Pd *," aPPniuted to com- mand the reeimr"t. Ile has 'beep Oolv"frili 'yeah' an officer of the St. Helens Engineers joiningahe corps in• Marel4B9o,becanne Captain is 1891. and Maj,:r in Octo:aer„! 1895. ' Two new offieers have been appointed to fljr)siunr Lancashire Reeiment. They are second Vent-4130t Ley and Second Lieutenant Slaughter tra,v4,rred from the lit V‘Aunteer Battalion Ea‘t Kent Regiment with head- s:mar. ers at Canterburr. Second Lieutenant Ley jlina - detail of ihA Ist Battalion South Lanoaehite Regiment at Bradford but Second Lieutanant S'aughter joins the Ist Battalion in S u'h Africa b.ing now on active service as an officer of the VAunteer Company attached to the 2nd Battalion East Kent Regiment. Several of the Volunteer office7s serving with Volun- teer Companies is South Africa, have also been given eommissi-na as Sec..nd Lieutenants in the regular service as a reward for their service? in the °resent campaign, including Captain rivrke-Smith, 4th Voluntier Battalion Liverpool Regiment, and Lieutenant Kenyon, 2nd Volun- teer Battalion ditto. Both now become Second Lieuten- ants in the Ist Battalion Liverpool Regiment. Lieutenant-Colonel Ewan has been appointed second in command of the Ist Lancashire Volunteer artillery, in succession to lhisjor Powell, who has retired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LOUAL VOLUNTEERS UNDER CANVAS. The Second Week at Conway.</head>
        <p>LOUAL VOLUNTEERS UNDER CANVAS. The Second Week at Conway.</p>
        <p>nspection by Major-General Swaine.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Volulteers in brigade camp at, Conway have scarcely enjoyed the same favourable climatic conditions as were experienced during the first week, but still on the whole the weather has not been so very unfavourable, and the men have enjoyed themselves immensely, despitelthe hard work they have had. From conversation with both officers aud men, there appears to be little doubt that camp life under the present system will find very much moreafavour with thettilkee4the regi- ments than those of the old camping days; The officers and men of the Ist and 2nd V.B. South Lancashire. Regiment and Ist, 2nd, and OM Lanca- shire Fusiliers, had ei• Pleasant experieup on Satur- day evening, when they attendea B.g.rand military and patriotic concert given b fOns.Tulls Riviere at the Victoria Payilsn, Calwyn Bay, in ceimmemo- ration of the tfaktng cl Pretoria. The colcert was under the patronage of Calorie! Haworth! and Mr Lees Knowles, M.P., honcOAry colonel;' and other officers of the 3rd V.B. Lancashire Fasillers, who were' Pre'dent, together with officers of the other battalions named. The pavilion was crowded, special:trains having been ruh-frdin all coast towns. The first portion of the programme was introduced with the singing of "God save the Qaeen," and "Rule Britannia "by the whole audience. The chief item was a grand military quadrille, " Tee British Army," descriptive of the gallawing of General French's cavalry, the taking of Johannei- burg, and the triumphal entry of Lord ttofrerte into Pretoria. The piece concluded with 4,See'i the conquering hero comes," and whilst this was being Played by the orchestra salvos of artillery and rifles were fired outside the building, the effect being I dramatic and stirring iii the extreme. I On Sunday the battalions paraded for Divine Service as follows ;—The Ist, 2nd and 3rd.Lauca- I shire Fusiliers were formed up at 11, about midway I between the let and 3rd Lancashire Yasiliers canape the bani of the 3rd Lancashire Fusiliers provid- ing the inusic,and the service was conducted by the charlain of the 3rd Lancashire Fusiliers, the Rev E G Reed, M.A. The Ist and 2nd V.B. South Lanca- shire neeiments were formed hp on the same ground immediately after the first service was dia inisssd (about 11 45 a.m.) Vie band of the let V. B. euth Lancaehire Regiment (Warrington) provided ' the music, and the service, which was much 'en- joyed, was conducted by the Rector of Warrington. The men were released after dinner for the day. On Monday there was a roll call only at 6.30 a.m., in view of the operations later on. The Warrington bat- talion furnished all brigade duties. rhe 3rd V.B. Lanca• shire. Fusiliers were detailed for musketry. Tee Ist V.B. and 2nd V.B. South Lancashire Regiment were detailed for outpost duty, and the lit' and 2nd V.BeeLancaihire Fusiliers for attack. The Ist and 2nd Soath Lancashire. under the senior officer paraded at 10 a.m. and marched by the coast road to St. Gwenan'a Church, where written instructions were handed to the cemmandiog officer. The dress was drill order with haversiclre and filled water bottles. Fifteen rounds of blank ammunition were carried, ten in pouch and five in reserve, and the troops took their dinner with then. The Ist and 2nd Lancashire Fusiliers practised the attack brinstien on the Mode, under their respective commanding officers on the early and forenoon parad.s. At 2 p.m. these two Battaliens were ready to march off, under the senior cmnrnanding officer, to take part -in the I defence *f Conway, which was threatened by lan advancing fdrc a. All necessary precauinas were leaken. It will thus be seen that the four Batteliens ment nod above were divided into an attacking and , defending force, the two Sonto Lane tehire Battalions I advan ong from the direction of PenmaenMawr,and the two Fcailier Battalions defeading Conway. The foie 33 we e I set in motion about 3 p.m., and some very i.e ivy oper ations in the hills took place. The South Lancashire Battalions took up a very strong position in the hills,and kle twill under cover. The two Fusilier battalions compels-rag th." defending forces advaacea from Cohn/m.7, and scaling the mountain endeavoured to drive the South Lanceshires back, an operation which couapletely failed. The Fusiliers were compelled to crass many open places, and in advancing to attack the defenders of Conway wore met by a heavy rifle fire, which in actual warfare would have resulted in heavy loss. Ultimately they withdrew, and the South Laticashires following up turned their repulse into a ront and cap- tured Conway. The battalions returned to camp about 6 p.m. loekireg well and fit.. notwithstanding the fAc'; that tiny had been under arms over nine hours. The Warrington men were supplied with a substantial hot dinner on their return, ana many of them were beard to say they could have done another ten-mile march. Tuesday was one of the busiest days exp-rienced in camp,and the camping grouad presented, to en obvrver stationed on the kopjes overlooking Conway Marsh, ow of the most interesting sights. The acari-t unifo•ms of the three Fusilier Regiments and the Warrington' tea were detted over the greater portion or the Mork, while the dark and more sober looking uniforms of the Sr. Helens mast added an accept,b'e variety to the scene. Ssveral companies of the Sslfores were raive I its two long, thin lines, undergoing their special inesketry course at 500 and 600 yards' range; other companies of the regiment were scattered about in different directions, all undergoing their firing practice:the ether two Fusilier Regiments were massing preparatory to another day's work at outpost duty on the hills; and the smart looking Warringtonians and darker-hued St. Helens were engaged in practising the attack formation across the Marfa. As a result of his inspection of the camp on Monday, it was announced that Major-General Swaine corn- mending the IsTirth Western District had expressed himself well satisfied with the mart appear- ance of the camp. Ira the afternoen there was another sham fight on tbe bias. The s Arne four regiments as were engaged in tire battle on Monday were told off to carry out similar movements. The chief difference was flieSniithrreinei-sfatre Reeiin ante &amp;at- the Whole Of thli.ffilir Working round to their atteek. an Nofewaynthrospia. the -Sycharlarite'nss and over ihe• hills,harid the .Bury anti Rochdale. C)rps marched 'froth camp: about two o'clock ,to defend'Conway, on Tuesday efOrning_the orddar adeparture was rev?re*d. This was done to enabhikhe eiefehders te make a wider detour and s,CITO poeitions before the attacking move- ment could be carried out, rile whole of the operations were carried out under the supervision of Brigahei- general Leith linsh- and were ex iellently conceived and executed. The South Lancashire Regiments were splen- didly handled,throuehont the eperatinns, the umpire's decision heing that the South Laucashires,as on the pro- winos day, had won the battle, the defendere' position being carried and captured. The men fired ten retinas of blank ammunition.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night a very heavy thanderetorm pas.ed over the camp, bat TOrtunately dld no damage. Th., ligirming was very vivid, and the pees of thunder vory loud. , Many of the men who had left the vicinity of the canna were drenched, and the. tents hal a glod soaking. but the discomforts experienced were of a very light character.</p>
        <p>- on WednPslay morning the 1.5% and 211 battalions of the Lancathire Fusiliers anl,the lst and 2nd battalions of thg, &amp;filth Lincash'r. were enga.Y.ed in battalion drill under their own officers. but in the alterq,:ln thew practised the attack formation on tilt) Moen. Whilst thii was gninz on the 3,1 V.8.L,F., (Sxifozdf. complPted neir special musketry course. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The bTiglde orders issued on Tuesday night, Wed- nesday stated that" the Gnaeral Offiner commln4 the IN.u.th-Western diatrict, desires it to he known that he -was greatly pled witn what he saw at (n way yesterday. The men lo Ated welt and c!ean, and appared to take an interest in their work. The camps were well pitee.atd remarkably clean and tidy. fle conzrato- lates Colonel Leith-Ross and rhe commandinz officers of the battalion on the success of their endea.vonrs to make ,the fortnight'a camn of really useful instructicu."</p>
        <p>Mr. W. J. Jeeves' Salary.—Large Increase The application for an increase of salary made by Mr W J Jeeves, town clerk of Leeds, who odor to last August was town clerk of St. Helens, came on Friday last before a meeting of the Finance Com- mittee, over which the Lord Nlayor (A.ldertnan Gordon) presided. After some discussion, in the ..dOurse 4which it became fully evident that there was but cine opinion as to the ability of the town Atak, Councillor Wilson moved -a resolutton jo favour of an increase of £5OO a year, yvhich wouil make the salary of the office £1,750. Councillor Wil- son's proposition was carried by a considarable majority. Tbe Lib..ral members ot the committee, ,with One exeeptionolbstained from voting, t;--.e opinion being exprased that, having regard to the comparatively short period that had elapsed since Mr Jeeves' appointment, the application was some- what premature. The recommendalion im favour of the increase will not be submitted to the Council until the meeting in July. MINING is LANCASHIRE.—The annull report of Mr. Gerrard, the Insp.ctor of Minfs for North and East Lan. cashire, was published on Wednesday. The total number of hands empioy,d in the mcaghire district durim.-; last year as 38.797, of whom 550 were females, 44 of them being under 16 years of age. The tuna quantity of coal raised during the twelve months -e7a,, 11,033,823 toes, representing a total value of -24,169,797, the value per ton at the pits' mouth berng 74. 61. and one-3eyenth ef of a penny. rbe quan.Ty shows an increase of 136,770 , tons over the previous year, vrhile the value is higher by ' one shiPinz and twopenea per ton. There were 49 fatal accidents causing 52 dea'hs, these figures showing a de. crease of 12 accidents and nine deaths as compared with the preceding year. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>_EXAMINER FRIDAY, J UNE 15. 1904)</head>
        <p>THE _EXAMINER FRIDAY, J UNE 15. 1904)</p>
        <p>family and the servants, but the promoters could widen the real and do it teithetically. The next objection he could not pretend to cope with, that he tad a nervous wife. (Laughter.) If that was a valid objection no light railway scheme would be carried through. The third objection was excelleat from the peint of view of the promoters and that wee that the road was narrow in places, but that was the very thing they were , going to cure. . Their proposal was to widen , the road, and then Mr. Dawn said the road was well traversed with vehicles and feot • passengers. That, also, was a matte: they wanted to meet. They were hoping to carry the foot-passengers at a moderate amount, and it would relieve the traffic. Another obj...ction was that it would he dangerous and depreciate nis residence, but ne (counsel) underctood that the residence belonged to someone one. Me. D awn also appeared in the capacity of director and secretary for Greeoall. Whitley and Company,and pointed out that the railway would be a source of great danger to the herses, drays and drivers who had to pass the meiseroad in-A) Cheshire for the distribution of the beer. No deuht the distribution of beer was a matter of great national importance—(laughter)---but the promoters would be ahle to assist the company in the distribution of that exeellent beverage all through the district. There was no reason why on earth, if the scheme was carried nut, the barrels should not b 3 placed on their trucks in War- rineton and tsken along the district. It would relieve the firm of the necessity of having se many drays on the road. No one liked brewers' drays, and it would be a groat boon to the country if they were absolutely swept off the roads, because they took up so much room. They might be certain of this that the thirsts of the people of Northsvich and other people would not remain unslaked. (Laughter.) Then Mr Down contended that the railway would not appreciably benefit the district, but he (the speaker) did notfsee why the public should not find it of great service, in fact, the promoters, might be th means of increasing the dividends of Greenall, Whitley and Co. (Renewed laughter). A ;lumber of private individuals also objected tut moat, of the objeetiens :referred to little strips of roedway which wculd be sliced out in order to make the necessary width of road and there might be an ancestral tree to be l'emoved, but these objections were of a very trivial nature. Arrangements were in progress with the County Council ae to where the wideninge were tr. cessary. His LORDSHIP: Do your clients prenose to purchase land by _agreement or by compulsory cowers? . _ .</p>
        <p>D. WILLIAMS: MS7 -reacliag of the two orders was that they amlunted to compulsory powers. His LORDSHIP : The first order refers to purchase by agreemen whilst the second order dealing with the second scheme seems to vary it and makes purchase of necessary land compulsory. Dr. WILLIAMS : It is quitl elear that so far as that point is concerned the conliti.ins of the first order are not to be affected by anything continued in the second one.</p>
        <p>ill 9 Lordihip : How will you m your if roll have only powers to _purchase by agreemeW ?</p>
        <p>Dr. WILLIAMS : The 01117 remedy ch ttiat CI Se would be to apply for an amended order if a difficulty arose. Mr. RHODES on behalf of the Cheshire Lines Railway, raised the question of the advisability of proceeding with the extension or number 2 scheme at that inquiry. It was clear that Dr. Wieiams himself knew very little about it,and it was evident that it had only been loosely considered by the eneineers. As hal been pointed out, jothis secon l scheme the Compa.ny asivd for compulsory power to purchase land Dist affected the Cheshire Line; Rtilway for the promoters proeosel to go on the railway company's land at two place. In the order the Promoters said they would have a junction at or near the Green bank etatinn and also at or near the station used by the Cheshire Linen Committee. The Railway Company had received no notice in regard to it, and he submitted that scheme number 2 ought rosily to be ruled out of Court. The notices had not been complied with and the scheme had been badly considered. It was stated that the cost ef the second scheme was to be £58,000. That was r,lmost as great an expenditure as the original scheme, and to spring it ueon everyborly at the last moment,end to put in a clause seeking compulsory powers whereas in the ori2inal scheme it was proposed to purcnase land by agreement was altoeether irregular. Ills LORDSHIP: Do you say your company have had no notice.</p>
        <p>Mr. RHODES: Abso'utely no notice at all. Dr. WlLLiebrs contended that the Cheshire Lines were not aff.med.</p>
        <p>Mr. RHODES said he hal already pointed out that they were.</p>
        <p>Dr. WILLIAMS Said there was no reason why thesecond scheme should not be gone into. Mr. CLARKS (Stockton Heath) stated that in the adver- tisement of the second scheme the public were allowed until the 17:h of Jure to send in objections, and here they were on the 14th holding an inquiry into the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. COwLEY (Northwich Urban Council) said it was quite true that objections were to be received up to the 17,h hut the Northwich Council agreed to take the ideas ot the district with regard to the scheme but leave the details to a later date. Others present cleoplained of the insufficient notice. His Lonositte said the commissioners had decided that it was not possible to enquire into the second scheme that morning. Evidence was then called in support of the scheme Na. 1 relating to the light railway from Warrington Co Northwich. THE ENGINEER GIVES EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>ROBERT HERBERT &amp;OTTER, the eneineer for the scheme, stated tha.. he had prepared the plans. War- rington and Northwich were at present connected by two lines of railway, the more important being the Cheshire Lines route and another route being theLonion and North-Western Railway. Upon the map produced he had drawn out the two routes from which the com- missioners would see that the Cheshire Lines route was through Manchester, the total dis- tance frorn Warrington being 361 miles. There were practically a dozen Verough trams each day and notwithstanding the fact that from Warrington to Man- chester there was one of the fastest short distance express services in E egland, the average time occupied in traversing the total distance was 92 minutes which included an average stop in Manchester of 22 minutes. The distance between Warrington and Northwich was ten miles seothat his lordship would see that the j urney to Manchester was a great waste of time. They did not complain of the train service as they complained of the engineering difficulties which prevented a quicker service. Tne London and North-Western route was a, shorter route but there were very few train.; during the day. In each case a stop was necessary. The object of the Light Railway was to connect Warrington and Northwich and to tiring the intervening townships and villages into touch with the two large centres of population. The direct route was about ten miles and V3Sf hoped. if thA Commissioners granted the scheme.to betable to at least halve eh time which it now tank to make the joumey via Yiancheqter. The orizinal proposal WAS to invade the territory of the Warrington Corpora- tion. As a result of certain negotiations with the Corporation the present proposal was to take the line up to the two bridges belonging to the Manchester Ship Canal Company. The Warringtoe Corporation had acquired statutory powers to constru :t tramways within the bernueti and it was Proposed utimetely to join their system with the Light Railway and have a circular route through Warrington back to their own line again. Mr. Scotter proceeded to detail how the prepasal ceul i he accomplished. The railway would ran along Knucsford- eoae.turn down Grappfinhall-road ard along London-road to Northwich. The intention was also to a.ree with the Manchester Ship Canal to pass over the bridges and connect the railway with the Warrington tramways wh.n they were constructed. The railway presented no difficulty except that it would havo to cross the Cheihire Lines. Ho was of the opinion that it would serve a real need in the district. The scheme was amply backed by a pewerful syndicate. By Mr. LLOYD: Who came first—the scheme or the Syndicate 2—The scheme came first certainly. Whera did that come from P—From me I sepnose.</p>
        <p>You iISVA been clown in the neighbourhood lecturincr to tile inhabitants about the advantage of this scheme ? —That is so.</p>
        <p>The whole thing came from Mr. &amp;letter's brain then ? —Yes, if you wish to put it that way. Are there mnre, than two local gentlemen in the syn- dicate ?—I think thde are.</p>
        <p>Farther examined witness said he never had head- goartets at Southport: Are not a groat many of these promoters Southoort eentlemen P—Sgme of them are and some are from Manchester and Liverpool.</p>
        <p>Are you resoons ble or the estimates P—Yes. In making cut your estimates did you consider the quantity of land you would hieve to buy P—l estimated routthly *hat vrould he the land required. Did vu con,id.r you would probably have to widen the road ?-10 some extent. Further examinnd, he said his amended estimate showed an increased cost of land. The witness was then crose-examined with r?fm.ence to the plans, and admit ted several inaccuracies with reference to gradients and diotane:4q.</p>
        <p>Dr. WILLIAMS asked whether the opposition desired to show that tho gradients were too steep for a light r til way.</p>
        <p>Mr. LLOYD SAM he was going to show that the scheme was ill-consid4red and insccurate. Colonel flonomor said the horizrotal dis'Ances were altoeether •ong on the plan.. Replying to further qutations by Mr. LLOYD, the witness said he was satisfied that there were wrong some fi,eurns flu the plans, but they did not materially affect the ech.-ne. It was proposed to obtain electric power from Warrington and N3rthwich. No agreement had ben signed hewreo the two bodies with reference to the supply of electriety. Mr. 'Fiume LLIYD Sail the Coeshire County Council had agreed that subject to cerf.ain clauses being inserted in the order they would not off sr any further opposition to the scheme.</p>
        <p>Dr. WILLIAMS said the promoters were WilHoz to a,!cent the clauses. He nroseeled to read them which a ere to the eff Tt that the company were not to be allowed to clnstruct the railway or any portion of it until a uniform width of not less than 27 feet of road-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WARRINGTON &amp; NORTHWICH LIGHT RAILWAY. Public Inquiry at Warrington.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON &amp; NORTH- WICH LIGHT RAILWAY. Public Inquiry at Warrington.</p>
        <p>SCHEME THROWN OUT.</p>
        <p>An important inquiry was conducted in the Town Hall. Warrington, on Thursday, into the application to the Light Railway Commissioaers for orders to !Authorise the construction of the Warrington and Northwich Light Railway and also the Western ex- lension of the same, which embraces Barnton,Little Leigh and Wilmington. The inquiry was conducted by the Earl of Jersey, G.C.B. (cnairman) and Col. Boughy,C,S.J. There was a large attendance of the general public and considerable interest was taken in the proceedings. The promoters were represented by Dr. Davies Wil- liams. In opposition to the scheme, the Hon. Horotio Lloyd (instructed by Messrs Robert Dav'es and Company , and Messrs Greenall and Stockton) appeared on bcKelf 1 of Messrs Greenall, Whitley and Company, Mr T J Down the trustees of Mr Joace. .-fair;:Rebert Davies, Mr V B Davies, Mr Henry Greenall, the Stockton Heath Parieh Council, Poole's mecutors, and Mr Fletcher. Mr Trevor Lloyd (instructed by Mr Reginald Potts) represented the Cheshire °aunty Geunool. Mr J C Phillips, Messrs Marshall n.)ttl fiehevelq -solicitors, ;Stoke-on-Trent) appeared for the:North Stafford Railway, Mr Rhodes for the Co ehire Lines Committee, Mr Trafford for the Weave rusteee, Mc Hatt Cook for the Salt Union. MrJohflWoite for Mr T H Lyon, Dr Hewitt lorVWW:nniagten Parish Council, Mr Cowley ant Mr I.kie or the Nerthwich Urban Dis- trict Council., tlr Ne win I.'l for the Mancheetir Snip Canal COMOR:I7. Mr Met( her for the Northwich Rural District Coco toil, Me F R R.o.rte, Graepenhall, for private re- teeetts it. the old Cee.t e-read, Mr Lister Kaye for Mr J C.-miter% P tor, Mr G eneee Egerton Warburton for Mr Warnurton, Mr Weeten fer himself as a property- owner, while the Rural District Council of Runcorn and Mr Harvey Talbet were represented by Mr Charles E Lioak T, of FoOdihatn. There were also present Mr Charles E Baker, Parlia- mentary agent ; the Mayor of Warrington (Alderman Roberts, Alderman He tton, Councillors Smethurst and F R Roberts), Mr Et Nield, Mr William Owen, the Town Clark of Warrington (tlr J Lyon Whittle), the assistant Town Clerk ("Mr Staneoy Wilson), the Borough Treasurer (Mr John Fairhurst), the B orough Surveyor (Mr T Lengclin), Mr James D 3,18 (water eneineer), the Rev H V Neat (yiear of St. Them te's, S-eock on Heath), Mr E A white, Mr C S Scatter (-ngine-Jr to the scheme), Mr J Arthur Co coley k to the Northwich Urban C .unc I), Masers 'Traffod awl Cooke, selicitore (N ortnwie.h), Mr J Rigby (Winsfo,d), Me H Bennett (sanitery surveyor te the Northwich Rural Council), Slr. S vintoe (highway surveyor to the N ,rth- wich Rural Council), Mr. J W Deakin (ohairmae o the Northwich Urban Council), Mr J 0 Brandrith, Mr J Weston, Mr .V Poole, Mr. Rayner, Me Hindley, Mr J Sandem; the Bsv OE Ride. Mr Themes (Winnington), Mr Newnatl (Great Balworth), Mr Cross (clerk to the Winniikt)n Parish Council), and Mr: Whitley and Mr Clark (Stocktop Heath Parish Council) TFIE PROMOTERS CASE. _</p>
        <p>Dr DAVIRs WILITIAM in stetine tntacase on behalf of the promoters -paid the tengetacif No. 1 scheme Irm War- ringttn to Northwich would be about 14 migs and scheme No. 2 which embraced the .Western extension was 9± miles. The estimate for the first scheme, exclu- sive oi rolline stock and electrical eigeipment, would he about £71,000, and for the second schema £58,000 making a tete! of £129,009. That sem was entirely ex• elusive of rolling stock and electrical eqeipment, and the widte of the gauge would be 4ft 83/4113a and it was in. teuded to run the cars by electrical power, and in all poisinility the system would be the overhead system. The gradients would be comparatively easy, as the engineer would point out when he gave the evidence. There were a few places hare and tnrre where the gradients were slightly steep. With regard to the • second scheme there would be ne difficulty in-- Mr. H. LLOYD: Are you going into the second scheme. Dr. Wieteems I understood the enquiry was to em- brace both schemes. Mr. H. LLOYD said some of them had only seen the order relating to the second scheme that morning and therefore had had no time to consider it. Hi 3 LORDSHIP said their attention would, for the pre- sent at any rate, be confined to bath schemes. Proceeding, Dr. WILLIAMS said the evidence would be practically the same in both cases, and he did not wish to take advintage of the fact that time had been too short for local companies interested to negotiate with them. Thee wished to leave the negotiations per- ctly open, and deeds could be arranged at a later date in London or semewhere else. The route of the raiterey followed the main road almost entirely with the exceo,ion of two or three places. There were Several places where diversions would be made for reasons which weld be explained in evidence. One in particular was rather a large diversion. near Comberbach HO9BO, and it sconld be made to meet the views of the o erner, who lived near it. 'f he CHAIRMAN ; I don't follow you. Dr. WILLIAX3 : It would require a light railway to follow it on the plan it is so long. (Laughter.) Con- tinuing, counsel said that the owner objected to the railway going along th-t road, but there would be an advantage in the diversion because the railway would cninet nearer to another village. On the qussti in of the advantages of the scheme, the first and obvious advantage would be that it would connect Warrington in a direct reti' e with Nerthwich and would pass through a good many villages and a tract of agricultural couetry which was one of the richest in the country. At the present time, in order to get from Warrington to Northwich one had to go by a circuitous route; it took a tong time to cover the journey, but details with regard to that miner would be given in evidence. j The agricultural interests represented by the area would be very considerable, and he might mention one fact, and that was that at Stockton Heath alone the sale of potatoes during the year amounted to ever 100,600 loads—an average of 2,000 loads a wes.k. He did not know what a load was but it meant soneething large. (Laughter.) There wan a market at Warrington ' twice a week and two markets at Northwich, and the Northwich district was the district of the Salt Union Company. At present the salt was carted fr in North- .lll to Warrington and distributed to the district be- came it was too far to take it along the railway ; ia fact, there was no railway through that-part at all. Now, with regard to the western extension. it would be shown that if it was carried out it would afford consi- derable facilities to a very large number of workmen employed in the works of Brunner, Mond and CI. who lived at B iruton, Winningten. and Northwich, and they would be able to take a penny ride to the works in the morning instead of walkine,as they had to do 110W.m4uy miles. Negotiations had ben conducted between the syn- dicate and the Cheshire Csunty Council,and the engineer would be able to give evidence on the subject of widen- ings. At first it was proposed they should come ant) Warrington, which had no tramways, and if the pro- moters had done no other good they had stimulated the Corporation of Warringten to obtain power to conetruct tramways. Toe Corporation objscted to them coming into Warringten, and it was natural they should do So, but the promoters had negotiated with the Town Conf.:ell and the position was this—that they proposed to come to the southern side of the canal, in two places. The Corporation intended to construct tramways and Rive them running powers, so that by entering at two places they won d be able to form a circular tour to Warring- ton and hick the other way. But, before that could be done, there was a little matter to be adjusted. They had here negotiating with the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany, and, with the exception of GOB trifling item, they had practically got their consent to croes their bridge and then they entered the borough of Warrington, and the tramways which would belong to Warrington would meet them at the two points which would be indicated. The Rural District Council of Run- corn was only remotely affected. That bedy gave the promoters their support, and the same remark applied to the Rural District Council of Northwich and the Urban District Council of Northwich. The Cheshire County Council, when they opened °ego, ittione, went in for a concerted action, and he believed, considering the dames they had got, they were perfectly satisfied. They had yet to determine the precise places where the widen- lags should be. Now there was a small Parish Council to consider, the Stockton Heath Parish Council, w ich came into existence in 1894. If the peomotera had been in the field before that date there would, of course, have been no oppesition from that body, but the Stoat m Heath Parish Council was a thing that existed—(laugh- ter)—tnd it was against them. Why that Council was azai net them would, perhaps, appear later on. but he hoped to be able to slew how that particular Parish Council received its inspirations. But that was for a later stage of the proceedings toe. Two Companies effected by the scheme No. 1 were the Ship Canal Corn- peep, and the Bridgewater Canal Conapany,snd an ex- planation was given as to the arrangements which had been come to with these aemponies for widening and crossing bridges. The Cheshire Lines Cemmittee raised an objection becalms the promtters passed a little mineral railway belonging to them, bu the crossing should not make the slightest difference in the world, as when they used that railway they must have some method of shutting the gates. The syndicate which was at the bark of the scheme was a powerful one, and he would put a promoter or two in the box. Tnere was no objection in handing in the list of names of the syndicate, not necessarily for publication, but as a guerancee of good faith and eacurity. Their opponents were the Steckton Heath Parith Ceuncil, and the only gentleman from whom there were written objections was Mr. T J Down, who did not believe in light railways on , roads at all, but that was purely an abstract o'jection and carete too late in the day. He had something of a personal character to the effect that his house stood at the fcot of a steep hill, and that the railway would ten east' Vie principal entrance and be a danger to the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <p>way had been provided aloe the line of route, the com- pany te acquire the necessary land and widen the road.</p>
        <p>li LORDSHIP said the real point with the clmtnis- sioners was that the promoters would have no power to compulsorily purchase land. They were simply em- powered to take land by voluntary agreement. Suppos- ing for instance one ot the land or property owners declined to sell, the protective clauses safeguarding the County Council uould fall to the ground. Mr. TREVOR LLOYD • Bifore the company could lay the lino they would have to acquire the land.</p>
        <p>COLLAPSE OF</p>
        <p>'filE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>"NOT THOROUGHLY MATURED." H's LORDSHIP : You see the sche:ne as at present drawn up has not been thoroughly matured. Dr. DAVIES WILLIAM : The only suggestion I can mike is that you should adjourn the inquiry and the scheme can come before vOu at a later stage.</p>
        <p>His LORDSHIP: The piens are not as accurate as one would wish them to be. There :are several mistakes in them, and you have not taken into consideration a good deal of the money which will have to be spent in carry- out the lines.</p>
        <p>Dr. DAVIES WILLIAMS: 'Dere is a sum of £129,000 put down,and I want to ask the engineer some questions on the estimates. His LORDsHip He has been asked them in cross- examination. It is no use our proceadin with the scheme, which requivS more consideration from the promoters. It is impossible to pass a line which depends entirely upon mutual agreements. Of course, now and again there my be a piece of land you may require,but here every piece of land which has to be acquired must hs acquired by agreement. Dr. DAVIES WILLIAMS . We shall have to start de novo unless the inqairy be adjourned, and that means a large expenditure absolutely thrown away. His LORDSHIP: Don't you think you are not prepared to go on with the case?</p>
        <p>Dr. DAVIES WILLIAMS said he had nothing further to aid. Ilia Lordship said the Cornrniiiioner could not proceed further with the application.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>RELIGIOUS LIBERTY AT BURIALS.</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS LIBERTY AT BURIALS.</p>
        <p>Peculiar (Pifficulty at Hill Cliffe.</p>
        <p>We have received the following correspondence between the Rev. J S Hughes, (pastor of Hill Cliffe Chapel). and the Rev. F E Powell, (vicar of Sc. James' Church.) 20, Cliff View, Stockton Heath, Warrington. R .v. and dear Sir, When th., other day I willingly gave my consent that you shoiVd officiate at the funeral of the late ---, in our graveyard,l was unaware that you would require to carry your ritualistic observances so far, as to bear a brazen cross aloft into our graveyard, a graveyard belonging to a people who are at the antipodes removed from all ritualistic observances, and therefore giving us needless offence, and I must enter my solemn protest against this abuse of our generosity. Although ycur fello*-clergy are willingly permitted and net rarely, Vs officiate, not one of them has offended in this mattcq before, nor have you. When on a farmer occasion you were entering at the head of a procession to conduct a servi3e in our Cnurch, and suddenly discovered it was a Baptist Church, you immediately beat a retreat and would have the people retire and hold the service in the graveyard, I swallowed the insult. but I confess that as a brother minister, I should like to have given you a good and v. holeseme talkinl to. But when in addition to that, you must go out of your way and do th it which no other clergyman has done, and invade our own private graveyard with your destructive ritualistic. prac- tices, I submit that it is neitber respectful or courteous, or in a,cordance with that christian charity which it is so desirable to cultivate, and I should like to be assured that on any future occasion on which perrn'ssion is granted, no ground will be given for such ()Ilene aa—l am, yours respectfully, J. SAMUEL HUGHES (Minister-in-charge). June 12th, 1900.</p>
        <p>St. James' Vicarage, Virarringtln.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir, I sincerely regret that anyt'-ring I did in connection wi,h the two funerals in question has given offence. But I hope you will accept my assurance that neither insult nor offence was intended either tl you or the congrega- tion at Hill Cliffe. I thought I might assume that the permission generously given to officiate in your Burial Gr4und was at least of the same kind (excepting of courss its entire voluntariness) as that provided by the Burial Laws Amendment Act 1880. This act allows " st:ch ChriNtian and oreerly religious servic-! (used by any Church, denomination or person professing to be Christian) at the crave as the person responsible for the burial shall think fit." Sines Nonconformists were mainly instrumental in obtaininz these statutbry powers in the name of religious liberty I thought they would be prepared to extend a similar toleration in the same sacred name. But your" solemn protest "con- vinces me that with some Nonconformists "religious liberty " means] not only perfect freelom for their own corsciences but also the liberty to prohibit and truculently denounce all religious beliefs and practices which do not accord with their own.</p>
        <p>At the first funeral (about a year ago) on learning that there was no "cemetery chapel" in the burial ground I preferred to take the whole service in the open air. And this was with the consent of the mourners who so far from being, in prowsaion at the door of your chapel as you imply, were still seated ia their carriageA. I did not officiate in your place of worship on precisely the same ground that Nonconformis9 when making use of our churchyards do not officiate in our pl-s of worship. I had no desire to "abuse your generosity " by taking any advantage from Nonconformity which 03 law of the Church forbids me to offer to Noecon- formity. _ _</p>
        <p>At the second - funeral (that of Monday) I used the ritual to which the deekiasedovhen alive had been accua• temed, and the "Christtancharity which"as you say, "it 13 BO desirable to cultivate," should be prepared to toler- ate practice; in others, even when these practicc.s do not commend themselves to the conscience and judg- ment of those "who are at the antipodes removed from all ritualistic observances." But are you so far removed from these as you would lead me to suppese ? I notice that therm are crosses of stone in your Burial ground,and I presume that crosses of flowers are occasionally brought by mourners. flow was Ito divine that you principles lead you to tolerate a permanent cross in stone, and a temporary cross in flowers, but do not lead you to tolerate even temporarily a cross in bras; I confess my inability to undnst snd your mental atti- tude, Two centuries of liberal thought and progress have not suffiei to rem ova the occasion of Butter's scathing satire concerning those Puritans who " Conformed for sins they are inclined to, By damning those they have na mind to Quarrel with minned pies, and 'disparage</p>
        <p>Their beet:and dearesf friend plain' porridge."</p>
        <p>. - Can you further tell me why I should assume you would tolerate the cassock, the surplice and stole (once vehemently denounced by your Puritan anc:s- tors as "Rags of Popery " and "The Livery of the Scarlet Woman"), which all clergymen now wear, and not the Cross which a growing, number of clergymen now ose P Are not all these alike " ritualistic observances !" On whet intelligible principle do you distinguish "destructive ritualistic practic,s (such as" the bearing of *brazen cross aloft into our graveyard ") from those (such as the use of stone and floral crosses) which in the opinion of the people who own the graveyard,are presumably innocent and useful,and therefore do not give "needless offoncsr Here is a suggestive idea for a thesis to submit to the young men of the Baptist College of which I twice you have the honour of being hen. secretary.—" The per- nicious destructiveness of a brazen °rose cerried aloft contrasted with the true ritualistic merits of stationary crosses in a Baptist teirial grnurei.".—lt would afford me real pleasure,—l trust, toe, real profit to be present at the public reading of these essays ! You, I suppose have a perfectly legal—perhaps a moral right to exclude me from your burial groerd And this I presume rou will now do in the name too of religious liberty ! For I cannot think of accepting directions from you as to how I shquld conduct the Church's burial service. lam too staunch a Protestant to submit to Popery even when it comes to me in the guise of a Baptist minister. The right of burial in St. James' Churchyard still exists and it is within the range of possibility that you may he called upon to officiate I there. But believe me I shall not presume-to dictate to you how you should conduct your burial service. Had I the learns authority over a burial ground 43 you have, it I would not he mire grossly intolerant for me to insist upon your carryine " a brazen cross aloft" as the con- dition of your officiating there as it is (forgive my ' direct plainness of speech) for you to insist upon my not carrying the cross as the con- dition of my officiating in your ground. To Me religious liberty so inestimably precious and cred I—implies the right of each person to have such relieious rites and ceremonies a! he thinks helpful and edifying. ; And I could not have the heart to write to some poor heathen concerning the ritual which he honestly in the sight of his Gsd thought was of help and comfort to him in the great battle of life, as you and so many of those who think with you, write of the ritual of your brother Christians, whose fault if it be a fault—was only in the hour of sorrow and death and at be foot of the grave, where the voice of controversy is usually hushed, to hold up the grand symbol of our coalmen humanity's only hope and consolation—the Cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, to some alas still as in St. Pull'a days, either foolishness or a block of stumhling.—Yours faithfully, Jute 13th, 1900. FRANCIS E. POWELL.</p>
        <p>P.S.—As the question—less that of carrying a cross than of our respective interpretation of religious liberty—between us is one of principle and of public interest I am sending the letters to the public press.</p>
        <p>When asking for Cocoa, insist on haying OLDBURY'S sold only in Packets and Tins as of-her Cocoas are often suhiti- toted for the sake of extra profit.—Advt (1)</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES</p>
        <p>At the Newton Police Court on Friday morning before Mr Collingwoon, John Higgins and Richard Burge were charged with being drunk in Earlestown and in default of paying a fine of 2s 6i and costs were earh sent to gaol for seven days. At the same court on ;:zaturday morning before Mr. Gillespie, J. Carroll was charged with begging in Newton, and in default of paying a fine of 5s and costs was sent to gaol for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>INDIAN FAMINE FUNE.</head>
        <p>THE INDIAN FAMINE FUNE.</p>
        <p>At St. John's Church, Earlestown, en Sunday afternoon, the senior scholars of the Barlestown District School gave a sacred cantata entitled "The Good Shepherd" (T. M Mattison), in aid of the local fund for the Indian famine. Toe scholars were assisted by Miss A Molyneux, and Mr Percy Nash, of St. Helens both teachers at the school. Miss Molyneux sang with excellent taste" 0 rest in the Lord." Mr A Brown conducted the choir, and Mr Harrison presided at the organ. A few remarks were made by the Vicar in support of the object, appealing strongly for help. The collection which amounted to .£7 4450 included a portion of the day's church collection, specially set aside for the purpose by the wardens —A collection was also made at Wargrave Church in aid of the same object.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>STORM AT HAYDOCK</head>
        <p>THE STORM AT HAYDOCK</p>
        <p>During the sqvere thunderstorm which broke over Haydock on Tuesday a barn at Callon's farm was struck by lightning and set on fire. The, building together with a large quantity of hay straw, and farm implements was totally destroyed. The St. Helens fire brigade,which was wired for,came with the steamer. it was immediately perceived that the effarts to save the building would be un- availing, and attention was given to prevent the fire spreading to the homestead which was close by. In this the brigade was successful,and the fire ultimately burnt itself out without doing any further damage. About the same time the lightn- ing was seen to striks a stack of hay belonging to Mr, G. Platts of the Wagon and Horses Hotel, Hay— dock. The hay to3k fire but it was promptly ex- ing uish ed by the police and some neighbours before toy serious damage was done. EARLESTOWN CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annusl general meeting of the members of this club was held in the billiard room on Tuesday evening. Mr. T J Gillespie presided, and he was supported by Mr C B F Barron, J Simpson, T Hasel- den, W Mawdslev, J Cotterall &amp;c. The minutes of the last general meeting were read and confirmed, and the financial statement showed the club to be in a satisfactory condition, with an increase of membership. The Chairman said he had no appre- hension of an election this year at all, as owing to the war, politics were rally in the back ground. There were substantial reasons for the Government not appealing to the country at the present time. The first was that they would not like to appeal to the country until the war was over, the second was that the Radicals would not like to take up the res- pensibility of concluding Map present war, and thirdly, the two recent elections showed that the policy of the Government had been satisfactory to the majority of the people. He regretted the l-orrible war which was now taking place, but it was unavoidable and was not of this country's seek- ing. Every cloud had its silver lining and the pre- sent war had exemplified the courage and endur- ance of our soldiera in soon a way that our neigh- bours across the channel had even given courteous though penitent praise to the way in which our soldiers fought. How many other nations had Colonies like the British; and did they ever hear of other Colonies helping the Colonisers in the same way that the British Colonies did. The Colonies had shown themselves a thorough part of the Mother Country, and with such colonies there was not likely to be any difficulty between the Mother Country and they. There was no doubt that the war would lead to a revision of the military system for he thought there was no question they were getting a little deficient in military knowledge and tactics. In the elect Eon of °fillers, Mr. Gillespie was elected president for the 20th time. Mr. J. W. Thorpe was appointed troasurer,and Mr.T. Tlazelden seeretory,with a strong working committee. A vote of thanks to the chairman wabeartily accorded and the meeting was brought to a close by the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPL /MY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Tilarlestown lyidge meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Warerave-read,on Monday evening, Bro. Cutler C.T. presiding. After the orysn- ing services Bro. Newman gave the report of the No. 1 Convention held at Tyldesley. also the greet- ings of the Cencilio-et-Labore Ledv, Manchester. On the motion of Bro Clarke E S. it was resolved to support the Bill for the prohibition of the Sale of Drink to Children &amp;se the Sunday Closing (Mon- mouthshire) Bill copies of the resolution to be sent to Lord Salisbury, Mr. Balfour, the leaders of the opposition and the local M.P's. Resolved to hold an open air meeting on July 28th, also to help Bro oring in his open air work. Bro Sharpe was in- stalled as marshal!, Bro Clarke being the installing Aber end Br e Newman acting as installing marshal The programme being." C.T. in a fix" be was asked various question, Bro Clarke asked "what is the essential difference betwesn the Good Templar Order and the open societies ?" • The C.T. replied that the first difference lay in the pledge, which is for life and which binds to neither make, buy, nor ' sell, to furnish or cause to be furnished to others as a beverage any intoxicants, secondly in the method of conducting their business which in the Order is done by the members themselves and in other societies by Committees Bro Plant asleed how did public-house signs originate? He said they began at n time when the majority of people could neither read nor write Po that it was no use putting out their name or their calling, so tradesmen hung out various implements of their trade or a facsimile of the finished article that they made and the publican could not. hang out a sample of the finished article or there was not variety enough of their tools to eißtinZniSh, so many began to adopt other devices which he classed under three heads : the practical, hnmorens, and suggestive, several examples of which he quoted, such as the" Labour in vain," which suggests that it is labour in vain for a man to toil all week, and then spend all his money in the public-house, and gets nothing but a burning thirst and a heavy head ; the " Church tavern," which ha more attraction than the Chorch of God ; the " Owl " which typifies the drink, in that it delights. in the dark end its fight is so silent and stealthy that its victims do not know their danzer till they cannot escape its clutches and are destroyed. Other questions were asked, and a pleasant evening was spent. in spite of the storm outside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE ECCENTRIC ST. HELENS WHEELWRIGHT AGVIN</p>
        <p>Dvid Rimmer, wheelwright, again appeared before the St. Helens maglstrates on Mlndav on a sumtnons for arrears of wife's maintenance Rimmer sa'd her huoband had been to prison several tirn -s on the order, and had poirl talthing sines he vrts last in gaol. M. Riaimfq• sAid his wit, (alight to draw the club money as he had arranged. Mrs. Rirnmer said ..it was an gone. Rimmer who gestic:l:Wed and arl-lre,sed the c-urt in a very lond voice, said tie was not gin g to pay any more His ciet,v said thee ought to co off ;rack at sixty years of azo, and he, was going off at sev-nsty, ten years later. If a letter was written ta the general secretary of his society the money would be paid —The Chairman • If the money is in ti a club, you had better eet it or; and pay, or you will go to gaol for 28 days —Rimmer : All right I'm ready; she eeiPeed me ten ye trs ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-15_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SACRILIME AT A ST. BLENS CHURCH,</head>
        <p>SACRILIME AT A ST. BLENS CHURCH,</p>
        <p>S. Helens Polie. C mrr. on Tuoaday, before Mesere. W. I. ThomFon and Colonel "11"1'-n.r, Mary 0' rola, of 4. Fleet-lane, wee charged with stealing a brass vase, valued at 7s 63. the property of the Rev J M Ceurtenay. —Chief Clnstab7e Wood eaid that about nine o'clock on Monday night a young. woman named Jane Friar saw prisoner coming nut of the nhur ch. Prisoler afterwards tried to se,' the Inge to 111!s Friar for 4 1 and than offered it to d uohtor for 3i, 0 Tolle was later on arre;ted by Constable Whalley. hehind a bacrhouse in Church- street, PArr.—Prisonor pleaded guilty, and said she was drunk—The Clerk (Mr. R. Brevris) : Do you know that you an. liahl t) ty, sent to penal servitude for life if you are sent to the AS'iVI P—PriValer: No Mr.—There were a riumbor of previous convictions against her for larceny and the bench sent her to geol for three months.</p>
