        <p>wasa first case, a fine of £2 Ile and £1 5s 6d costs would be ' Imposed in the first case ; and £1 5s fine, and £1 5$ 6d cost for neglecting to notify. s A BLACK SPoT IN SUNC3RK. —Joseph Venables, a boatman, pleaded guilty to unlawfully keeping a brothel, at Runcorn' on the 7th June, 1930, and divers other dates.—Rebacca Mather and Bile,. Murphy, two women, were charged with aiding and abetlng.—Aurphy did not appear.—The cases were proved by Sergeants letewster and Gregory, and Con- stables Bowyer and Nicholls.—Venables was sent to prison for 2 months with hard labour ; Mather for 6 weeks with hard labour ; and a warrant was issued for the apprehension of Murphy. Alison OFFENCF.S.—Thomas Norman. Cambridge-street, Runcorn, was fined 5s for being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street, on the 15th of June,—The case was proved by Constable It Gilpin. —James Hessond, 40, Bentick-street, was fined 53 for allowing his dog to be at large without " name and address" upon it on the let of June, 1930.—P.C.Gregory proved the case S IBEET PROFiNERS.—For using obscene language within the hearing of the public, Bmily Green, Runcorn,was fined 2s 6d and 83 6d co its ; Ralph Done, of Weston, was for a similar offence fined 53 and 8s 6d costs ; Andrew Connolly, and Jane Connolly. man and wife, also indicted on a similar charge, bath pie ided guilty and were fined 53 and 83 6d costs, or seven days each in default. --40--. STRANGE FATALITY AT LATCH- FORD. --- A Gentleman Fatally Burned. Inquest. Mr. Charles Blunt, deputy coroner, conducted an in- quiry on Tuesday afternoon relative to the death of Julia Smith (76), a retired gentleman, of Willow Bank, Acker's-lane, Latchford Without, who died on June 10th, from burns received on May 14th. Mr. T. Smith was foreman of the jury. Alfred Smith, son of the deceased, said deceased re- sided with his wife. He saw him on May 15th and ' he said that he had caught fire during the previous night. He said that his (witness's) mother went to bed which was on the ground fl or, and he sat on the side of the bed in front of a lighted fire and read the war news. He must have fallen asleep, and when he awoke he found his clothes were burning. He hurriedly pulled them off, and he said at first he was not burnt. Wit- ness's mother had told him that she woke up during the night and found that the room was full of smoke. De- ceased had broken ona of his fingers and was burnt about the body. A doctor was called in who set his finger and attended to his burns. At first the deceased took it as a joke say- ing that his injuries were nothing, and refused to comply with the doctor's orders to go to bed- Deceased slid he did not feel any pain until the Friday, when he took to his bed. Thera was than a change for the worse and he died on Sunday June 107. h. The doctor did not think he would live so long, because he thought it was a serious case. The Foreman : I suppose when he fel his newspap,r caught fire ? Witness : No. The newspaper was intact. The Coroner : Was he subject to fainting fits ? Witness : No, net at all. In aeswer to a juror witness said deceased did not smoke. The Coroner : You are of opinion that the death was accidental ? Witness : Yes, most decidedly. Eliza Androws, nurse, said she had attnided the de- ceased up to the time of his death. He had never said anything about tha cause of the accident. The Coroner said it was quite clear that deceased died from the effects of burns, but how ha met with the accident they did not quite know. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death." —.... ,Te , it is Not I ~ Difficult' .* , ~. if you go about it in the right way, to not only get prottpt F I relief, but to Positively Cure I that distressing ailment ~, a . • 1 • ineivatesifiOiv,o ---.., The only way to cure indiges- 1 tion or dyspepsia is to give the 't stomach a rest. You do not need to stop eating in order to do this, 1 you may eat any food you desire if you will only take one or two t g 1:3 od.